[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ab9250-28af-5908-8d6f-09833d521f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445643} {:answer/id #uuid "67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445655} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8e760c-2dd0-5dcf-8836-3c5e2deced71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445664} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6cc197-cc16-54f4-b4ca-9fa9bd9e20c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445666} {:answer/id #uuid "de9daefe-3ccb-54d5-9516-f29765d78d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445668}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445655} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5c7c2a-000b-5483-8ada-2cf63e301ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "021b7b63-3816-5a7a-9f77-baf36bf193bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-65864" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that $$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c059460-c4c2-55a2-8585-7e51d8edae78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445644} {:answer/id #uuid "47522fd9-62bd-5474-804d-67ac7a770b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\sin }^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445650} {:answer/id #uuid "4b40ea9e-cf34-5238-9134-0581b982e962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445651} {:answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} {:answer/id #uuid "e85749fe-f788-582a-a8ba-6a6fb947faa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\cos }^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} :answer-field/id #uuid "47babdd0-5f4c-5c9a-9ce1-4d934697545f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95051" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\ln }^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445652} {:answer/id #uuid "76ff76d1-a44d-5785-aeb6-f49cd08b8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445657} {:answer/id #uuid "869d2a2c-eca1-5a81-9c8c-bd38ea652d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445658} {:answer/id #uuid "9980bb90-a6ef-51f4-88b3-4812a2e11e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\ln }^{2}⁡(2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445662} {:answer/id #uuid "e898d08a-c5cb-5c48-be98-c6dd2a755c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\ln }^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445652} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ea0794-709e-5625-aee2-f32cf13014aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-36234" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,2t⟩$ at the point $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042a3167-88c1-58d7-9cb9-db2e59913bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445782} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcb67a6-a89e-5cb5-bf50-d01668c64856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445783} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fc5106-7c16-50e1-afac-b55e9efdb392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445804} {:answer/id #uuid "c56a9809-f9cb-5e51-a1c8-7aa478b44cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445805} {:answer/id #uuid "f647841e-742e-5179-a5c3-3b9852200fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445814} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7552f1e-9180-5b58-ac47-de556c1b13e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7dc3d2ae-4ebe-55e5-a13e-304e391443f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81347" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡y}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f860dfb5-9cc2-564b-a719-e277d51c0fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446068} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0bc8af-0212-5f26-b710-65114290ccbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "264068ae-fe2b-5f78-b146-741b451e1198" :question/math-academy-id "q-344474" :question/problem "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1 \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+cos\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\sqrt{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n![](images/e4/e42ca640ae08d60ea81bdcb7c3c3753f5dc74fb6eea49b5b0f999e3463b5f8f0.png)"} {:answer/id #uuid "001907a0-d758-52f3-b5d8-ac5e8cf1f77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446311} {:answer/id #uuid "75e28f10-a377-5525-ba66-0cdc4c8e054e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446331} {:answer/id #uuid "df9cb88c-fbc1-50f3-a483-943aeb28c2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det({A}^{-1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bc837a-bbcd-5027-bfb5-1c8984cd1027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446416} {:answer/id #uuid "601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446423} {:answer/id #uuid "9f15347b-2581-5183-b776-03898fbfc72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446442} {:answer/id #uuid "a6701104-c6a3-5c18-9c13-31528c875706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446446} {:answer/id #uuid "cda725f3-ec0d-574a-ba5c-8fd6946ee6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446423} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7409f8-33ba-5d76-9294-d18b4e765a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "004a7cc4-06c2-5abf-9f5d-0dfb86998289" :question/math-academy-id "q-83434" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+2$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(1)+2 \\\\  & =2+0-4+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =2(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0+8+2 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0-8+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $18$ at the point $(-2,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4873054d-389b-5657-b900-41aaf6b8506c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and  ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446418} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee71f7a-8717-5e5a-914c-2dd0b5abd661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=9$ and  ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446437} {:answer/id #uuid "90e36d8d-b6c9-504e-9e35-d0e7572f1045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446438} {:answer/id #uuid "d431810c-ed86-5648-8955-de7e2061891b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446456} {:answer/id #uuid "daf4365c-6130-56d7-9fa9-feb169811efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446418} :answer-field/id #uuid "e141f0c3-e7b5-5bab-8504-cfef40829a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49397200-f106-5784-934d-a685f0e1e2bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83436" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4xy+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 8\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y,\\,2y-4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y &=0 \\\\ 2y-4x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+1 \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t+8{sin}^{2}⁡t-32\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -32\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{8},0),\\,(2,2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(-2,-2),\\,(2,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{8},0)$ | $(2,2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(-2,-2)$ | $(2,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ | $-7$ | $25$ | $-7$ | $25$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001a4990-bc6c-5661-a5d2-d58cf6282115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446402} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6f3732-88ff-52f3-b96e-e2ff73cb9542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446429} {:answer/id #uuid "833fa5da-99e1-5b84-94da-cea7de15a066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446431} {:answer/id #uuid "836e3ef0-8c12-55a7-9852-b91d4df5f503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=3$ and  ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446432} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c3cddb-38d0-5a99-aa10-c92fe4168383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446429} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c12a56a-e777-5adf-89ed-09fc3bb0be46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8fb41ea9-db84-5bfb-a8a6-5486c5782ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86720" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x,\\,-2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=0 \\\\ -2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+3 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(2,0)$ and $(-2,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446425} {:answer/id #uuid "a52483f7-a6b6-5cc1-abb3-7db0b579ef56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446444} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ee3bb-67c1-5270-8d84-3e8e37bf67a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446447} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a76143-6f93-57f5-9713-8433062ded47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446448} {:answer/id #uuid "efcb8428-944f-5ad5-960c-f08b95cb6ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446425} :answer-field/id #uuid "81180c93-e1c2-5e9f-adf8-f0c58db4c467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" :question/math-academy-id "q-83437" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has no critical points." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0213af8b-e153-5a9f-bcae-d28d03ed5a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446634} {:answer/id #uuid "09d9b377-d154-58c3-9816-cc93e5ae714f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446635} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1e288e-9f0e-5aa1-a99f-65dcd2f40da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -35=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446677} {:answer/id #uuid "c55c3bab-e95d-5b9c-87f7-f189a3394e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446693} {:answer/id #uuid "eb843c49-7c9d-5188-a745-423ff5070742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+15\\lambda +5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446705}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446693} :answer-field/id #uuid "7076766c-bcdf-54a7-b381-7806ea7001fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b0fd647-11bd-5cf6-9bab-c8f19524ee42" :question/math-academy-id "q-107986" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 5 & -10-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 5 & -10-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-10-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-7) & =0 \\\\ -20-2\\lambda +10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+35 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ee468a0-d352-5289-a776-61c9098a0f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446663} {:answer/id #uuid "956a468e-b06e-5bee-8c3c-ec586866b569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446681} {:answer/id #uuid "a173a3e7-290e-5032-aef3-774d0ec21fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5$ (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446685} {:answer/id #uuid "d243faea-e0d3-5179-9ed1-a7f88786cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446700} {:answer/id #uuid "fa490009-2047-5aaf-930f-ab551852d4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446700} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b447c11-2343-5e3b-9317-039ab756a40a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0d05d968-12a6-5808-b925-0cd007f4210b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107982" :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(P-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 3-\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-5)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 6}{2} \\\\  & =5,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311e8f46-648a-5c98-ae9c-c09e69f012d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\lambda =\\sqrt{17}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446648} {:answer/id #uuid "6efd6b7d-bdc0-5be0-b513-c19d0b047800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446664} {:answer/id #uuid "84f65cb8-d626-588c-b078-563da250f9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446673} {:answer/id #uuid "9954f005-4623-5ab9-8eee-41291e4fa96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446682} {:answer/id #uuid "c13f05a3-3e57-51ad-abad-91529834d620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =\\sqrt{17}$ (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446673} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dae1e56-95f5-5008-8851-38bbfc769453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "463f0d10-4286-59ff-a21d-e7909700880b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76363" :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{13} & \\sqrt{17} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{17} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{17} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda )(\\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda )-0\\cdot \\sqrt{17} & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\sqrt{13})(\\lambda -\\frac{1}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a3c3fb8-6cb0-50c4-9ef3-126838f4ce82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446646} {:answer/id #uuid "57e4e92d-8154-5972-94f3-6a2be941a297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446656} {:answer/id #uuid "8688b5da-0cea-5d1e-9ba0-2678fa02cf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =7$ (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446674} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4c8e25-fb81-5e27-b673-2b99b02a167e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446686} {:answer/id #uuid "ed046ba9-c6f5-5ba6-8016-8beac476dce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446706}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446656} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc20a709-7b8c-57c3-b33e-d46ed2185a69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f368600-216e-5acd-8869-df093ce09e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106996" :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(C-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =-2.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d692e8-2c9b-54e7-a366-01c7bd296385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-\\sqrt{7}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446661} {:answer/id #uuid "70faf1f1-23c6-58e4-bf59-0e879757a31f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}+\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446665} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ec6264-3ee5-5365-b892-5188665949f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}+\\lambda )(1+\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446696} {:answer/id #uuid "f29c22a6-dc15-5878-997c-e48683c9fce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}+\\lambda )(1+\\lambda )-\\sqrt{7}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446710} {:answer/id #uuid "f8320f69-edc7-50c1-b592-2506954adc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446713} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe5a947-f078-594e-9bf7-dde3c2b3ec2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "548202ea-0779-523d-b823-a5921b15b56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76342" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix $M=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-0\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de09d46-850c-56fb-987f-3452a117032d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446642} {:answer/id #uuid "74c14f3c-9cab-5b7a-ac87-feb91e66599f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446667} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb7b9b2-8faa-570c-b54b-291054c89688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446679} {:answer/id #uuid "c805c3e2-c747-567c-9254-772bf262c4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-7$ (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446695} {:answer/id #uuid "f57eb3c3-c429-58ea-8bc7-183c23c639f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446642} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dde9c69-e4af-5813-b923-f6b3bb4c7cb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "649da0b4-9af9-52b6-817f-cca5e0eb449f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12298" :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $U-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nU-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(U-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 6+2\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 14}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 9}{2} \\\\  & =-7,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1143f7a7-3c20-50bb-b995-564624c93a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =9$ and $\\lambda =-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446639} {:answer/id #uuid "17a34526-21cf-53cf-aa39-d12402af539d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446640} {:answer/id #uuid "1fdece1b-ac27-5958-a1d9-138dda9c3a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =7$  (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446643} {:answer/id #uuid "33cfa4f0-b28e-5bca-a516-62e8607d6074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\lambda =5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446649} {:answer/id #uuid "43843bb9-eeeb-51b0-be25-7866a7b2aeba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446640} :answer-field/id #uuid "3850d5ad-9421-5f33-bc57-b428d8dacd98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "688b15c3-44c2-5c91-89ca-dd25252cd049" :question/math-academy-id "q-36772" :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $L-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nL-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -9 & -11-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(L-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(L-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -9 & -11-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-11-\\lambda )-6\\cdot (-9) & =0 \\\\ -33-3\\lambda +11\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +21 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =64-84 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $L$ does not have real eigenvalues."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ccfd10-2e0b-576f-8601-ee2b077cab77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446644} {:answer/id #uuid "29073a5f-e09c-5fc6-ac4e-971be56c9568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499}