wer/id #uuid "f4265219-f53f-5148-b2f2-d73b22c820e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{z}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f1b62c-f9ad-5062-b0e1-04d51f193f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{3}{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478564} {:answer/id #uuid "1506c3d3-af67-5f60-9fbf-f85df4c42324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +3{r}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478586} {:answer/id #uuid "61401e41-69fe-5937-b2e0-2a0aea6f6035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478699} {:answer/id #uuid "b9805fe1-fc17-516a-93d3-42522d950906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478860} {:answer/id #uuid "d46accdd-6789-5230-a789-5a2c14364ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478586} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfbb460-7f02-5f92-88f0-57dd4c8eadf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43a13109-9c88-5e83-9206-805f97530d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-95045" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{3}+{z}^{4},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +3{r}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a74912-9939-5160-b2f2-fc9f75995b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478590} {:answer/id #uuid "489fb4e2-401a-51bd-8367-54c21281a25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478661} {:answer/id #uuid "af934f8e-6b47-50f3-976d-130962164ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478844} {:answer/id #uuid "d704c90e-1c68-5f35-9050-f1ea9764f3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478900} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d81125-ee3b-5e23-ba6b-3f5a3fc654e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478844} :answer-field/id #uuid "77f83ac4-379e-5a13-8c4c-6ea313e7d773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4437c604-b7f0-5633-b7e5-9971f03bd2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-165283" :question/problem "Given that $v(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=\\frac{∂v}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂r}=2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =(2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bf02a3a-6507-5e24-9dfe-fdc65427917b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478718} {:answer/id #uuid "cb480f73-b28d-5d1d-bf28-114396c6f5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478886} {:answer/id #uuid "d40c479d-3512-5ad6-901c-ae8cc9ca656d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478896} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9ed06d-7edb-5dc7-a0b5-315d2f4f833d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478932} {:answer/id #uuid "f4006751-8828-5338-b289-001c5c562f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478896} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c55d75d-2389-5139-9713-9b0001a3628b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "824e717c-810d-5647-9a31-c9225776fabe" :question/math-academy-id "q-165280" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=2y-xy,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d25be9-c593-5605-a401-5d2bd749db79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478585} {:answer/id #uuid "7368bb2b-738b-5553-9b24-d72d10f86673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478728} {:answer/id #uuid "84dcc6f3-d690-5f72-8730-48a6a222d69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478763} {:answer/id #uuid "952ab814-3b74-5511-bb1b-17a92c942267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478803} {:answer/id #uuid "e73b53e8-31b8-587a-95b0-e9d5813a7d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478728} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bff08b3-d2ad-542d-9f8a-a1ea880775f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88d1b83b-2766-5063-936f-a018502be867" :question/math-academy-id "q-95137" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6d8b25-1be0-507f-9e45-600b11128f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478574} {:answer/id #uuid "194c2f16-5d03-56da-8f8a-2451146b01a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478595} {:answer/id #uuid "27127698-eb1f-5556-ba20-69f2d7bce69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478617} {:answer/id #uuid "80a52cdb-4432-54c2-aba3-ba66914bcbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478752} {:answer/id #uuid "91e129af-e354-55a9-b266-adc2bb6d4a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478595} :answer-field/id #uuid "74be8c02-d536-5d47-b824-c1806a52210c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5298f54-bfaf-5a4f-ac68-b78b607bf15c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95115" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "893956da-d416-51f2-95f7-dc12fb1c1725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478773} {:answer/id #uuid "9713ade3-5604-530d-b802-1fdfc640a4e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478807} {:answer/id #uuid "b62c08b7-43fb-5995-8666-a7c19ff8a830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478854} {:answer/id #uuid "daa43c07-cab4-59d9-96d9-b09db9596eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478903} {:answer/id #uuid "f69418f1-6362-5b4e-8489-03eacbf8b9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478807} :answer-field/id #uuid "45082c16-ebec-5599-aae3-e180ec356a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e54cb552-8c05-5b9c-84f1-978bbe7cbf63" :question/math-academy-id "q-95168" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}={x}^{2}+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188706c6-3a9a-59e2-a321-b5f7df7e0d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478593} {:answer/id #uuid "48daba33-40ff-5944-964d-6c1da24ee2b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478662} {:answer/id #uuid "b483533f-bf6d-5af3-85eb-651c6a343298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "rz{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478851} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd84997-1bc6-5e77-af1c-2104ef3b0bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478869} {:answer/id #uuid "cb23270c-5341-5bc3-a497-83e69396e2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478593} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce5f4636-c6b9-5bae-8b55-2a495ce5b39c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb779ef9-c9f2-5338-9ff6-677718e082ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-95169" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂h}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂h}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=yz,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta } & =-(rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "051a22a3-65f3-5f49-a3e6-9cda4896dcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8cos⁡x+8tan⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479160} {:answer/id #uuid "065571da-f7da-50c2-aa5c-4c6080ff286d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3cos⁡x+3tan⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35826e01-f398-5718-9a3f-7a016441228f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9csc⁡ycot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479170} {:answer/id #uuid "7067a561-7da2-51bd-9646-a858acd80007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9cot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479199} {:answer/id #uuid "87a0a1e8-418c-5f72-a834-2ea3096e694e" :answer/type {:db/i