, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡x(4sec⁡x+7tan⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{sec}^{2}⁡x+7sec⁡xtan⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 4{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 7sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4tan⁡x+7sec⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f1409e-faf6-5671-a59c-873872ecf37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4cot⁡x-7sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1965499e-81be-5d3a-9f8c-15a148e70db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3tan⁡x+8sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479283} {:answer/id #uuid "47942bed-9fad-5ddd-88e3-1393ad47f6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3cot⁡x+8sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479290} {:answer/id #uuid "5bacfae1-9c83-5550-86e1-cd9f512de486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3tan⁡x+8csc⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479292} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b71b49-3ed2-51f3-9780-b2c69fe7f1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3tan⁡x-8csc⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479306} {:answer/id #uuid "f601d223-6b63-50ef-ab15-51578b3de84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3cot⁡x-8sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479283} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7341a7-fa0a-54fe-b478-20dd9864726e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e51262c4-111b-5af6-9c4b-384b623d1a51" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int sec⁡x(3sec⁡x+8tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sec⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =tan⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡x(3sec⁡x+8tan⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{sec}^{2}⁡x+8sec⁡xtan⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 8sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3tan⁡x+8sec⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "e6461d80-2bce-59e7-80b3-6fa7a49e95aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6sec⁡z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479311} {:answer/id #uuid "ebfec536-e400-5f90-aed0-e83c1e748f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6cot⁡z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479312} {:answer/id #uuid "efb19c95-2013-57ff-8764-cba8d0c38900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4tan⁡x+7sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479313} {:answer/id #uuid "f601d223-6b63-50ef-ab15-51578b3de84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3cot⁡x-8sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c81a280-e468-5bb1-ae24-06ecd33d692c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480007} :answer-field/id #uuid "369e2fe5-a4b2-5cf1-a353-9ab634eb9000" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24285fdc-6262-5d37-b204-181d4efdc6b1" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡3} & ={(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548f8af3-6988-5dcf-b7db-7042ec33f9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 2e^{x} + \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480018} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5c6f98-32c7-5e01-876f-dd32bf0f5f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 2e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480029} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfe2a01-f83d-5791-a0bb-9056c4f88b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480031} {:answer/id #uuid "b992cfc5-1e95-5320-aa79-472613633d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 2 \\cdot e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480036} {:answer/id #uuid "dff80c7e-a9c2-5066-8ca4-139ee627d565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480029} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1376578-3b74-543a-904f-628166133f63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27963039-7ab9-50a1-a524-335dda2120d3" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\ln 2 + 2e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf23329-5460-51e6-917d-f464bb0d4626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480022} :answer-field/id #uuid "d50e007d-5e38-5d66-ac2c-89d4723aae0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3368f5c9-3775-5d06-9195-e0e05c5824a2" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31e88a41-78ca-580b-a312-79116f88663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480010} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcde689-7645-5ddd-989e-d3c8f722028e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480013} {:answer/id #uuid "50eade78-4e28-509e-9c5d-091c94140008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480015} {:answer/id #uuid "84a258fd-c8f6-529f-a8a2-a5a143aa098e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\ln{\\left(9 \\right)} + 7 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480028} {:answer/id #uuid "bc11084f-7105-5e40-8cf2-b58e3962a347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480038} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0428780-07b4-5eb1-a923-27ac73163009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "40eee14f-7f06-5ca0-8856-ee0dc15c1493" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-ln⁡9+7{e}^{x}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡9$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡9+7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡9)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+7\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(7{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =7{e}^{1} \\\\  & =7 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78806f5e-a59a-5036-8a8a-e637dbb300bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480026} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a8a426-86a3-500f-b11d-f1d5bdfd0d6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5104b80b-063d-53dc-91b3-3f3e99aa0687" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡9$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡9} & ={(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-ln⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69473c5c-ae42-5677-bf46-38ec4c571473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480024} :answer-field/id #uuid "59172bf7-c76f-53d2-a87d-8c7ee520c969" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5184f338-d6d0-533d-88bb-8b37da109e6c" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡2} & ={(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c9cce5-6b1c-514b-83d7-a145c5acd6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480045} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6843cd-87ec-513b-bc48-aa3b5a960609" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5f698a42-ae62-5802-8f77-88360f877560" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} & ={(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf34f77f-125e-5a29-9908-3c47b6785d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480040} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05ed301-7707-5213-90a6-1584eb6e88ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "78a2b5f7-5cef-5012-a53c-264a4e19606b" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5726971d-109b-59ca-a9ae-86038320a437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdae0fc4-9547-5bff-bd8c-baecad3058a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b423e41e-b9ca-54b7-896b-f125c7eb77b4" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}+2$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}} & ={(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc1213d-28ba-50b4-a7d5-d912d5920958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{16}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480025} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd6ad36-c3a9-5988-b038-55ae25095101" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cf08154e-5ad6-5738-b7be-7d9a74b0856c" :question/problem "G