a476edb15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482267} {:answer/id #uuid "d29ad024-3d69-560b-a1b9-aff53f30896a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482279} {:answer/id #uuid "dcecb712-fe3e-501f-918d-8a68c107c94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482298} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d548e7-7e33-5671-81f5-4f4350c23585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482323} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ebb7ed-a4dc-5ae7-a75b-d082ec3f13f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482336} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0c92ac-4a7e-5612-8b1a-cdab04a75fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482338} {:answer/id #uuid "f40a7aeb-b349-5ecd-b0c6-8c4dfad465cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482354} {:answer/id #uuid "f53097bb-f59d-547e-b750-92460571ca57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482355} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d5ffdc-8a6e-57db-9529-bc88c792c05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109a83cd-781d-5694-9781-188bd9aaeeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481816} {:answer/id #uuid "127f15fb-77a4-5f53-a36b-526f40d40b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481824} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6483cc-ffc1-5e9c-84c3-e71f0a0fde29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481952} {:answer/id #uuid "50912d13-7637-55d7-a096-19d196f870ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481969} {:answer/id #uuid "89405e80-2f4a-52b4-8de1-3252e9336dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482092}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481816} :answer-field/id #uuid "453a80d3-4572-568f-a182-685985b15243" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0136e908-03ab-5353-9218-7031e36c58ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-161787" :question/problem "![](images/61/61bd212879bc2f37dba7637bb3b126e33c5f7b0fbebd5a676874d9b3f6bc38da.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6y$ for $x\\ge 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(0,3,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/f5/f52fe1c2e73dcbe972d72d263a5a43f2c99dbd573395fbf6e3a3b78b46e1f461.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}[\\rho ]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-0)\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =6[ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =6(\\pi -0)\\cdot (-0+1) \\\\  & =6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15050ad1-bbf7-5ae7-b969-2f07d4306ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481832} {:answer/id #uuid "6488d04a-03fe-5654-a722-67d715654669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482007} {:answer/id #uuid "7127edd8-9885-5717-9356-bf13d8c61bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482036} {:answer/id #uuid "801036a5-3ed7-528a-92f4-c8ef0bebef28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482070} {:answer/id #uuid "92f38256-28ff-58e2-87f8-76d91c5a2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481832} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c56d916-834b-5bb9-948e-1a1744f8d9b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1521c62d-edb7-5cf4-9412-1fda40e8b968" :question/math-academy-id "q-161758" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d4efdf4-7132-5a7c-902a-3084f4ac7da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482028} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc3a237-3a9b-5a3e-86b6-46c598ab506a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482150} {:answer/id #uuid "dcecb712-fe3e-501f-918d-8a68c107c94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482298} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0c92ac-4a7e-5612-8b1a-cdab04a75fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482338} {:answer/id #uuid "f40a7aeb-b349-5ecd-b0c6-8c4dfad465cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482354} :answer-field/id #uuid "34e8d1a7-8446-506e-a289-5e07cc9420e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31ed4558-6b89-5259-81f7-c1113b514f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161755" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71daa61c-f673-5487-82fd-dfae267f950a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482039} {:answer/id #uuid "84aa3c04-cf41-557b-8aea-45aea8b80596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482082} {:answer/id #uuid "af8ed3a1-6f17-5628-a41f-6b72eaa00e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482188} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f7ffd3-d593-5a78-9afb-ab0de4615675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482312} {:answer/id #uuid "e76d4b36-6b12-5f00-85a3-5cc9eafa0940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482312} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ef04a2-40cf-5a45-9600-60f9cd13b11c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4913e4d1-197e-5ba9-a574-72530dddbf74" :question/math-academy-id "q-161789" :question/problem "![](images/08/085e28508da725e445c410fedc08a1992dccc5e0a7b99b3248182f58ffae2c07.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4z,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a730a72fe9f3f69878ef647215e8ed05e913f23bf8e0fab01bad6271e860335.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{[\\frac{1}{2}{\\rho }^{2}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta  \\\\  & =8{[-\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(0-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad64f33-04da-536f-b19e-fda75bf79d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481920} {:answer/id #uuid "5250dc04-dac8-5129-8673-b9a3ff91da85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{4}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481973} {:answer/id #uuid "53891021-4964-5177-a087-40074c3388c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481975} {:answer/id #uuid "bac58cc0-72ac-5a02-bb3a-4587ad3cce80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{4}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482219} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ebb7ed-a4dc-5ae7-a75b-d082ec3f13f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481920} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce36ee07-fbbc-5e29-8d0c-a6748c1dafd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a047214-1415-5946-9ec9-da29ccac571a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161774" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/02/02834e98afa14d5193e5d560f85457e73caee7f5938af5ac8b3fb607165aad53.png)\n\n \nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92943d53-64ab-5eb3-b983-45be687c3731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482119} {:answer/id #uuid "b744b3f7-8807-53b3-a991-de88c0855263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482211} {:answer/id #uuid "ba023976-39af-52af-8116-b3398085ad4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482217} {:answer/id #uuid "bef84112-3ae3-59b4-8acd-c836d7e8a7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482230} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d548e7-7e33-5671-81f5-4f4350c23585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482230} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8e45e3-b187-55ef-8895-829cbde8baad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "75e6fbad-2ffa-554a-b73e-206d1de35163" :question/math-academy-id "q-161921" :question/problem "![](images/54/54ff64195ccfadddfe2b3ca0bb9e193147fedae20d8dae6a53a5ecddc3c26f5d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-16y$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $8$ centered at $(0,-8,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-16y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-16y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-16\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/86/868214a1c2b30da5b16b7f0c307b369bdc9f2d2201ee0ad5a3f5da2a8bdc6f49.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127543e8-793d-5a57-a8aa-34e7946706c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{36}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481823} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3c2c33-40a9-5bfb-8e72-a111146071ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481854} {:answer/id #uuid "9172fead-91e9-5a66-a2fa-3f44604487dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{36}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482117} {:answer/id #uuid "c77d6454-6af7-5b8b-a02e-7768e6bb2a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482249} {:answer/id #uuid "f53097bb-f59d-547e-b750-92460571ca57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482355}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482355} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1dcc32-b424-50ae-8bc7-6bf9e3e11130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80477a9d-e7af-5d9c-aba6-cf99ec36f94b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161762" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $6$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =((\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2})\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcb9cd7-9763-5072-aea1-40956674c5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481805} {:answer/id #uuid "3f89b472-b769-5a09-b612-61bb4b463d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481933} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3113d8-4993-57ed-9b8e-75b1863a90d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481995} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b152c3-3ea3-55b4-aa0c-ac2b236c1936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482242} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d5ffdc-8a6e-57db-9529-bc88c792c05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481995} :answer-field/id #uuid "a40abc4f-2947-5476-abce-6432706d5eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac302ee6-6bbe-5388-bf60-4247113c7536" :question/math-academy-id "q-161750" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32cfd7a5-0554-5c53-becb-3f321129eb74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481897} {:answer/id #uuid "370eb795-4874-5cf9-8f5c-cdd4a06415a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481913} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9b0a73-84b0-590f-9a4e-7f5d19046ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481923} {:answer/id #uuid "bb855a55-4eb5-563e-aef7-ff1f60e275c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482222} {:answer/id #uuid "cad5272c-2c13-5856-85fe-8cf9637d07f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481897} :answer-field/id #uuid "747ee5c7-5db1-5a10-9753-ab1d396bd128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6dfd2f6-e989-5dc0-8a5f-19807faa0173" :question/math-academy-id "q-161743" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3434e6cd-ecc2-5e44-a0a7-04b070694a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481902} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc19d2a-7bec-50b3-8ef7-793fd45f87b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{20\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481990} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbc2c65-9f4e-5d10-88b8-a6de92a9ed7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482022} {:answer/id #uuid "9edf5219-4946-5276-ac39-fe2b2d568af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482153} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a1f2b2-3392-5a63-814e-8db5a857c76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482153} :answer-field/id #uuid "46a73c28-db4d-5b08-9210-44c3f3e37fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ba16213a-d9f2-5675-b515-f66161bd7267" :question/math-academy-id "q-161922" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf44412afd6f2a8eaab468676b4f6a9828f5000eec287234f9834a41e6816d5a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=20x$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $10$ centered at $(10,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=20x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =20x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =20\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/16/165ed50d8f94f3b896f4d19647fb514f544221872a6df241a6ab6c3797bbd698.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0693ddaa-d57f-543c-88a6-214dedc7f19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\ln \\mid 7\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0693ddaa-d57f-543c-88a6-214dedc7f19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\ln \\mid 7\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482726} {:answer/id #uuid "24edd72b-715e-5a20-b0ca-bc6d4e2db918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 7\\coth x - 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482736} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc5a37-f636-5626-9a99-a373d2b4423d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 7\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482741} {:answer/id #uuid "ee541a2a-4ae9-5a6e-a578-9429f597af8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\ln \\mid 7\\coth x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482792} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b56a90-21a3-5531-9006-1dd7d3e8aa29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482792} :answer-field/id #uuid "7302cb6a-8492-5b5f-81a1-bf9531732d52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c398ccb-7526-5cc6-8db1-8a59a9908269" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\csch^{2} x}{7\\coth x + 1}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet $u=7\\coth x+1$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-7\\csch^2 x,\n\\qquad \\csch^2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.$$\n\nUsing this substitution, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{\\csch^2 x}{7\\coth x+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=-\\frac{1}{7}\\int\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{7}\\ln|u|+C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{7}\\ln|7\\coth x+1|+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe antiderivative is valid on each interval where $x\\ne0$ and $7\\coth x+1\\ne0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e315d9a-b4bf-5cb3-b8f6-6a0dbacad0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\ln \\mid 5\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482751} {:answer/id #uuid "70a7f11a-d3a7-5538-881f-52fd516e4a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 5\\coth x - 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482757} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac5f141-8211-574c-a6ac-c02d7bc0ee4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}\\ln \\mid 5\\coth x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482768} {:answer/id #uuid "be393951-fcfe-585c-8080-6b2fa216061e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 5\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482778} {:answer/id #uuid "f54cb666-0323-52f7-864c-3e9c51669b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482768} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac1d5a97-9421-5ba3-bdc9-ee671f9d2143" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3bf041bc-30ad-591c-8fbb-083bb48f845f" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\csch^{2} x}{5\\coth x + 1}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet $u=5\\coth x+1$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-5\\csch^2 x,\n\\qquad \\csch^2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.$$\n\nUsing this substitution, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{\\csch^2 x}{5\\coth x+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\int\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\ln|u|+C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\ln|5\\coth x+1|+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe antiderivative is valid on each interval where $x\\ne0$ and $5\\coth x+1\\ne0$."} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc5a37-f636-5626-9a99-a373d2b4423d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 7\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482741} {:answer/id #uuid "59ee84ab-2073-532b-8046-9ef988b617f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 6\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482749} {:answer/id #uuid "5e315d9a-b4bf-5cb3-b8f6-6a0dbacad0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\ln \\mid 5\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482751} {:answer/id #uuid "6af5598e-fcdd-5093-a2bc-d17f5957f1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482755} {:answer/id #uuid "71ca45c0-b5a8-5059-92d1-5f4e6912628a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482758} {:answer/id #uuid "7c803a1f-d083-58cf-88a0-a1b2ac3de33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 4\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482763} {:answer/id #uuid "812dc02b-7e05-5eb3-9f95-062b2764166f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\ln \\mid 4\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef1fcaa-1646-5614-873d-25a0df23f7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2\\coth x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482743} {:answer/id #uuid "43b92367-cc58-5489-9b0f-151faf1d9e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2\\coth x - 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482744} {:answer/id #uuid "6af5598e-fcdd-5093-a2bc-d17f5957f1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482755} {:answer/id #uuid "71ca45c0-b5a8-5059-92d1-5f4e6912628a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482758} {:answer/id #uuid "e43dbeec-3b99-5fd7-9570-7caac7a652cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482743} :answer-field/id #uuid "07f85100-8712-59cf-a27d-56dd1e288072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d7703e8-d1c3-5dcd-a034-b13a6e5e635d" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\csch^{2} x}{2\\coth x + 1}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet $u=2\\coth x+1$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2\\csch^2 x,\n\\qquad \\csch^2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.$$\n\nUsing this substitution, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{\\csch^2 x}{2\\coth x+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln|u|+C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln|2\\coth x+1|+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe antiderivative is valid on each interval where $x\\ne0$ and $2\\coth x+1\\ne0$."} {:answer/id #uuid "a5795b39-533b-549c-9d87-942fa5ad45e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e559296-77e3-5ce6-9097-3e91f43b48e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\ln \\mid 4\\coth x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482730} {:answer/id #uuid "7c803a1f-d083-58cf-88a0-a1b2ac3de33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 4\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482763} {:answer/id #uuid "812dc02b-7e05-5eb3-9f95-062b2764166f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\ln \\mid 4\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482765} {:answer/id #uuid "a5795b39-533b-549c-9d87-942fa5ad45e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482771} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0b05d1-cfc6-5619-8a71-3a49fac424d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 4\\coth x - 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482730} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f79df49-f55f-5434-bbe8-e27ec72c9856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae74f884-ae1a-5c74-b1db-3a06095c05ad" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\csch^{2} x}{4\\coth x + 1}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet $u=4\\coth x+1$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-4\\csch^2 x,\n\\qquad \\csch^2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.$$\n\nUsing this substitution, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{\\csch^2 x}{4\\coth x+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\\int\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\\ln|u|+C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\\ln|4\\coth x+1|+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe antiderivative is valid on each interval where $x\\ne0$ and $4\\coth x+1\\ne0$."} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1f2263-13a3-5a79-9615-1d482ce9b6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\ln \\mid 6\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482777} {:answer/id #uuid "be393951-fcfe-585c-8080-6b2fa216061e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 5\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4864266c-e5e5-515b-96a0-ab9e8b35f984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}\\ln \\mid 6\\coth x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482747} {:answer/id #uuid "59ee84ab-2073-532b-8046-9ef988b617f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 6\\csch^{2} x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482749} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f5d408-6e65-55f1-8aa8-27f86c94cef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 6\\coth x - 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482772} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1f2263-13a3-5a79-9615-1d482ce9b6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\ln \\mid 6\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482777} {:answer/id #uuid "da6323a2-207f-5205-883d-61a3c6b128a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482747} :answer-field/id #uuid "199783f9-8097-5c43-9068-0b16831af24d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c918efe9-1489-587b-b27f-7807064bc2ae" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\csch^{2} x}{6\\coth x + 1}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet $u=6\\coth x+1$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-6\\csch^2 x,\n\\qquad \\csch^2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.$$\n\nUsing this substitution, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{\\csch^2 x}{6\\coth x+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=-\\frac{1}{6}\\int\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{6}\\ln|u|+C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{6}\\ln|6\\coth x+1|+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe antiderivative is valid on each interval where $x\\ne0$ and $6\\coth x+1\\ne0$."} {:answer/id #uuid "da6323a2-207f-5205-883d-61a3c6b128a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482786} {:answer/id #uuid "e43dbeec-3b99-5fd7-9570-7caac7a652cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2\\csch x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482788} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b56a90-21a3-5531-9006-1dd7d3e8aa29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482793} {:answer/id #uuid "f54cb666-0323-52f7-864c-3e9c51669b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}\\ln \\mid \\csch^{2} x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482794} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd0b7d8-497a-5308-9309-036b9effbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482828} {:answer/id #uuid "11be0602-805c-5751-80e1-20619f10d315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482841} {:answer/id #uuid "1663c28c-4cc3-57a0-901a-0782cd1f5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482854} {:answer/id #uuid "38ff41e9-dd02-5d21-92a8-581369d18b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482910} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483115} {:answer/id #uuid "e62cdbaf-ad20-51f2-b72a-b2f5ab5a24eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{18-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{8-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10-26\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},\n$$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f3cc0b-c41a-5321-9ece-52fd3df64297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482965} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6d1a51-1150-5aa3-a923-b68b4f05b638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483003} {:answer/id #uuid "704a8b44-8729-5565-b1a0-2928425f981b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483007} {:answer/id #uuid "c19dca01-b95f-5b0c-9166-3c0789c3ce6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483137} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcdefc7-8d2b-5c46-b8de-33fa51d9e81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483137} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd91fbcc-9a1e-515e-941d-2e16f7668942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c8f03a1-964a-5fd4-a60f-a2aa229186e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-43048" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=6$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(6) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500ad374-633d-5acc-8fc6-2c954b7ceee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482954} {:answer/id #uuid "7daf2602-5ddf-5b93-9c8f-85bf86b1183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} {:answer/id #uuid "c4195c77-54de-55db-b8bc-7aa9584420e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483138} {:answer/id #uuid "dd638516-5b00-5d2e-b7d7-70996ad3e87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483185} {:answer/id #uuid "eb789a46-0304-547b-8796-b1b162722c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} :answer-field/id #uuid "416c49b7-a3b7-5531-9507-24f1c4541b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6017b841-30bb-5249-81e7-cac41367e88a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123548" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9ea317575efcb32d8234309e80fecfd4385c99dbe69dd9c33d3fa7d80313bb6.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d839b3-7560-5512-ab94-65b25afb8af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482817} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd0b7d8-497a-5308-9309-036b9effbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482828} {:answer/id #uuid "11be0602-805c-5751-80e1-20619f10d315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482841} {:answer/id #uuid "1663c28c-4cc3-57a0-901a-0782cd1f5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482854} {:answer/id #uuid "e62cdbaf-ad20-51f2-b72a-b2f5ab5a24eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482854} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f7e3dc-e6fb-53cc-b486-dbf54e61e1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80893bd5-f8f9-5d69-b7d0-36e40ddfd184" :question/math-academy-id "q-76572" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614ec109-ddf5-5d69-8b6f-788cffd7d795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} {:answer/id #uuid "654af1d0-1b91-562a-9226-854b3fe81f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482990} {:answer/id #uuid "6bec234f-7e3b-515c-a2ad-33a2c8025357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482999} {:answer/id #uuid "960e21ae-a0b1-56a4-a8e3-7aa555730f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483072} {:answer/id #uuid "c60010fa-7943-5d78-8597-6d6fec062ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} :answer-field/id #uuid "87114391-cb4d-5092-83cc-b2e18baed326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dcd1fd46-503a-5471-9362-496d88e411cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-149819" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){cos}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)[{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)]}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}[1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{3}\\right)]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{3}\\right)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236acfde-42f2-51a3-82ca-3600a09ce0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482876} {:answer/id #uuid "38ff41e9-dd02-5d21-92a8-581369d18b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482910} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac4e6e-fe81-5a84-9636-cabf10c5314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc1c101-f384-5d1e-ab31-e736b726cbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf074d1f-8612-5220-b189-30860f156e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4160743-42ed-5b1d-80ee-6f99ed405134" :question/math-academy-id "q-149786" :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061d1324-c902-50ac-9f19-d8b4a5784809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483526} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1f1314-7647-545e-b012-8ae1c258500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483531} {:answer/id #uuid "6dac38e5-adce-5d03-bb83-d08ba2555997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483533} {:answer/id #uuid "856d6ef1-16cb-533f-8b58-6fd8f077de44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483534} {:answer/id #uuid "89742711-38f2-5693-8a77-c2b1336920a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483533} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fe8258d-1897-5414-82b1-02fddba2f0e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "06ead9d9-ba18-5b78-9c0b-6c3f9d9d335d" :question/problem "The derivative of $y=2x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =2-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2-{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488ebcb1-34b1-54b2-bb0f-d3834972fc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+2{sec}^{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483530} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9aa3437-62e4-5f8b-b2f8-54736d53760f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16f98c04-c89a-5e4f-bf27-ed015bececed" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+2tan⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+2tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+2({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285ce513-2344-54e5-878f-50e519f82f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+3{sec}^{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483529} :answer-field/id #uuid "e01d02f0-76ac-5033-8fce-0f78e59a051a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec85ab69-3759-5a26-ad41-7f26f7d7092f" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+3({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+3{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "366bb306-e5b1-5bce-82a8-a14197bda5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483664} {:answer/id #uuid "5c836208-8886-5ac5-a42b-bc5298710b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483742} {:answer/id #uuid "66dd1cc6-ae10-55ca-805b-f651571a416c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483766} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e66de3-9711-58eb-a4b6-9c6f7f7d5114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483922} {:answer/id #uuid "cf227642-8c60-5fac-8a8c-4941679de5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483922} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac158dd-e2ea-5d6f-9ec0-895c0248c590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "00ee9625-b07b-5511-af62-7520bef2fbbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-82287" :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- For the matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 3-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+2det(B)=5\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04efdac5-3dd6-5c5f-8a26-b2759cf8283c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483576} {:answer/id #uuid "59ee36a8-3df4-5abd-9635-4a64fbb2b20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483735} {:answer/id #uuid "6881cacb-49d1-5796-ac41-85af50ef8c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483770} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5ed0e6-bb61-546d-a212-902bf1dcb965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483861} {:answer/id #uuid "9c10c03d-47ea-5d76-845a-038143841e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483861} :answer-field/id #uuid "68dc34c5-a4cf-5701-b2b5-6b8a0f11f690" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "099dc702-c2e9-5790-a5a1-762c52cdef4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130632" :question/problem "Let $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ be square matrices. If $A=B\\cdot C,$  $|B|=24$ and $|{C}^{-1}|=4$, find $|A|.$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nAlso, given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({M}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(M)}.\n$$\n\nSince $|C|=\\frac{1}{|{C}^{-1}|}$, we get $|C|=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|B|\\cdot |C| \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acfc248-b5ab-54fa-8eb8-3479fdad0217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483590} {:answer/id #uuid "4b107cbc-1aec-5e53-8ab7-cac57bdb14f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483702} {:answer/id #uuid "7e63a3c5-498c-5a2e-b046-09467c9969f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483824} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c2c137-72d0-5dfc-8aa8-6b6268ba7b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483930} {:answer/id #uuid "e76766a9-b27c-5c4f-ae2b-31a477472e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484026} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ca7507-fbd3-5777-94c4-ec567590b1b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bcf73b5-94e8-5857-a9f9-54d536a26fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-88265" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(8-(-3))\\cdot (-5-1) \\\\  & =11\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483673} :answer-field/id #uuid "83efe503-c6c7-5398-8b5a-e3c31636860c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34386db1-c26a-52c0-8482-81ad51998468" :question/math-academy-id "q-358781" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a728dd52-17c5-5bbe-8ae1-9335ea32a209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483909} {:answer/id #uuid "c97eaa67-5423-5696-a85d-e65f81cf7222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483965} {:answer/id #uuid "cba33b1f-74be-596c-ab36-00796559159b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483974} {:answer/id #uuid "e79780cf-7d17-561d-9e14-064715c15dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484027} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a2cfdc-549e-5021-bdb9-3d2b4d0cce92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483974} :answer-field/id #uuid "7173e51f-ca84-5cf0-ad24-28894c605bbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "76f24dd6-dfcb-56d2-ac88-5f5a55e3e851" :question/math-academy-id "q-88273" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(H).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(H)=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(2-(-6)\\cdot 3(2-3) \\\\  & =32\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483884} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d3585e-134e-53e5-b3ff-d54d2085a716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd425a39-318f-5566-a2e2-b27e6f8eab87" :question/math-academy-id "q-223098" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩.\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c437605-0d0b-5911-8b6f-efde64e63238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484907} {:answer/id #uuid "4b086b6e-3f56-53aa-ab01-3ec1ea51c4f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484982} {:answer/id #uuid "7328d2ac-ec4a-5f15-8f58-faebd2e52167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485064} {:answer/id #uuid "7663b203-9ab4-5d32-b8ff-e2d87fc22238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485070} {:answer/id #uuid "9697230b-8f6a-5224-a0f4-267b300f27ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484907} :answer-field/id #uuid "048ab02b-a33b-534b-a1c7-2bf319cf0b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "35c37699-f2ab-5493-8b62-dd508d98be99" :question/math-academy-id "q-84784" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. If $A=PB{P}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $2P+Q$ is **not** always orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(P)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f44f82f-0911-50e6-9955-8927e6dd0cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485928} {:answer/id #uuid "90f4e46f-b8dc-57a7-9776-c1970edf16db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486026} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2315d4-5189-559b-9833-b72d4cf16c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486129} {:answer/id #uuid "e84a642c-b9c7-5a08-aa9a-27173d4089c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486191} {:answer/id #uuid "f76e5d23-1c27-5b09-a2a5-f67217c56999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486026} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b2e81e-78c5-5bf6-853c-77f6b9242995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47ce2283-c10d-5cc5-8c7c-2556a8407b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-79233" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=-\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{sin}^{3}⁡t,{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ T(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩ \\\\ N(-\\pi ) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that  the position vector of the point where $t=-\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(-\\pi )=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),{cos}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )⟩=⟨0,-1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\pi )\\times N(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨0,-1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|\\,0-4+3\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe83c22-72da-50e6-834c-b9c00e4e385f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485820} {:answer/id #uuid "2f22d5a5-4012-55e2-9f31-a82a58b92e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485853} {:answer/id #uuid "371ce1fd-1b49-5044-b188-c4be07437ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485869} {:answer/id #uuid "a47da2ab-eb87-5c1e-a609-65ef7d35cb5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486060} {:answer/id #uuid "efccc9a7-cb24-5d0b-bfc7-6c5800ec814c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485820} :answer-field/id #uuid "ada9aa39-633e-53ef-a441-e0ab2035bd78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8febc0ce-a013-5227-b117-55a62f849158" :question/math-academy-id "q-98807" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),-(0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c824f8b-b12d-596a-b458-0b38eb6a5d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485847} {:answer/id #uuid "36129af3-9d71-53a7-ab78-a9f6e3fc98d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485867} {:answer/id #uuid "477f2e8f-f5d1-50d8-aa98-26b5ffbf9d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485896} {:answer/id #uuid "7074b204-75cd-52eb-8138-272ab4f76fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485958} {:answer/id #uuid "bebd21ce-52e7-5d1e-9493-b2bc0f6b6c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486104} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dfc3adb-1e75-5b3f-be5e-d82fddc905b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb6bca21-eef1-511a-9545-14146c32b845" :question/math-academy-id "q-99652" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)),0-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6684f7-7607-5c50-8a99-f0423b0f6cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485852} {:answer/id #uuid "3993447f-c94c-5806-9658-5a3fd2138263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485877} {:answer/id #uuid "76c40781-b607-5cd9-9d60-36ce71e2a7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485970} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c0152b-83d2-5b8d-9343-96df53ed9b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486064} {:answer/id #uuid "cf43edcf-7e65-57a5-b57e-f9bee6b95852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485970} :answer-field/id #uuid "42dd43f0-f4c7-5c5f-9fd8-9efa733dad89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2c660e0-cf11-56d9-9d4f-3449a41167f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-127612" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069c8840-8426-5a49-8cd5-194f9542f047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485779} {:answer/id #uuid "2e481c8d-1f08-54f2-a266-5bbd2762473f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485851} {:answer/id #uuid "93d03cdd-069e-5ebe-b2a7-900eec2dd6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486030} {:answer/id #uuid "a74c6630-89ff-50d5-a8b8-e49610f1abd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486067} {:answer/id #uuid "b94c247d-0ad1-594f-abae-d08d3203dd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486099} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58af114-b2ae-5846-9a75-5301fb89508f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c746a2db-cfe0-519b-a0f3-936f85b88bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140520" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 7-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35+12 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076372f8-106e-57e0-804d-a269c2fc2d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-\\sqrt{2},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485781} {:answer/id #uuid "757938d5-ce6f-5416-961c-6ffe6161c32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-\\sqrt{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485968} {:answer/id #uuid "c54c8b06-a439-54ca-a60f-361ab446e91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486119} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc2311a-157a-542b-a6f7-94dbd06a477a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486225} {:answer/id #uuid "febb6e78-dc07-54ef-851d-b1fd5014d597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486225} :answer-field/id #uuid "411e5861-73e4-579c-a78f-046093d8a6ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8e555e8-b363-57e5-af62-374122f991ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-127694" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡5t+25{cos}^{2}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t-0,-({cos}^{2}⁡5t+{sin}^{2}⁡5t),0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7b7700-3e95-510c-a153-82350219ac3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486620} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486626} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa61d86-b0f9-5a61-a276-87b1e8131516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486655} {:answer/id #uuid "21924e2c-8b26-5c46-847f-8a11b488aaf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486661} {:answer/id #uuid "2b532e7e-fcc3-5f04-a240-fffc7b69c0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(C)=-2\\in C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486679} {:answer/id #uuid "2f73ae8d-f2e8-5cf5-bf54-d34ec79a90c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=3\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486690} {:answer/id #uuid "31002c35-5985-5fde-b87e-39583a093fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486696} {:answer/id #uuid "314f5e4e-8552-5814-90d8-a38916281f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=1