nswer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4864d7f7-3780-55dd-b783-6573d4daf004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486750} {:answer/id #uuid "54d00e77-0e88-595d-bcb3-3ac3e038a005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486777} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0b2308-5ec0-5f5e-9192-b34d743148a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486793} {:answer/id #uuid "62975047-760f-52de-ab4c-f1ffc5e4d74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486793} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3fb587f-d8af-5cf0-9966-1bfb2fed1833" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acf7e0f-2106-53cc-9639-b3207e1a41ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486756} {:answer/id #uuid "54e7b8de-fe38-5075-be29-cec30a3e2c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486778} {:answer/id #uuid "935532cf-dd16-513d-9732-e19cb9a54202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486917} {:answer/id #uuid "be190364-76b5-55c4-af60-92f63074f555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486917} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ec095c-9a68-5427-9d9b-5bd0d6b73ffe" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60981b2b-035b-5158-a36c-3b0a5f268ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-253560" :question/problem "Consider the set $B=(-1,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded above, $B$ has no upper bounds, and $sup(B)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-1.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=+\\infty .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21924e2c-8b26-5c46-847f-8a11b488aaf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486661} {:answer/id #uuid "96d1ac88-15ae-5c73-a141-867400b768e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486929} {:answer/id #uuid "970f9583-5c69-59ca-ba57-45f24412729b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486931} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cbdaa1-5a69-566a-b744-1cb7429950de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486996} {:answer/id #uuid "bb516a43-df07-5641-b235-fe98c46c7066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486996} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d681e8-6c35-50f8-ba73-9aaaae945560" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79e51d03-1539-5ef1-be77-7756304d3608" :question/math-academy-id "q-253831" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/78/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=2\\notin A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903caf77-7916-5c64-b9e2-451a898585c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e79c778e-75ba-5f9f-8b03-a34683fe89e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "956f7419-f174-5327-980b-e8eb653a771d" :question/math-academy-id "q-253859" :question/problem "![](images/37/372aa80e15465694347dce5325537a10be1217e83050d609dfeec0347bad1116.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5b6359-8ba6-54a7-9c57-08e6b84383f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486622} :answer-field/id #uuid "9607233e-8d50-5f50-8cd6-15fab5dcb74a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2831798-1168-5ea1-b772-79e02ad4074c" :question/math-academy-id "q-252989" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{n+2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $A?$\n\n$inf(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the infimum of $A$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1)+2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)+2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)+2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)+2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(A) & =min\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fa61d86-b0f9-5a61-a276-87b1e8131516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486655} {:answer/id #uuid "31002c35-5985-5fde-b87e-39583a093fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486696} {:answer/id #uuid "7e78293d-aec3-58b5-a329-6318d6d9600d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486867} {:answer/id #uuid "800778ee-d630-5816-9df2-7a05075e38f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486873} {:answer/id #uuid "95715de7-9216-56ef-b8bf-3a456d9858be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486696} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0ca0f3-190b-55df-a54b-77392ca10b39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4eaf9cf-b8c1-5f93-bf45-1dc2cc8b5b67" :question/math-academy-id "q-253525" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/72/72fa47f25042b5187a87262b23e0976429909011b1516bc6018e70cd4f881f11.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=2-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486626} {:answer/id #uuid "314f5e4e-8552-5814-90d8-a38916281f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486698} {:answer/id #uuid "39e361d5-1d4c-5a24-9857-936433e94187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486717} {:answer/id #uuid "3a52c1c9-d934-5a2b-a0a5-58f417b57457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486718} {:answer/id #uuid "c27fc419-17a3-5364-b645-76f73a7ab6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486626} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4df8d23-1851-528c-ba22-425776fee81a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aafdf357-e5ff-5cb5-9dea-31946c44ff01" :question/math-academy-id "q-253910" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/42/42f6ea3d1c14b0ccb8774e284dc6812486ace7eda512e9c1b94df27e09445e6e.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{2}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{2}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b568e14-4a85-5aaa-bcc8-8992d65e0615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486825} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b104c6-1821-5e63-ba3c-9ed8fdb7a761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487066} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4c07c6-ea87-547e-81bb-3747ed48d208" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11110ba5-df4f-5d23-9728-d5226ca00e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486629} {:answer/id #uuid "2348a4bc-e6a2-5dec-82ad-49e02ecaea77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486664} {:answer/id #uuid "2f07c23f-4620-576c-835d-4636e57a30b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486687} {:answer/id #uuid "880331aa-e53f-596b-a542-a70d2ffa08c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486629} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa59b90b-b072-5672-8d8f-940ec810e0d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b532e7e-fcc3-5f04-a240-fffc7b69c0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(C)=-2\\in C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486679} {:answer/id #uuid "5aeeaf2b-e22f-5929-a602-8a420267ddc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$sup(C)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486799} {:answer/id #uuid "71e29f52-5060-5d27-9c9d-18f7d7566204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(C)=-1\\notin C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486842} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2c87cf-ca24-56a0-8947-a8ba427cb930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(C)=-2\\notin C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486905} {:answer/id #uuid "c617c0bd-a5c7-575e-b606-3fd8870f54a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(C)=-1\\in C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487033} :answer-field/id #uuid "cac80e9a-f2fc-5d5a-bce9-bb59b0d3e2fa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d66afd5-954f-52aa-85ff-8a35f220352d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smallest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486761} {:answer/id #uuid "8606d74b-1672-5547-ac2f-bf30ca3c0c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greatest lower bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486884} {:answer/id #uuid "961420ad-19e2-55b8-b2a0-134bcc6fe491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smallest lower bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486926} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9cbb60-93e6-5126-a662-317f82561921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greatest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486761} :answer-field/id #uuid "cccdc7ac-4c98-5946-bd46-0e11422f5ea2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "be9aa3b1-68a0-5981-aa70-f10a99f6fbac" :question/math-academy-id "q-253556" :question/problem "Consider the set $C=(-2,-1].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(C).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $C$ is the {{field-1}} of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $C$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $C.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $C.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $C.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $C$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $C,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(C)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is also the maximum of $C.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $C$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(C)=-1\\in C.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f35f40f9-7147-561f-97bb-d22e896e1a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6{e}^{3}+3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487113} :answer-field/id #uuid "f39b1857-9db3-543c-8023-433cabfb635c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dec6c4d2-4bd8-5abe-a87c-7b60b0c36fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-351045" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}x\\left(\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{log}_{5}⁡(({e}^{3})^{3})\\,({e}^{3})-{log}_{5}⁡({1}^{3})\\,(1)]-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡({e}^{9})-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{3}+3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44beebbd-07c2-5851-ba19-b05775c541e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486743} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e05f0be-24c8-5b6d-94b7-cae94e3dae5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df0bc2ee-bd6a-5b2e-965b-ef9ca16078ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-253543" :question/problem "What is the supremum of the set $B=\\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}?$\n\n$sup(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $B$ is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the supremum of $B$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(B) & =max\\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252a827a-27e1-56cc-a90d-f6f9674a6156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486668} {:answer/id #uuid "433e28c3-b394-5e48-a90f-bc877296e02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486739} {:answer/id #uuid "6d28a39c-a153-5fa2-a9f5-3ad9ccaca055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486833} {:answer/id #uuid "bae24965-ce67-56c6-accb-5b0bf1b67ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487003} {:answer/id #uuid "be43907f-8580-500b-b7d5-33eabf652329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486833} :answer-field/id #uuid "83eacc45-d857-565f-946e-439539864205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e2daa4c1-fd00-5b69-bf1e-328646061d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-12571" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡(5x))(2\\sqrt{x})-\\int (2\\sqrt{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040a4111-a501-5a1a-974a-448a32278cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greatest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486601} {:answer/id #uuid "4f23177a-93ce-5142-9f40-15b70c960726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smallest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486764} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4c1e75-9d45-5d8a-9117-bfa1cb4073ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smallest lo