e {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le \\pi $ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>\\pi $ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,\\pi ]=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\pi \\notin B.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(B)=\\pi \\notin B.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31543bec-7415-5c0a-b5bd-5fe9b03eaa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490604} {:answer/id #uuid "66c0e3c3-432e-504d-94dc-f89ea3bb6e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490704} {:answer/id #uuid "7d275fd5-7007-526a-9ff9-feaa35579085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490760} {:answer/id #uuid "df2b4c83-9aa8-57e9-a751-379ed8038581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490933} {:answer/id #uuid "fc17b842-d919-52cc-a3ae-905dd3045738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490704} :answer-field/id #uuid "756d9f31-195b-5f29-8f95-74ae53b26047" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "402a4d55-ba8d-55eb-b6d0-1bc9813966c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-253134" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/69/690c0466ffac72597e9378539d1540d6baaa49c7cbe77bee8cc83ab00f065635.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{2}=(-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+1}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{2}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{2}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\in A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d73fffb6-c0cf-56c1-a90a-76d9eb8a337d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490919} :answer-field/id #uuid "55659e34-da0a-5194-8763-2b2f67c805c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "423e9057-895c-5e42-b0de-0a5a3aa83973" :question/math-academy-id "q-253547" :question/problem "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+1}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $B?$\n\n$inf(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=0,1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}+1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(B) & =min\\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}=\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb116da-9df3-555f-baf3-f38a3417b2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490567} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8ffe60-36fa-5b57-a95b-d911f94b7da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490656} {:answer/id #uuid "790bc354-260c-50f7-a9db-e386374e2b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759