-ba77-b735a234672f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2969aa96-f753-5b99-b7a8-4a6298263693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smallest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490588} {:answer/id #uuid "3d29fb49-648e-5a0a-ac6e-c63f1310b6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greatest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490626} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd70f81-2241-589c-8399-e08961b2a81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smallest lower bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490627} {:answer/id #uuid "55a1680b-477c-5c0b-94eb-9979f7a37072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greatest lower bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490588} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa62c7a-3eae-53d1-8bfb-2c44ea119440" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "622a3a58-55bb-5010-91b6-37bedd13a1ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-253559" :question/problem "Consider the set $D=[\\sqrt{3},4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(D).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $D$ is the {{field-1}} of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $D$ is the smallest upper bound of $D.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $D$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge 4$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $D.$ Thus, the set $[4,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $D.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<4$ is **not** an upper bound of $D.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $D$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<4.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $D,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(D)=min[4,\\infty )=4.\n$$\n\nNotice that $4\\notin D.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $D$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge 4\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(D)=4\\notin D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea94f50-c7a7-5689-a3e2-07536e394575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491602} {:answer/id #uuid "619e8956-a4dc-535d-9bbc-e8f309b768c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491629} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8db3ff-eee5-5fbf-93b0-295940eacd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491775} {:answer/id #uuid "c37deb26-59ff-5b78-9971-a3b1623542f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491790} {:answer/id #uuid "d2235f79-7130-5140-b61b-f3ad01e68069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491811} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2c4d76-d1ed-5ea1-9132-eb93755f0b15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "06dbce8a-d24c-5a95-a85b-452eb260bee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-139463" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)<0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${a}_{11}=1,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2870427b-9fcb-5fd7-b0fd-1a1a1b0dc4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491538} {:answer/id #uuid "353d2804-a43e-5df9-80ef-d3844280008d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491559} {:answer/id #uuid "7e064b2d-d342-5924-8c38-37c0f7e7f14a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491676} {:answer/id #uuid "97265fa4-83a5-5552-82a3-d03e500d17ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491713} {:answer/id #uuid "a67f422f-ea71-5192-afa8-1cf02f8737c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491745} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c46275-3046-5183-a66a-80853ae0abe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6c5544d-5a76-5194-9e05-adccd06c8464" :question/math-academy-id "q-36223" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$,  given that the curve and its unit tangent $T(t)$ are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,1⟩,\\,T(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of the osculating circle: \n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the radius of the osculating circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9e2ab8-f0d5-5abe-b74e-db6a46609bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492276} {:answer/id #uuid "208b060c-d7f6-5417-9e03-796577024e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492282} {:answer/id #uuid "96521b38-e0b7-58e6-b1db-eeedf6a34c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492507} {:answer/id #uuid "c0497484-5b80-5f14-9367-302334efa418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492600} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b05c42-2662-52e1-be4f-95016554eeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492600} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd63ecac-8751-5520-937a-d3c900eac80e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c7906bc-d806-5034-89ba-962181be4d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-170402" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $0.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4c2ff5-edba-514a-87a6-58daf1e367d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492271} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0214e5-26b9-58f4-866b-8bc611fb2ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492397} {:answer/id #uuid "d70b4dc0-8536-581a-8bd5-450e119abdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492642} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d2221-dc50-598c-ba5a-1cb5379b5184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492672} {:answer/id #uuid "f99577b0-7bae-5456-97fc-603ae4a89241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492642} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ec5a8e-4792-5032-a904-759d0e616ab6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1e463920-832a-54ce-b343-95505e40d97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170527" :question/problem "The minimum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9,$ is $-18.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-18,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-18.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-18:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-18)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+18\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-18)I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "39a9acae-d6a3-505a-9113-cf64e8d3f284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(z-1){sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493486} {:answer/id #uuid "e118469f-bcd0-5c50-aab2-4bee0d032ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(z-1)^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1be8b56-e0bc-581a-9ccb-692511badae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493679} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f4070c-c95c-5ed1-901f-4528153978ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43534459-b512-57ce-a24a-a07661c93918" :question/math-academy-id "q-352679" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So, we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{\\,3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d681392-0578-56c7-9fe2-42e3d7339e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493615} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2d075a9-50ba-561b-90ab-b8b2901e72d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d59d88c8-9c62-51d9-bd12-48f5c362cc67" :question/math-academy-id "q-352652" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "02c3d150-55f6-525d-b55a-6951b89555eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495516} {:answer/id #uuid "0641edc9-9f46-539c-b517-a7dff5678e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495527} {:answer/id #uuid "19b76607-eca3-5a84-8112-1e82b79c1646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495561} {:answer/id #uuid "1afd572b-1bde-5282-aebe-538e26120c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218