ho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495912} {:answer/id #uuid "c1695142-7e5b-54c7-a0c8-e74c43072eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495936} {:answer/id #uuid "c737b74c-b7cd-528f-8697-a21b09e1b183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a717243-48be-536d-910a-6d83ab68f329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495536} {:answer/id #uuid "1afd572b-1bde-5282-aebe-538e26120c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495564} {:answer/id #uuid "5a35fc1c-68fd-5d54-b891-f97bb27891a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495714} {:answer/id #uuid "90820d05-7799-52ea-bb46-3e07581e83ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495829} {:answer/id #uuid "cf09a2be-f3f1-58fe-aff8-83664e28906f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495829} :answer-field/id #uuid "851dbeb9-5199-5c05-92dd-f09fe944b1b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07d5c9c5-374d-5c01-b3ec-d01486174f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-161744" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/d2/d2b9c6f3541c47f37ad7f2ba52d739787e39600ec71fe3530f0ad2846c24880b.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b76607-eca3-5a84-8112-1e82b79c1646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495561} {:answer/id #uuid "3454506d-b82b-5713-b11f-01ee75904e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495633} {:answer/id #uuid "37432da4-1104-5db3-8f3f-d145e4ff681b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495638} {:answer/id #uuid "c1695142-7e5b-54c7-a0c8-e74c43072eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495936} {:answer/id #uuid "c737b74c-b7cd-528f-8697-a21b09e1b183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495946} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb52fe98-2353-5c03-b8bc-f23854e8e611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b06611cf-54db-58f0-8277-482f7aa27724" :question/math-academy-id "q-161753" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0782d6f6-9606-57ec-9b7b-5f331ab47d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495529} {:answer/id #uuid "844ba137-ce42-54c2-ad73-0a6795c8a9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495794} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e1bc82-3dca-5d12-a26c-e853c58acc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi }{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495972} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f9f8b0-bd2d-567a-a334-e4d0096c528c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495983} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f5beda-e35c-5b36-a994-c61647e4e3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi }{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495794} :answer-field/id #uuid "452ba3e6-053b-531a-8d38-cad4a5db1e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2868a77-a9f6-5df5-bf81-a733f20b3a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161765" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c3d150-55f6-525d-b55a-6951b89555eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495516} {:answer/id #uuid "0641edc9-9f46-539c-b517-a7dff5678e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495527} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7b57ca-d67c-5580-9698-a81cf1ce11e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495813} {:answer/id #uuid "9a52e985-8c43-591c-a2b1-487e13e3bbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495854} {:answer/id #uuid "b2362d0a-41f0-5512-8b16-672410d72346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495527} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbd266d-a5fa-5b3b-84b2-668b33154b73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3dbf795-87d9-5ee7-946c-df26586b5ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161860" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d2a71ffdce7f02cdfa78881c7e355c9aada4134cf06b663a2deefb3d779b9ca.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8x,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/04/0475e2e272a30318dc4cb31457539a312544b6e14d7e6a8ed3f911d41404b575.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496747} {:answer/id #uuid "3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497127} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496747} {:answer/id #uuid "3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497127} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497197}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :question/math-academy-id "q-352889" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle K(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462d45d4-2587-5767-bbda-82dc90eb9b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496809} {:answer/id #uuid "480be999-cd10-5a4f-83f5-02eb7f7b0275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496819} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1a635-ce6b-5e3d-a212-6ca66dbb9f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0f4e40-8515-583c-aa83-8de5f9788688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a94d15-fe13-5d85-9f7d-69d7a8a6ed69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed7b5966-6097-5c94-afc7-cde3e72271b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344323" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b25027-8221-5098-9167-254d753c3213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497909} {:answer/id #uuid "4f11998a-ad24-5209-8a9f-c63c403858e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497989} {:answer/id #uuid "5bda7aff-8f4a-54a5-aebf-3a9be4dd94f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498017} {:answer/id #uuid "9a175c4f-3333-5a30-a0ee-942d881a95f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498148} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8dc81a-a234-558b-87aa-921ac69d0c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498017} :answer-field/id #uuid "594aa2c5-6ab3-59e0-94ef-12b55bd75a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "023c52b0-f119-5895-8d3f-31a9468a20b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130768" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=6.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d0162a-0c31-57bd-bb88-fdfa4dc8e68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "columns" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497822} {:answer/id #uuid "5f91266e-571d-5556-9e28-0c64ca72bbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "diagonals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498025} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4ccd02-457b-505d-be66-2a3ce1983dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "first row" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498265} {:answer/id #uuid "e0717cf0-2277-5799-aeb4-bd7df48e84fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "rows" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497822} :answer-field/id #uuid "691f6c70-3e94-5cde-bb06-839111864eb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498407} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4909c353-7e8a-5cb8-b25e-ad22a33b46c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497966} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9b9084-c9c1-58b5-a890-2458f7916ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497970} {:answer/id #uuid "aca1d897-83fa-5953-b387-6aa8cac56164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498183} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d5cf45-87b2-58ae-b6b6-07a8c858f7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498183} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b2f140-f551-5793-a29c-4fb476575aec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37508ceb-d284-5a06-b3ec-936f412805af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497922} {:answer/id #uuid "674daa87-495d-58ab-9af5-9cdc5062e82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498045} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9c66a3-67a6-5fd0-9447-e4938b51f47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498094} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a29fc1-da0c-59ab-8e24-af6b4d9da47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498045} :answer-field/id #uuid "848fedd1-7778-5bd5-81c5-da60ff5ead33" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef6c5e9-0b48-5bca-8fc3-2d7acdfe98a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497988} {:answer/id #uuid "726a5dc3-65f5-538d-af5b-3ba8daedca86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498071} {:answer/id #uuid "83149379-a893-5cb3-ad7b-b43718777042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498104} {:answer/id #uuid "e0da1ffb-345f-5ae1-91cb-7693db413e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498071} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e5a0f2-8f10-5531-ae7d-52d463866902" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3fdf52f0-09f2-54b2-864b-627c885837ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-336086" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of invertible matrices." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices."} {:answer/id #uuid "160e2a0c-f074-5874-a5f2-17cc1fc2cae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498777} {:answer/id #uuid "2f63601b-8c18-5c62-9c1d-b23197c0248e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{sin}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0989efb5-1038-59ac-8680-60117382e68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498746} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce9d6eb-3f8f-56eb-a2d0-a4cfacaf7268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498792} {:answer/id #uuid "38d06719-2aef-52ec-b523-72bbe2d5ce07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498855} {:answer/id #uuid "51cc30b4-2098-5551-9641-1f9551e409a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498920} {:answer/id #uuid "52b44f14-c340-50a7-9cae-50d08c357aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498920} :answer-field/id #uuid "f107053f-9248-55df-a0bd-cf3499751023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85d77d15-6969-5117-952d-e2b202d58514" :question/math-academy-id "q-15123" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{({\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160e2a0c-f074-5874-a5f2-17cc1fc2cae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498777} {:answer/id #uuid "6d20f288-51ab-5e95-ad79-fa5df49f89a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498973} {:answer/id #uuid "a35283a7-4adc-5de1-826d-87a387cd4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} {:answer/id #uuid "c87b6998-b8e9-5934-9893-be14c9c09927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499169} {:answer/id #uuid "d102d768-01d6-510f-bd39-ea2452eac6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafcbac5-e575-5e1d-93e5-5e527facd2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d774fdc1-484c-5e5e-b140-afbc35a348d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51202" :question/problem "![](images/87/87a3563153fe0310767a79a96df0df5f6c0824027776b761df4a792b8b9b235e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a682535-8c56-55dd-a035-f6b7cc19fc38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500486} {:answer/id #uuid "6f323c8b-a972-54d0-bbd1-34949e7b25d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500694} {:answer/id #uuid "7be1cdf9-0f94-5f70-8486-f5416dfcc558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500724} {:answer/id #uuid "98ff3c6b-5897-5008-9dfd-a379593dd6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500779} {:answer/id #uuid "a385d26b-a169-54eb-af49-c35e70069687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500796} :answer-field/id #uuid "4264e826-1eca-5166-bc99-fe720168444a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea3775f0-4a19-53d1-b4e1-4eb3518e099f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97670" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{27}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={3}^{2}\\cdot 3=27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313fc3c9-7590-5457-9934-cda93c6d94c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501555} {:answer/id #uuid "4784394b-3ad7-56c7-a59a-38b9a0ea2047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501597} {:answer/id #uuid "55ddd461-5a2f-57c6-b993-62a0d935299c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501623} {:answer/id #uuid "b176ee50-07eb-571e-ad55-5d2c49c89f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501821} {:answer/id #uuid "c216a131-75a7-5cd7-bffb-fff0f617da7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501623} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d770560-9db0-5560-9355-e7dc79cae0de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "221288b9-d358-51b3-a7a7-1f68c1f5fbe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-174396" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(r)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(r)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0$\n\nIII. $r\\in A\\cap B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(r),{1}_{B}(r)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0,$ we conclude that $r$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c956e81-ff45-5e0b-9f25-09b9d9b31488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501668} {:answer/id #uuid "736ab622-ab04-58e2-9a58-9098136b55bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501677} {:answer/id #uuid "7638f7ea-4425-5857-8b90-4445c121054f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501684} {:answer/id #uuid "f91bb51f-796c-5238-a7f9-e0d6cedfe524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501962} {:answer/id #uuid "feed8f1c-d04a-5f27-b4a6-6f25785faaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501971} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b452e7a-b4a9-5355-886f-bd8e112df6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95e868f8-4928-5f0b-9ec7-afed42df8e19" :question/math-academy-id "q-174406" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(2)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(2)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0$\n\nIII. $2\\in A\\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(2),{1}_{B}(2)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0,$ we conclude that $2$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:answer/id #uuid "560e7f3e-0d28-5092-9dc8-f284fb849140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503147} {:answer/id #uuid "96918186-4f3f-5672-8772-478e2533d76d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503278} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d646eb-777f-5cfa-a5f5-fbdbfeb38a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503310} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7a5e34-b197-57ec-bafb-007ac6b6c167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503389} {:answer/id #uuid "da810825-b5ef-5b80-8415-5e95017d7de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43fca01d-214c-5770-b687-855abe991a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503112} :answer-field/id #uuid "a268cdd9-081d-55c2-903c-5d29e55e0ccc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1e63bbce-6bf5-50ed-a823-bd6bd4ad0965" :question/math-academy-id "q-342673" :question/problem "Simplify $tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+1$ |" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}-1}{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}-1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53998127-850c-52ba-bb5b-642fd8be09d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503142} {:answer/id #uuid "646349e3-b930-50b5-8fa8-ca955067823e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503176} {:answer/id #uuid "79f97822-f108-5494-a037-0fa3ba02771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503230} {:answer/id #uuid "9947b2d7-78d2-510d-a31f-a38e1ffbf62d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503286} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c637b0-037f-510d-bebd-8c09cb3851b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503286} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b28165-ff27-54aa-8d84-67be182b4e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5347ea8e-b936-5670-b92f-f003075bcc63" :question/math-academy-id "q-138073" :question/problem "Find the  maximum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "560e7f3e-0d28-5092-9dc8-f284fb849140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503147} {:answer/id #uuid "96918186-4f3f-5672-8772-478e2533d76d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503278} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d646eb-777f-5cfa-a5f5-fbdbfeb38a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503310} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7a5e34-b197-57ec-bafb-007ac6b6c167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503389} {:answer/id #uuid "da810825-b5ef-5b80-8415-5e95017d7de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503419} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfa9a94-c719-5199-ba06-985601fc058f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "828244f0-37d1-5218-b4cd-65ea8c882592" :question/math-academy-id "q-95329" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $g(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={sinh}^{2}⁡x$ and $v(x)=2x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sinh}^{2}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2x+1)^{′}}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2)}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ad65d6-22ea-5a70-a6fc-c7a394b78979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503111} {:answer/id #uuid "84bc382e-bb1a-53e0-9616-39f48f878732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503253} {:answer/id #uuid "85e05f05-a7d1-5ce5-afb1-7cfe2bb20cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503254} {:answer/id #uuid "957979fc-282e-5501-883c-530bce9d9ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503275} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8a228b-4bc9-587e-9614-77df0af9703d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503425} :answer-field/id #uuid "53d0c528-6bbe-5382-9d7c-bfcd745b246c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8706edb2-e78b-5b3f-a44f-7250c01b8006" :question/math-academy-id "q-138072" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be416ad7-fa5c-5f2a-8f8e-5c23bca4c530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{\\frac{2k}{3}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503362} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28fb333-812f-5331-bf83-e683ff5050d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "957cf846-05af-527e-b9b5-8a808e2c175e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342677" :question/problem "Simplify $tanh\\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $e$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$tanh\\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${e}^{2k/3}+1$ |" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(k/3)}-1}{{e}^{2(k/3)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2k/3}-1}{{e}^{2k/3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71d7d57f-eadd-59fa-9fed-90fc5e33c3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503212} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e1d1c8-31e4-5631-aa5f-e93437d60a0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "be80c98f-2a34-525c-9b0c-3079917fdbf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342759" :question/problem "Evaluate $sinh\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}\\,-\n$$ {{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sinh\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}-{e}^{-3/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "182d4fea-cab3-5c77-92d8-85665c78e002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504074} {:answer/id #uuid "441e43be-1210-543b-a060-7ab6375d721e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504153} {:answer/id #uuid "5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504193} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9bc1c5-97ac-53e3-8d0c-ec9cfcd0faa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504218} {:answer/id #uuid "7610f7a5-3984-5ff3-a2fc-d3a6f3076d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x-\\pi )$ and $b=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504233} {:answer/id #uuid "d377c5cf-aeae-5ea0-8d58-7d902cda0add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef9605e-2699-5174-9d74-bcee640c79f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504173} :answer-field/id #uuid "e35ad816-07aa-5999-9c62-cec4f07d6b3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "060bce41-c046-5d08-898b-cfb8261e7661" :question/math-academy-id "q-342552" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k), & x\\ne -1, \\\\ 3, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(k-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(k-2) & =3 \\\\ k-2 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ k-2 & =125 \\\\ k & =127.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=127$ removes the point discontinuity."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07485527-04b3-57d3-a577-5a960471513a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\log_2(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504042}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504042} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce33564-9610-5802-9f60-271794918cb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504489} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a12617f-583b-5e92-913a-797edfec8b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504079} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2f8892-a963-5ac4-967b-d51cd720e5b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "368aa1bb-68dd-5e11-83de-3c6725fca7d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-346198" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca3157f-ba62-5d76-ad5b-12e2928c4115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504057} {:answer/id #uuid "672913a8-5d68-5d5d-8a83-91ab5694722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504213} {:answer/id #uuid "83342381-c8c1-5af1-a5c4-33a732276bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504259} {:answer/id #uuid "896c4b66-2e45-5e07-b655-87368942ef1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504270} {:answer/id #uuid "98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504302} :answer-field/id #uuid "264050bc-0a3b-5838-a4de-f7f48cdcac28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61f7a11b-44e6-5a13-bd4e-c02585b3aa3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338347" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d4fea-cab3-5c77-92d8-85665c78e002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504074} {:answer/id #uuid "420d139d-afbe-5844-a8dd-8419647c77a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504145} {:answer/id #uuid "65ad5bd6-c5c9-54e5-bc74-3bf9d08af3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504207} {:answer/id #uuid "86880236-33cf-5fe7-ad90-0ffd13b9f4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504266} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c9c22c-95a4-5257-aadf-de4e6f6689bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504207} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f97b4d-3e4e-5d71-8051-0c4a12624cb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73c29bd9-9c93-5235-9a81-b902322f8bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-138837" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by  \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bfb878-d116-5da1-9fee-d2b65e89a110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504104} {:answer/id #uuid "370e9d6b-2f33-5aaf-afde-460829004686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504127} {:answer/id #uuid "7d20f992-30d4-5d65-b87e-1bacd33bdcc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504253} {:answer/id #uuid "a33f01a3-064b-547d-a6b4-4222a8515448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504323} {:answer/id #uuid "d75814c9-ace7-5611-86fa-6aa2f33bbe8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504323} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e20a55-64e5-5910-8426-c1682701f362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "819d9389-7897-53fe-92be-f89ba9882964" :question/math-academy-id "q-48544" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k), & x\\ne 1, \\\\ 2, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡3(1-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡3(1-k) & =2 \\\\ 3(1-k) & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 1-k & =3 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=-2$ removes the point discontinuity."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\right){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8da8f1d5-799d-5f59-b8d4-b6195a7bf724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504281} {:answer/id #uuid "98c059aa-5dc8-5f1e-bc28-ea423a043d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504303} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1b4241-9760-5288-9766-35e472eeb9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504314} {:answer/id #uuid "ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504384} {:answer/id #uuid "f79b5c6d-0b46-5c5d-8893-792aa3b17df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504384} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f2f07fa-cb25-5801-b6e0-0205e0afe77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ffda6d3a-e6e4-5195-9f9f-69837bd2b6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338353" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d3b25e-e4d9-5fd1-8ed1-6f85c37a6a81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341314" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}\\right)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b61954d0-ee42-5fdc-ac63-ec94caa6896b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505458} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea4e9517-341b-550d-a4d1-9501b5d7afb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21de70c5-f015-584f-8044-af40301c4b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-341520" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{x})$ for $x\\ge 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=($\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain. We have $y\\in [0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the right-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & ={ln}^{2}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require real $y$ and $x<\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ for all $x,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}))}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4354c19a-ced8-5d91-8370-a595dbdb9d13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341486" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}{1-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,\\left(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc5eff9-4511-5ebc-9346-fef6d3ff2cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505293} {:answer/id #uuid "61a52828-b97a-55bc-89af-abdc326b7088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505301} {:answer/id #uuid "a747a90c-e5d0-5aef-9c1b-23569a104c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505440} {:answer/id #uuid "bf18c3df-1eff-5189-830c-ed12cf41dde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505476} {:answer/id #uuid "cae550b1-5979-52c8-970e-b1b797158855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505440} :answer-field/id #uuid "7876217b-a5c3-5d52-8219-aba955692660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b6577b4-d24d-5962-9f5c-436ba639df8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73371" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\n$$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5976a6b-1d1d-57ce-8564-63c89f695029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505555}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505555} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5cb8799-b859-5b82-be20-c6134d8d1332" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e2444a41-f0c0-50e5-91b8-38bd6aeb8a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-347942" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\n$$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{18}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b15c20-ae96-5510-bdf6-238327aebb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} {:answer/id #uuid "9420b75f-9ddc-56f2-8501-2ed81e404fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506163} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa83dc4-ef7c-5fb9-9ca5-7ad2f609a832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506198} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa37242-7820-582d-b32a-092b97e43ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506282} {:answer/id #uuid "f9347296-ee2c-5eec-91b9-fff04cdeed07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b45ba0-9d41-52b1-8d31-cba0ed77bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "06abf62c-6dfa-52f6-818c-3858ac967b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-138850" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39eac370-b597-5b13-a139-ed248af8a490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506009} {:answer/id #uuid "3e37cd6e-52b0-57de-acb7-d9a7a4ef0fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506014} {:answer/id #uuid "6c234f23-48fe-556c-a0b1-24c477bfbdfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506100} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bacdde-7997-50ac-944f-7a50e4a49e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} {:answer/id #uuid "deac3608-20ae-52c0-97b0-276c7fcb4d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dff71e-7253-5db5-a8db-526f8787e942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11c48698-c6fd-5725-a4b0-1737e6fdc309" :question/math-academy-id "q-138831" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4721e6-a280-5e36-9c3e-3d0197e080fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\tan(x)\\ln(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505940} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bed9cfc-3a69-5d51-ba57-741bfaabbf0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "61a2565a-e401-5260-aae7-f12b167eb293" :question/math-academy-id "q-312658" :question/problem "Given that $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={x}^{x}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+{sec}^{2}⁡(x))$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}+{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24dcf1bc-40b1-5496-a498-3a7dae4ffe85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "271" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505972} {:answer/id #uuid "94c691dd-0578-5e12-9cc1-90a7a7bb4085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506165} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f872ba-2453-55d3-97e1-d1431eaa6668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506204} {:answer/id #uuid "e2265b1b-5fa7-5e22-a840-d2ec09a5ad1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506326} {:answer/id #uuid "eef1c07b-39fe-56ff-ad05-22dd4b396724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bb00dc-f20f-5ab2-8e37-6185dcb84a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "adde1803-10ec-5c3d-8c04-d7e2c5d99587" :question/math-academy-id "q-138830" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(ax+63-8({x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd58159-33f5-5db7-909d-d7a72f3ebd4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7fe715dd-222c-5303-8e5d-661af680f9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-314062" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=\n$${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL=\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283bc5fc-1222-57a7-8638-1198c9f5bbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506769} {:answer/id #uuid "2a76ca6d-751a-52d0-b413-74f07f4e9730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506776} {:answer/id #uuid "73a19e0a-5cd5-590e-bcf6-edf436ff926d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506944} {:answer/id #uuid "a78393aa-8611-5a23-aef6-c92572946ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507041} {:answer/id #uuid "c8abe998-8843-5f0b-99d1-f0f2263b0eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507114}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507041} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6663233-d334-5d8d-952b-0dbf2517514c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eea34af5-c2e9-54d0-b79f-c71cb047b89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48621" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(cx-4), & \\frac{2}{3}<x\\le 1 \\\\ 2x-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at $x=1,$ find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c(1)-4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(c-4) & =1 \\\\ c-4 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ c & =2+4 \\\\ c & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6cc1010-4fa1-546b-948e-840f04280c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^{8-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507932} :answer-field/id #uuid "d219bdc7-f555-5794-9513-0ba34124b55e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02669dc0-8a6e-5ef4-aa36-77e644af949e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343799" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=f(x),\\,y(4)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(4)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+2y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+2y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(4)=3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4) & =A{e}^{-2(4)}+{y}_{p}(4) \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =3{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(3{e}^{8}){e}^{-2x}=3{e}^{8-2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼3{e}^{8-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508605} {:answer/id #uuid "b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508660} {:answer/id #uuid "f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508605} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7031cc-3266-50a5-b23e-e80324be569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508652} {:answer/id #uuid "b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508660} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b8581d-d97f-5540-9faf-641daf95fba0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-344320" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12433c4a-668f-57da-9582-b6ee6515dc42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508415} {:answer/id #uuid "5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508526} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a54554-40d8-5b1a-a681-1f39ccace6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508691} {:answer/id #uuid "d353fe5f-d6bc-5d38-8a44-d344cb894fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508730} {:answer/id #uuid "f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508526} :answer-field/id #uuid "53289756-546a-50f2-80e0-c0a1e0a48184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" :question/math-academy-id "q-95111" :question/problem "Given that $q(x,y)=xy,$ find $\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "65eac0db-4498-5219-a599-16571ff5b0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509900} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a8b398-e764-5a3f-9020-8353ddf15a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{sin}^{2}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510072} {:answer/id #uuid "f40d8dc6-4b68-5931-af1a-b12969faf5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65eac0db-4498-5219-a599-16571ff5b0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509900} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ee05e3-c0ef-572d-b71a-0521af7c7c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510151} {:answer/id #uuid "e13473ff-e770-5bc2-84cc-d2f5a63a463b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510166} {:answer/id #uuid "ec060073-eee0-5eb4-a23b-64f1baba3d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510197} {:answer/id #uuid "f40d8dc6-4b68-5931-af1a-b12969faf5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509900} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d304d66-3b35-5b7a-a10d-e4c98f3bf8ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db419926-6089-5205-b99f-0938320c28f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161799" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 3v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y(u,v,w)=3v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a997286f-4db9-53ee-8849-22966e083d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{280}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510578} :answer-field/id #uuid "aac06ef3-2984-5073-a683-edc5d64f81a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "107b0d08-c0c2-5e3b-a247-c4238dbd6d8f" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{7}\\right)-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{56}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{280}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e54747-95d9-5771-963b-49dcf4295948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510574} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b44817c-904a-519b-9cfd-76497778888f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "295bc951-4997-5df9-ab67-20bc32e8016a" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}-\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "782fb4f6-27a0-5506-9beb-555ca18e4273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1784}{1323}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510577} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2dbf444-3856-5b85-8fac-b9843fa4dd21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f9be678-4285-5f11-ba52-fd42fc00b682" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{7}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{27}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1784}{1323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0e44b6c-314a-5501-9b95-06b4d75b5730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{513}{140}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510581} :answer-field/id #uuid "23459979-cbd9-5238-a961-af2acea3f96e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "554f7f1f-b6ff-5426-9de0-5f0d3de16c53" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{28}-\\left(-\\frac{21}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{513}{140}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c3e732-c3bb-5b5f-b786-d49ab1ea186a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{113}{210}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510582} :answer-field/id #uuid "25acb654-19ec-53d5-9cf7-5fa54e4d05cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f71665cc-a4c6-5094-b683-7caeca8eef3e" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{8}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{8}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{35}-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{113}{210}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d3ba017-304d-5563-b51d-7e1f4f2d1a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510596} :answer-field/id #uuid "a00250fb-51e3-5ea7-bb3a-d6728b1caebb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ae33cd48-4286-533b-a46d-20551d42a300" :question/problem "Calculate $det(R)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(R)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-8)(13)-(14)(-7) \\\\  & =-104+98 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7510b5f-6370-56f3-aaeb-ffce4ec766ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510621} :answer-field/id #uuid "d775c716-9480-511e-b2f7-a5ea4fe55174" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b02768f5-2401-5388-9659-b87f57ee6732" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7)(-15)-(-12)(7) \\\\  & =-105+84 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de85e9f7-1fad-524b-bd8b-7104cec09347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510619} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d20830b-2284-575f-9545-dae95ed220c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b1a64264-a586-541e-93d2-598674ef4459" :question/problem "Calculate $det(S)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(S)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-9)(9)-(1)(9) \\\\  & =-81-9 \\\\  & =-90.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249f6d39-c352-5b6e-b0a3-aad8dbe4af0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510728} {:answer/id #uuid "40e4413d-62c5-5cf3-8a6d-bc0951e1d6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{83}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510731} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae139e3-a55e-5972-9da7-3d735e750073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510738} {:answer/id #uuid "cf16047a-f770-52e9-8ba0-da97bd2d6088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa164a1-1ef9-5a0d-9231-f146dcc770a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} :answer-field/id #uuid "af481ffe-765a-562a-87bb-3829101afbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2014768a-a233-5eee-b9c5-afc2675420b0" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdb5bb029197875aaeebfc899dff4d25597b657e64ea95d1b34d09df0a37789.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1909811c-da8d-539b-aa89-cdc5b420a83f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510725} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc3517e-1300-5538-b247-d948b579f7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510729} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9189a3-d0b3-5483-9c84-198750079c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510748} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b2d445-8bcf-5fd5-a74d-dc7c7c42cc23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e48c42-ff53-5bcc-b008-e6ccbde37ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c54992-c621-521f-8066-259a4e4e6c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 1