swer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cd8750-e5e7-52ce-b115-cbd111d3d902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510726} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba5953e-1bc3-52b3-949b-b1a3372ec514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510734} {:answer/id #uuid "dca87888-b247-5218-bd97-b52279791479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510746} {:answer/id #uuid "ed349bbe-0002-555d-b774-e4baa909f28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{79}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510749} {:answer/id #uuid "f51c1080-9d67-5d5c-9ad2-f5bee4c4e810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510734} :answer-field/id #uuid "89a72e76-dfbf-51a2-96d4-d8c440244cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55b50355-dd9b-57b2-ba6b-ec0a86439c05" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2c66fc4d5fa2ac5b2d6263764cf1eaa4e845680561833c5bfa46743df3408d2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af1047be250d877d836851da681e583ada8f01d2022e5c9671aa83173ed23fa3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{39}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39\\cdot 10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc4984a-76a7-5e40-bec6-4baeb697eb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510732} {:answer/id #uuid "93b2d161-28c4-50ff-962d-90ee2fbdac17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} {:answer/id #uuid "a332604f-d236-5a81-8925-b96c1993b9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510739} {:answer/id #uuid "b382a9d0-fd0f-530d-8c12-c535c8651899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510741} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc011f-2ad6-5dc2-af67-11ebde52edbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} :answer-field/id #uuid "68af0948-d377-54ba-bc95-3bf16b5f82fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cab2c352-408a-5716-9c82-d09e82069fc3" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd9061a8ef1b2f0d8fe9d730f18c5e10bef42a9acd33f23d36efb3728ce0614b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{15}{17},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ba/bad705982f073623644ed540e26cfad849b2dd078c5c64772a0be3937d618a27.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{15}{17}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{17}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right)}=\\frac{15}{8}.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1888a529-95b0-58f6-9146-6b50fc190c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510838} {:answer/id #uuid "354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510900} {:answer/id #uuid "3756a535-be12-51cb-a4f4-eebb7d355566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510908} {:answer/id #uuid "442f019f-9950-54ef-b315-5fed1c784a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510936} {:answer/id #uuid "494e353c-817b-5ca7-a101-f7151bd4954d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510944} {:answer/id #uuid "557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510964} {:answer/id #uuid "5b788302-0dfd-54bf-a49b-6d49283dd409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510974} {:answer/id #uuid "959f6e54-dd4c-5921-b12b-794921892ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f93c0d67-7bd9-5f1c-85c2-95df089580db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511292} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71666a0-c57b-5c11-820a-d694a30bc7a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "065ae9b3-7009-5705-869a-1bfc3920deab" :question/math-academy-id "q-343672" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5f0d970e95b7e2e2656ef579f9d5be49eb9eddb433a5921149347ef349139c5.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $6$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $g(-3)=2.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =2 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ g(5) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(4-6) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,0\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510786} {:answer/id #uuid "2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510855} {:answer/id #uuid "4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddles points at $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510937} {:answer/id #uuid "8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511043} {:answer/id #uuid "cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510855} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-105280" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) &=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb8c2326-431f-5feb-b9b6-755c7c458e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511300} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d3b2238-2f4b-5f46-852b-25cc3958669c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b07da73-507b-5a7d-831e-9986becac517" :question/math-academy-id "q-342847" :question/problem "![](images/39/393c612a7ecb682e28bf9de46024a3681e8f6e58a9add78c0b24a870aa00fa7a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,4]$ given that $f(0)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-1 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+(-8) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,-9\\}=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-9,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a5535b1-fd90-5e4a-8596-20391200d35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511007} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb28b96-8669-5b5e-98ee-1ee25daf74e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20a7e745-4621-5ebb-935e-e596b9f08fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-342903" :question/problem "![](images/31/3123021ea5734c0ff2e8edcb716245b603de6f9dde7ebf0d367e1eeb8383bdf8.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $10$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,0]$ given that $f(-6)=2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-3,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =2 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3-10 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,5,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06acb265-9aa4-5e5c-afdc-84b8445440f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510792} {:answer/id #uuid "17e65082-0896-55d8-99e6-6536ee35ed4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510836} {:answer/id #uuid "81decfb0-353d-554c-a766-c2d475210694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511048} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7d0305-07ee-5c49-a171-c47f7420e291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511118} {:answer/id #uuid "c08ac167-4934-5d02-a974-ed7fd8f64367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511048} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c15a59-5adc-5127-8074-bf8ca6a5dc2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "223797a4-3fdc-5efb-a9f2-b733398cd415" :question/math-academy-id "q-80946" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=(-1)\\cdot 1-{0}^{2}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80f28cbc-9bc5-54e6-97e1-466a4f97e761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511046} :answer-field/id #uuid "4003c727-78b9-502a-bc48-15371f44dc21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "27335de7-ab94-5f77-a70c-95b3aeec92aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343704" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3f57d0658f89f37df9a838b4a79cb9d4ed3d4183187013923c26fb3bd836e10.png)\n\nThe function $k(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,7].$ The graph of ${k}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $k(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $7,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $k(x)$ on the interval $[-2,7]$ given that $k(-2)=2.$\n\n${k}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $k(x)$ on $[-2,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${k}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,7),$ and ${k}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1,5.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $k$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-2) & =2 \\\\ k(1) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ k(5) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ k(7) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{7}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7-2) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $k$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $k$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-2,5,3\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $k$ is ${k}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $k(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ad1452-16bf-5aaa-b266-a0c4eefba9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511096} :answer-field/id #uuid "8811494e-64ac-563f-b9e3-157298f88e64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b3942d1-426d-544a-a802-5cef91893443" :question/math-academy-id "q-343680" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4b4735b3154603284ed6f232ae640c5f47a536a5c26b75bb99970c8d0cb2c08.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $3$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $h(-4)=-6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-4) & =-6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ h(2) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+(5-3) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-1,-4\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1888a529-95b0-58f6-9146-6b50fc190c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510838} {:answer/id #uuid "3756a535-be12-51cb-a4f4-eebb7d355566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510908} {:answer/id #uuid "557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510964} {:answer/id #uuid "5b788302-0dfd-54bf-a49b-6d49283dd409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510974} {:answer/id #uuid "c886c152-17ed-511e-a809-d63c2213db33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510964} :answer-field/id #uuid "024d0b3c-4d66-50af-a789-68c23cbe417a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3eee5be8-fdde-5369-b6f0-771204d5bd54" :question/math-academy-id "q-161775" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/e6/e63035cbe368a4f41b7197d70880d0639f004e26996fe6b5055e69966e0f66ee.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "759a68b1-2538-543b-9c09-1d4d9da7d95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511025} :answer-field/id #uuid "262dabb7-a19e-557c-b02a-f2846842eb57" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "457b4315-a413-5f6e-81e5-9a7aa0226cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343884" :question/problem "![](images/01/01dad591c5f02e0528e9092a058b44806cb0bab6f709347f8f90120c58ad25bc.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ if $f(-3)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function  ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/eb/ebcaf43d56e8854ce17d89ce66a811ad1f505f7ebb855362798d845164a80377.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+7.5 \\\\  & =5.5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(7.5-1) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,5.5,4.5\\}=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0792ef62-08ea-5707-8327-c6e5163ce588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510795} {:answer/id #uuid "a8805474-e6d8-5e64-b86f-dd449112f5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511138} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b47704-e864-540f-bb6b-29319e7d7b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511161} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a946c6-0bd9-50e0-8185-2e9896bd42e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511228} {:answer/id #uuid "dc68c10e-ed5b-5324-8eca-dc60da0273a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510795} :answer-field/id #uuid "46eb44a3-a8e1-5a19-aeb3-6ef173f8b41b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4760d6e4-5155-5d78-b6d2-f55eb97036fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-144866" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbbbcdb54dc8d6f96957ca01f4eeccea0c24c79eb95fc6c10953534d38e4d724.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-8,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6,$ $2,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-8,0]$ given that $f(-8)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-8,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-8,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-8,-4,-2,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-8) & =2 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,8,6,10\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc09cc98-aa3a-5fb7-8896-022fdc71a474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511302} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a1b22c-006e-5530-8085-81d9408aea6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47b1ac6a-9da0-5717-9e95-4b0413ae2c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343665" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7aa23647a61b4d13e9468eafc34947e8b01046a31d67da8ee997476e13dec95.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $2\\pi .$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-3\\pi .$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-3\\pi  \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\pi -2\\pi  \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3\\pi ,-5\\pi \\}=-3\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-3\\pi ,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b09df16-6636-5d5a-a07e-b977e5c99a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510801} {:answer/id #uuid "2ead93a4-4662-5088-8f13-942128d4fcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510883} {:answer/id #uuid "4291ba92-5f2c-5dc3-a5db-2d7ca1b77cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510931} {:answer/id #uuid "5670001a-de2c-5e94-bfc2-8dd60d548162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510966} {:answer/id #uuid "809430b8-a1ca-5594-b25f-9c41dda2a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510801} :answer-field/id #uuid "878756a2-046d-51e8-8047-b9ae0e732f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "488dbbc3-e305-587b-8387-bc6f8dfc6d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-15742" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8500caba027f22623c3e20287f92f12e56aad667788708189f68205f4122195.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $8,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]$ given that $f(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3,7.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,7,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(7) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8+2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,5,-3,-1\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "238fef3c-4f22-5034-924b-0380723ee9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510857} {:answer/id #uuid "295da837-a754-53d4-ac11-249c126eceb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510867} {:answer/id #uuid "36f1c22b-e984-57a9-89c4-5c0b33dfae7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510905} {:answer/id #uuid "bd46d8fd-7d57-5880-a3f6-77bb4a90c0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511171} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e926f2-c0b7-5989-b9fe-45190a83cbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510905} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5acdfa9-775d-577e-ae8c-9aadaf73c349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55621ada-4f38-5ee1-82ac-26e8704cbd38" :question/math-academy-id "q-141619" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa70775eb1b2968406c61e1bebad52139f492890e71a9977582fc1898ba5ee8f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\n We see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-9 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-7\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ecd168-33d4-549d-bb83-5f68dbca2132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511214} :answer-field/id #uuid "508b7401-7584-5847-a9c7-fe4a7c28f982" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6cbda409-a223-57c5-93ac-3f78fe61c842" :question/math-academy-id "q-341244" :question/problem "![](images/73/73610eebf575c612fc5b64ec6df264baffb338bdf3f2dbdc848477262874a54d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $7$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2]$ given that $f(-3)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4-7 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{5,9,2\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4d2bf2-2853-5cad-97a9-4d01b5dc71de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511204} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa6557a-d133-596a-b431-eb4eace2e7f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f946771-a506-530c-b84d-03471b2aa334" :question/math-academy-id "q-343853" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1d5208f46af834deda70086b9ef4b91defe7359f625d9fb264b370965196a0b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-4,3],$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ trapezoids and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ae/aea529468efff36403aca0bf9ee9cc6bc4c5680476ccb81314388bb315f700df.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+2.5+0.5 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+2.5+0.5+(-1.5)+(-4) \\\\  & =-3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,2,-3.5\\}=-3.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d05ee4-a3f9-5845-aabf-04165b4653e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510787} {:answer/id #uuid "11fc7f6f-2388-5e11-b63d-7af13c584f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510816} {:answer/id #uuid "3766ec0d-0113-5d0c-ad91-d63c83936a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510909} {:answer/id #uuid "619f3b03-eef6-5b71-ac90-870978677b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510989} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f3b9f0-1089-5653-bb60-63efb1012505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510989} :answer-field/id #uuid "eafdb283-9289-5258-96cf-a740ad61cf63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "907c4d01-952e-54f5-819a-03c35c5b1210" :question/math-academy-id "q-15744" :question/problem "![](images/39/392562d7675b8ebddd33cbdea448481654f9e643b088b949d4337618517c537b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,1,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/82/82f6ace89a1d880390b5f6c2dbe344a6230d5bc91ba11521ac996b5e59ebc49c.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(1) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,\\frac{11}{2},4,5\\}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{11}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864ead19-4057-5f6a-8c06-48fb2c7562de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511059} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd05ee6-ac91-5d21-8ecf-c0b6a272af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511059} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de15137-bf56-596a-a38a-ac01796200ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e66d128-cec6-555a-8143-73ec6e9676c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510878} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23dd8e-fbc6-5790-9b1d-fe0b23c91599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511075} {:answer/id #uuid "df2fadf9-ffc9-50bc-80ad-3b43d5a65713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511244} {:answer/id #uuid "e374467d-7122-514c-8ff6-30b09a2ff344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511244} :answer-field/id #uuid "253cae4c-287a-57dd-940c-b84a887327b5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be46685-8a2f-512e-8ecc-5f60c5fb734b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510975} {:answer/id #uuid "a6eff1ea-c328-5cf5-ba20-f5e842a11bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511135} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d2133a-3545-57a6-b6ce-a65c4be200ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511135} :answer-field/id #uuid "8864a19e-a3dc-5d3a-b104-94d3cbc88d3b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aabc6bb-ca4a-5cb9-9ccd-4e3aecda5a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510916} {:answer/id #uuid "4a723052-6e2a-5c5d-a43a-4a384522e9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510946} {:answer/id #uuid "baf10e71-ca8e-5c28-b131-42d39b4e9fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511165} {:answer/id #uuid "dde8b71b-c391-5449-aef8-6a80127dccaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a017d0-45f8-582e-844c-12a17fcdf030" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ad7e028-7ba2-59c0-89a8-df188134628e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343448" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1577bbd3-e41b-5487-8909-19851b51a970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510829} {:answer/id #uuid "c09c46d9-008d-5f1a-994d-aae5ef1caf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511180} {:answer/id #uuid "c681754b-a4e1-554d-bb13-0f1a0241560f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511191} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0c97bf-a701-5927-ad4e-e2760701cfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511203} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2619ca-cf89-514f-821b-3f08e732ac00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511180} :answer-field/id #uuid "c936d966-d407-55f3-833f-5a0d48ccc990" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b464b81-5baf-5338-aa06-b3e5f256249d" :question/math-academy-id "q-144837" :question/problem "![](images/66/6684670c5999281044dd1e08faabbf06e7aeb3fef7902835ea9ea6c07d148b5a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,4]$ given that $f(-2)=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+8 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-3,5\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149ea365-6c9e-5896-b572-37eac2a934cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510825} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d8d771-54ee-598d-ba3a-511af4adfe5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a52ab505-f7f2-54ed-afb3-b43bed5cfef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343959" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cb97b20d164c2976ae046f83edbbb499514ff3c7ddfbfa9b703918457eba467.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,6]$ given that $f(-4)=7.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$  Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of  $f(x)$  for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/e6/e67e51128fe4392e814eec6d9d6ab17d7d7718af38fd556a0a47c603174e9dcd.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =7, \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+2 \\\\  & =9, \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24, \\\\ f(6) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =24-4.5 \\\\  & =19.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{7,9,24,19.5\\}=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=24,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051f2eaa-30f6-5a12-9a77-9f95ac0b3e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510788} {:answer/id #uuid "3257bf5c-fa92-5d61-948f-9e8f3c674e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510889} {:answer/id #uuid "5896cb3f-bb8d-57b4-adca-c7ec3f6be4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510970} {:answer/id #uuid "bda1540c-a5f2-5558-ad30-970a0713c6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511174} {:answer/id #uuid "dba7e57b-8972-5648-baf2-34e857e37757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510788} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e9e8ce-81b5-5865-823b-16944100d9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611" :question/math-academy-id "q-123643" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1619dc0b-d53c-58c4-92e8-5b68a431da0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510831} {:answer/id #uuid "35312181-3164-564e-951d-368b81aed2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510899} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7493ae-3582-5f1c-82f8-0c2dae976bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510948} {:answer/id #uuid "90ead743-a1fc-5f4f-9075-c4fd3b6866f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511080} {:answer/id #uuid "c72bf197-8bb8-5cea-9e15-63920f550d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510831} :answer-field/id #uuid "60daa8fe-705e-5990-9f0d-a1092f6eb497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b6ef3a51-3ce7-571a-8d2a-d26c504b1810" :question/math-academy-id "q-161923" :question/problem "![](images/98/989c9452bebc4ee6c2253c920655830a0b3d0adf0ddb7b90c1ea6a9528ef1448.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ for $y\\le 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/df8a9da3b88f3b40d85005b156215f7fa7760257f284acf98ec186d5a2e3ab8d.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function  $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e051c3b2-7e07-5470-85d6-e4ed12752211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511247} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef945b41-3caa-5828-922f-822c233714fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c4376358-d419-5ca5-9d5f-60ee48a180a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343902" :question/problem "![](images/86/8678ae0607275abf4732943add33116314569efc3afb44e92a15d6e78eeacd64.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of one square, two trapezoids, and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe0ee64fd29812d2ee536d7285f5e4674d8985a2dc1e27bf230dafb0159046e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-5 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5+(1+5+4) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-5,5,2\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b64010-1aad-525c-a643-83c025278cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510813} {:answer/id #uuid "531fd7eb-5005-586b-9cde-b6aaf6bb72fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510962} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfa8155-3c5a-57cf-aa28-23ff73257326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511037} {:answer/id #uuid "8505f759-4149-5bf6-ab53-8f74686ad96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511056} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea34fee-19fb-5ba9-8775-891452be3904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511037} :answer-field/id #uuid "d65b68cd-44fe-525c-bfeb-caea3373b3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce30ca10-ec44-5f96-b53c-efeee52b65e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-144853" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffa54937d4977e1b2e815862ad20fa7c05338fa4015f6ba42c5617c8957ea45d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,4]$ given that $f(-3)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-6 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc30760-ee1f-55b8-aae6-31bcfde7b6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510951} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa69f0f-6fad-5e66-afb8-956cd67e5b53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d62efc40-a923-5983-a507-878080289da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342845" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9a2a079f471072c80becb1188a2862f6f5d0d699eb40989b01c27a1f079fc42.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-10,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-10,6]$ given that $f(-10)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-10,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-10,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-10,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-10) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(-10)+{\\int }_{-10}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,11\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-10.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51efe52f-7214-5bbb-9fc5-08d1743885d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510959} {:answer/id #uuid "54a9f437-47aa-5650-a6f4-a78a12c4e80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510963} {:answer/id #uuid "7f930dd7-377b-5995-ba4d-b11719784356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511042} {:answer/id #uuid "ad934404-4176-5896-bb92-6ac4ba4eb09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511143} {:answer/id #uuid "fd276e11-0fed-5dbd-b2a9-4d15eb6ab208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510963} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbfdf821-69fb-57a9-a5a9-97d7a8ead96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d754760c-74db-51d7-aeca-199ef191761d" :question/math-academy-id "q-19084" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2aea7b223ec18094d626e8d8937a5844e786cb1b936fb3b6b478f672e4d0fb9d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.5.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of one triangle and one trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d8/d811c4920b4ddb05170afc1f0c38fe216c23139556b6f7e10829b663e894a7fb.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1.5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1.5+(-0.5) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1.5+(-0.5)+3 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1.5,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510900} {:answer/id #uuid "442f019f-9950-54ef-b315-5fed1c784a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510936} {:answer/id #uuid "494e353c-817b-5ca7-a101-f7151bd4954d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510944} {:answer/id #uuid "959f6e54-dd4c-5921-b12b-794921892ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511092} {:answer/id #uuid "db09b7e2-e0ce-5f11-ad20-482fba461cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510900} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b1c6d3-79e8-5764-9d1a-bcce12f2ad8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d7a0c5c0-c3dc-5ed9-a9e6-253b15c1791f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161749" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03319aea-2d62-5506-acf1-b821d450c5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510783} {:answer/id #uuid "16e8f84b-a22c-5b49-a071-bac5c3c9a64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510832} {:answer/id #uuid "2395c4b7-bcdc-5eb7-b5ef-f78390bd16a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510858} {:answer/id #uuid "68c5f4d8-ebe7-53a0-b429-1c1c5cdacd4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511004} {:answer/id #uuid "ddac1aa6-f011-564e-bebf-e731b91c1c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511239} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6dc031b-5260-5385-b1ed-663b87376e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d9b31f47-0173-5340-9051-1c0bbc0f3595" :question/math-academy-id "q-15776" :question/problem "![](images/52/527c042c57a965b22f4e9841e59087291382704d6ea29f5f444926257444d5f6.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,4]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/f5/f512273895da627c314f00a55bd507d6ae43cd1c6af95d98bd4b475903303e7d.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1)+3 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1)+3+(-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,1,4,3\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b167983-81a9-54ba-b03a-a6f83001fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510872} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d3a2c9-a5db-5dc5-82dc-e70759c371f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de089661-beea-5ee0-bfa5-18ccd2aea3c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-316990" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4077eba1972d32e7ab9b7bfbe550654395412076f175f48b1d78a49a21641ae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ if $f(0)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/aa/aade60ca0b004cad7f0bb1e2de4e5ea354e1f87625b3d915e745381c489c41a4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5 \\\\  & =7.5 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5-4 \\\\  & =3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{5,7.5,3.5\\}=7.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=7.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06649de1-9bda-5107-be59-afa45a2653cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510790} {:answer/id #uuid "066d1a43-ed49-5af5-b3b0-3b9a16e1713f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510791} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd42e4-12ae-595c-9c10-d746ae13d982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510952} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1432d6-a3c5-5ca4-b770-1eb65c25bbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511212} {:answer/id #uuid "da227ff9-882b-5bb9-b70a-72f338b132dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511230}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511230} :answer-field/id #uuid "99541a9b-c65b-5a54-8eab-cc4568e265c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e22ec0ee-78ef-534d-a2b2-dbeef1b12436" :question/math-academy-id "q-144833" :question/problem "![](images/56/567044329a721ac622d2f66e675577e202d65d3de145a93fa430edfb31ced4b1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,4]$ given that $f(-2)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,4.$  \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d007e8f-8054-5ee3-b94b-d992a6985939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510949} {:answer/id #uuid "656b3af1-56aa-563c-a9f2-d823f796cf39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510997} {:answer/id #uuid "6f636ec8-1a9f-5adf-abaf-6e27b19d60cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511014} {:answer/id #uuid "8884fd70-0ce7-57f2-8714-e84fe064ead3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511064} {:answer/id #uuid "fd903076-dead-5638-8545-5cbc8bcb7552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511305} :answer-field/id #uuid "71d68880-e750-51f3-aaab-c459cfd8a58a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e74c85cc-5f64-54fa-a3ca-fa87e47bcbd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-15774" :question/problem "![](images/80/807e4e931a2872dd6d2a570547b75f68935b3ea761cfaa8cda279a0e194130f9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $5$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=-10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3-5 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-10,-7,-12\\}=-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-12,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018bc768-711b-5d52-a148-77cb9027022b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510775} {:answer/id #uuid "300e0a0a-af59-5614-8783-8c1c2dc2d69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510885} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd4b27b-c642-530a-b02b-8264bfd8d742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511008} {:answer/id #uuid "83a1f267-00d7-5bf5-b8f9-aa9cda7687e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511052} {:answer/id #uuid "f212ed43-bce1-5e26-afde-d86e1d480f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511008} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a75d733-5d77-565b-9745-e6fa1b878aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e788b4b9-a09c-5261-9e6a-52c7021cb74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144856" :question/problem "![](images/78/78d04b9ba0d4ad4e84ec45e88a199af6dbbdb04b1fa9d7513f23d9eb64a99a47.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $4.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,0]$ given that $f(-4)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,-3\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba863d0-df64-50d8-8f3e-d48da3e80c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510846} {:answer/id #uuid "71962e20-5aa7-5aa9-bce4-7e12d2356e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511019} {:answer/id #uuid "b076a541-1d5b-5195-a894-5f6fb12c5287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511149} {:answer/id #uuid "da3d597e-169f-5c12-981e-e45eb43612aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511231} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ce9f06-450e-5881-8fac-5218ef358d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511019} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ff88f9-afef-5744-91de-630c6ef0b4b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f1f17c93-4d26-549b-8492-2ec95bf66e77" :question/math-academy-id "q-141882" :question/problem "![](images/82/82a08c804381b11ae118691d82c4801b655cd8e0b131677f67ed6e63b8400d7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[2,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[2,6]$ given that $f(2)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[2,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (2,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are$x=2,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343c6fe7-74c0-52a2-bc86-d208dbf32b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511689} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5a672c-e194-5807-a342-905cf49227f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511693} {:answer/id #uuid "75399b50-a49f-5812-89c2-ed1173a8611d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511696} {:answer/id #uuid "c39a656b-c2c8-5877-9d30-c0d08addf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511706} {:answer/id #uuid "ead3e7bc-0d8f-5814-a629-e38097f034cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511689} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a5d9b12-19cd-584b-b779-a468e4d6ca2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c5094d1-3c19-540e-a83e-e53a0c0e885b" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a6e00e08f331baf932661a341627dd6403ca5c33f17b5fe51f2343198c1b5f1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{7}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/608cde6d839f956b950438c50f54338fb7ec7b816ffc21ac38515dbf79a9f980.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{7}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a32365c-3ae5-53ac-a149-f47a33daf7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511683} {:answer/id #uuid "931680d4-ddc2-5fe3-a179-0b349fd50d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511700} {:answer/id #uuid "ab910f87-b9dc-573a-978e-cb0375562e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{76}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511703} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f2325b-f13b-5140-9d7e-7661e658a40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511709} {:answer/id #uuid "f4058ccc-66d9-56ac-a956-faf95687a1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511700} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b7f5f26-505c-5de5-95d8-c1e0ccb5a7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "31b52705-15c7-508e-bc5b-87541ef55e38" :question/problem "![](images/22/2242f7a5c4b3386e8f493799e71645145e9290fa5eaad66f986931d9e38006e2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a6/a6a3acf1948838a3d1d8f0effe0e1cdb30282b289f89009cbbcac716b4b73700.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{38}{25}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b266fd5-0108-5d1b-b55c-e7ab7da30986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511682} {:answer/id #uuid "3231f0e8-0e93-53ea-825c-432c55cfd011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511688} {:answer/id #uuid "38075e8b-f56a-5110-8581-0f19e65d1cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511690} {:answer/id #uuid "6c632c64-803b-5920-ba44-0bd15d2842da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511695} {:answer/id #uuid "a47751bb-fbec-549f-b413-287c65e22ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511690} :answer-field/id #uuid "85df7c4c-736c-5b18-b67c-bc225c6084a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9e339c15-6319-50b0-b160-b0fbf1d94d58" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0cdc84ef38e2c2074bdab95458a97452209858b74a370b65bd7f393c1bde908.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69ab1b2e23a2815343db2b914204aa5864e73254dd1aa22d501c08b8ae155714.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{17}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{34}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{64}{49}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037cbbc0-15eb-5ca5-9687-929e9c03c3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511681} {:answer/id #uuid "5524e754-213c-5297-b6c8-38ca120bb015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511692} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc4b2b4-ef85-5136-ae4f-475a2d6e7ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511698} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1ea995-d6ac-507a-b61c-f3087c637df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511699} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2e1cc1-cb15-5e19-b2ed-06cfc90ef286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511714} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa6be0a-2bd9-5771-ae42-ec9c7d256501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba9e8819-628f-5c44-89ce-c66c0188feda" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd7cf164aa9ac00697292aabf03d616b62b216014ac00a0f36e9194ad7685ce2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df6444faa947e137efc95beeb24c59e8e685830096bf4019fef776abd87c4bff.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{6}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02858282-63f7-5705-8f8e-00f513fcf0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511680} {:answer/id #uuid "b84e32c1-da66-5b76-9568-280c26d49655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511704} {:answer/id #uuid "d39f7a6b-0199-5e05-a5c4-d51a6cc4d9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511707} {:answer/id #uuid "f13958bf-0d8f-555a-bf51-2bf79723a49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511711} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd43435-b072-5f01-b302-1d59e6fd5742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511713} :answer-field/id #uuid "21996560-b703-53d3-bfc5-aac31475eee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd4c56b2-c037-5749-b1cc-f43f2eb74d1e" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a35a31ccce6ce9e50c386b920c8c4b0fecf23a4f29741e982dcd6c69bf38453c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/09/0961629419accd50af36ce88aecafe33ea264d278d18d6f71c4aa4d76c06be97.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{13}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{26}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{24}{25}.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "239c326a-0a3b-5c29-850c-cac8919a3673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡16=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512177} {:answer/id #uuid "4779f4c7-29b4-5acc-9108-58e66d5a7ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡2=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512249} {:answer/id #uuid "4794e66d-dfd4-5289-9730-730f5a69be16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512250} {:answer/id #uuid "5e21fa3f-1c6c-5a3a-b2e9-4f260f1443e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡2=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512321} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e5741e-9798-5186-9c1e-7f4c4d89acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡16=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f24ec07-37f7-5bf9-9196-8f7a42ebd048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512206} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2cc657-19ea-5669-92aa-e552ae923d2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ef4f411-6c67-5c13-837f-d11ff722cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342898" :question/problem "![](images/19/192a69596e851fa35b02bbfd6bda06f01814d0d1c05b5698a4179d00a4bb14d2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-8) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,-10\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a5ad1b2-f2c3-5cb5-baa1-6f44df6de444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512425} :answer-field/id #uuid "035cd52a-864b-5a48-b335-680aca817a12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "783f4f6c-a7ae-5188-a502-9fe2f7c3c94b" :question/math-academy-id "q-251862" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $16$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{16}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=188,\\,{S}_{yy}=354,\\,{S}_{xy}=212.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=212.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{212}{16} \\\\  & =13.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fbcee1d-43be-51a7-9bb4-2317e13ecb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512471} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9e9bfc-789a-50b5-b6bd-12951e72a53e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a823921c-a56b-531a-8f8b-ef8538df5571" :question/math-academy-id "q-341256" :question/problem "![](images/91/91441cecdbe477e4cf0115c1f7c4cf413fa36786690ff7e025ac2e62c711f66e.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ if $h(-2)=6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/19/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5495561b-b8e3-5cea-ba85-ba6d6ddebae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512289} {:answer/id #uuid "567d486e-aad4-556e-b6f9-b033c500c0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512295} {:answer/id #uuid "9a15af2c-3fdb-5eb8-9d83-be8b4bab64ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512462} {:answer/id #uuid "d97dcea9-edb6-5328-b0e6-580c59c6fd22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512618} {:answer/id #uuid "ee74ca24-de88-5417-bf75-85352a8d8dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512680} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6416470-1d7a-5a75-a4b7-d19f68263b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aeb0f117-1019-5572-b78f-39043f8c3ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-104255" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c23b2d07d656695feda6ae6e92d192661e30f2738b6af8342813b44f2885667.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $6,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,6,0\\}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=0,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a616be15-c64d-5f24-ad7f-1562b756919c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512492} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4924e73-d6d0-52bf-abba-4d9b0890fbd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af73b3f9-9505-5255-a05e-b12a6848bab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-251859" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $10$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{10}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=5,\\,{S}_{yy}=7,\\,{S}_{xy}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=6.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb46238-0858-5e29-93f5-3b1abf6e8453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512264} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc4c62d-f986-5b1e-8753-4ec4676a3b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512311} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6a7300-844d-5db4-8005-d85823b210da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9336c13e-c8cb-5577-898b-73fc979337d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512448} {:answer/id #uuid "9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc4114-78f9-5557-a713-9afe7cf481ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5322b59-ac05-57c5-bebd-3ff95532c124" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" :question/math-academy-id "q-343834" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}\\right)\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59866eb1-6270-5d69-87ee-cd8defdbb03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512309} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f7fac7-0bab-58a8-b469-4851316d7779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512472} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c7e36a-3c3b-5caf-a88f-dad913ccfea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512602} {:answer/id #uuid "e32cfebd-6726-5a8f-8ae6-17f8a1288601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512643} {:answer/id #uuid "e58b9ca2-7225-576e-b1c8-3e4ca5e53b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512649} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63c5698-714c-5871-9be3-b86baeca70c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de36a313-5e05-5888-a50c-ca20f5e00cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-19083" :question/problem "![](images/42/429cba100e1cebd4a2048226f3c9d252fdec171b33679ab8ff6ba21014386aa4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d2/d20759e0c246f1fad5e0a36e495302c6b8de0e23a5f8ed25bc291147750a1342.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1)+4.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,3,7.5\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d8ff16-032a-51c4-9910-7cfbc5ab703c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512450} :answer-field/id #uuid "8586adde-a0fb-5449-942e-6459137f3b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e2f10b5f-3a80-55aa-94de-01383831523e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342899" :question/problem "![](images/98/98290071482fa2a8082932304fbb0d0cc0db54c47324479da7979079e9542551.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $9$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]$ given that $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6-9 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,5,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36011859-1afd-5d56-97d3-4bd6c1c53226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512219} {:answer/id #uuid "713dc69b-eedb-57c7-b566-963d0364f484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512369} {:answer/id #uuid "7557f1ef-873e-57b4-b6d6-7690a0ee0f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512375} {:answer/id #uuid "a804836a-c5a1-5048-8cb1-94c9973a4fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512500} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ae3264-3b35-5e19-bcf1-18378d20d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512219} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f815379-499f-5491-abe7-bd5c1ed810d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" :question/math-academy-id "q-334642" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.24.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.02.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1092e238-1e6d-5e1d-95b0-0c6a67c145f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512135} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3887f5c-297a-596e-b84b-b76944fcf812" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f237f691-54ad-5d16-bd80-dc2031f08456" :question/math-academy-id "q-342944" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab8e0cbaf83156609db79ec08202c8f77acfebd03663dfe273e4d75f9db03afb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ if $f(0)=4.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2,4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ea/ea837252a62313eae506ef3d101c0b70121f6380694a49c2f54e1a19702949a5.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3)+0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,5,2,2.5\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "02628719-18d0-583b-8c86-306dce390d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{6}⁡2=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513531} {:answer/id #uuid "02a79f54-78e6-5cbb-a712-39cdeefeb6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡64=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513533} {:answer/id #uuid "06ab3879-2e3a-5271-93d4-d0935bd2ca32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{sech}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513546} {:answer/id #uuid "080d06a2-ac80-5731-a0d0-206b199bf240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{9}⁡81=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513553} {:answer/id #uuid "089b12fd-140f-59f5-a8a5-715dca5123f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1}⁡0=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513554} {:answer/id #uuid "0d05d41c-03ea-5483-92aa-af31d2669524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{e}^{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{tanh}^{2}⁡{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513563} {:answer/id #uuid "1036a5d2-4490-507d-98fa-001436fca0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1/3}⁡(1.587)≈4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513573} {:answer/id #uuid "10b3f0de-10ab-59f8-9482-ea6432ae1a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1/16}⁡16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513574} {:answer/id #uuid "11a34fc4-eb35-51d7-a29a-e83a642d8a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513577} {:answer/id #uuid "11bc61a1-c5df-575f-a750-df7c1b87d279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(1.587)≈\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513578} {:answer/id #uuid "1271b9bb-4fdb-5546-ba5d-c1a6d89b1fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1.414}⁡(2)≈\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513580} {:answer/id #uuid "130dd748-2866-5a43-a676-5219cb240dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1/16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513582} {:answer/id #uuid "15289765-de82-59df-97ba-0d29417be3c4" :answer/type {:db/