 "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515745} {:answer/id #uuid "423dd6da-2e83-5a29-8ca3-14205a0d3359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515755} {:answer/id #uuid "735d9684-5834-58d9-8ad9-37bb2fe12482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515772} {:answer/id #uuid "920765f7-8e65-58e0-be2c-82a5a1673a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515780} {:answer/id #uuid "c828bbfd-efc6-53f3-bdce-cb9177bfe7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515745} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfebc5db-f450-59fa-a0c9-e6fce96500f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a223131-a445-5eaa-af96-514c74007b30" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=6,$ then $tan⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "286fc4cf-0b96-5584-9c0b-fdbe8d9d8fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515742} {:answer/id #uuid "911bc086-94be-5561-a621-f006bd611101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515779} {:answer/id #uuid "94712f0d-7669-5b34-bc7f-8564e9df9d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5} - 7π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515782} {:answer/id #uuid "d3697941-8007-5777-a40c-4083efb0a248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515792} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3299db-288a-5f5f-8ecb-2e603d91d23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5} - 7π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515779} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a33238-69fc-5ac5-882d-e95da4c4963b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3d9809ad-44ef-540c-8012-839a65ff28c4" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = \\frac{3}{5}$, then $\\tan (-x - 7π) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x - 7π)=-\\frac{3}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dedcf8d-5c89-5da8-a2e6-587e3beff25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515760} {:answer/id #uuid "67333e84-a4d1-595c-a858-3e8d9c88f4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515766} {:answer/id #uuid "674411a3-a74c-5f3c-95ae-44ff5f2a9b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8949ca-157e-55ec-b57f-38460065fbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515774} {:answer/id #uuid "e58fba32-8728-5e5c-aee9-d05031d08535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515766} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c773ce7-b806-53fa-ac8d-5fa5adc13bd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "68c7b09d-a6f5-532c-a961-160da57515ab" :question/problem "If $tan⁡a=-3,$ then $tan⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-a) & =-tan⁡a \\\\  & =-(-3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce69c68-6e82-56e1-ae85-b28cb746819a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515738} {:answer/id #uuid "5eab6039-33ff-57b5-99c8-e4fc5a344530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515762} {:answer/id #uuid "a77976be-f2c5-5ad2-8b69-c45956fcce11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515785} {:answer/id #uuid "d70643b6-9934-5860-9cca-469cc903b473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515795} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ce702c-9e35-5b78-bd86-b3335e2e1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515762} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab1383b-dce0-5efe-aece-9a3a03a0a4fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d024b2e-6668-5de9-a316-68d5087b7f9d" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-6,$ then $\\frac{2}{3}tan⁡(-x+2\\pi )+1=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+2\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}tan⁡(-x+2\\pi )+1 & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b256a66-dffd-5744-a8cd-388957d080dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515769} {:answer/id #uuid "6c073a98-13c7-5c27-9d53-fc967682b24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515770} {:answer/id #uuid "cb639f57-2b8d-5292-89c5-d5105f866b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515789} {:answer/id #uuid "cf745f86-8585-5069-a02e-7ae9b862f9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515790} {:answer/id #uuid "d6931356-36dc-52f2-a09f-5e9d078eed60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515794} :answer-field/id #uuid "13afb8d0-e29c-5e03-8275-97617083d5a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "90ae461d-564d-5a00-970d-e4fcb1014552" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-8,$ then $\\frac{1}{8}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+3=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-8) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+3 & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8}+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e858aee-aaf3-5d29-b8bd-ade58fa7d13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515739} {:answer/id #uuid "3906c88a-6cae-5eae-a3e4-0a590c5f854a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515749} {:answer/id #uuid "42e6e239-6429-513a-845b-06a06182439d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515756} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa7169a-7e10-59d1-b9c9-bb98ee7d9981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515777} {:answer/id #uuid "b653017b-5c45-5ebc-ab75-53d6c1b84da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515756} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b32ad3-3562-5293-912b-f6dcacf94d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d4e8bdc3-e66f-5636-b57f-ea5c00423763" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-7,$ then $\\frac{7}{8}tan⁡(-x+\\pi )+\\frac{3}{8}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-7) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}tan⁡(-x+\\pi )+\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{7}{8}\\cdot 7+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbbdfef-ff18-5840-a720-bf3a6e3607ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4} + 6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515752} {:answer/id #uuid "543421b3-6f98-5b39-8bf2-a75be506ac0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515761} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c4bac2-ad78-587b-af3a-667ee44fb5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515783} {:answer/id #uuid "d814092d-bfbb-5323-a7b1-522b7df39eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515796} {:answer/id #uuid "deab0bda-34c3-53e6-babc-d4b07ef2a1d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4} + 6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515783} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15360d1-03e2-5d57-9abe-a5cfabf7bc07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f09b88d6-2fb6-5a40-9a66-3a39d967393d" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = \\frac{7}{4}$, then $\\tan (-x + 6π) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+6\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x + 6π)=-\\frac{7}{4}.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "69f76729-7902-5b5a-8a10-7b18b0c93c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516343} {:answer/id #uuid "da1dde96-344e-5189-a136-92a1f985bda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516572} {:answer/id #uuid "ec967118-cd09-5a54-87b7-75d3a51a6469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b3c7ed-0ad2-5549-acf8-acdbd9e0e4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3e^{-4(x-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516341} :answer-field/id #uuid "600c0667-9438-5a96-b5a3-42ddde81d0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07505f7b-3ca5-545d-9207-d1e1cfa4ebbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343946" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{-4(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ -3 & =A{e}^{-4}+0 \\\\ A & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-3{e}^{4}){e}^{-4x}=-3{e}^{4-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-3{e}^{4-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152d058e-e060-5154-920e-08f3d6b98426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516153} {:answer/id #uuid "2db6b4d0-742c-582f-b93d-371992bf1b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516206} {:answer/id #uuid "31db2f98-07b7-509d-a62c-b977feaa1ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516217} {:answer/id #uuid "97a54a04-fc50-5f6c-af22-ba0a20eb76c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516426} {:answer/id #uuid "b8075a70-ebe1-5696-8f99-e244b69d1b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516206} :answer-field/id #uuid "d539a766-54de-5613-af75-0127b62c8b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f86c4d0-d8e3-5aa8-b5e8-035ca8958055" :question/math-academy-id "q-66712" :question/problem "The equation ${log}_{64}⁡4=\\frac{1}{3}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{b}=4.$ What is the value of $b?$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{64}⁡4=\\frac{1}{3}$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=4,$ $b=64,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${64}^{1/3}=4,$ which is also equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{64}=4.$ Consequently, $b=64.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69f76729-7902-5b5a-8a10-7b18b0c93c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516343} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2e710c-dbbc-5a88-97d0-fac56a741c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516353} {:answer/id #uuid "a3050a44-f811-560c-a588-19c646545845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516449} {:answer/id #uuid "da1dde96-344e-5189-a136-92a1f985bda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516572} {:answer/id #uuid "ec967118-cd09-5a54-87b7-75d3a51a6469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516603} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d1d587f-06a5-573f-87f3-c5fd92794c8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1e29259a-0f4e-5a3f-8104-feccbcb01883" :question/math-academy-id "q-95500" :question/problem "If $y=2\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce8fb57-7506-51c7-b90a-ddd7717dc73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516406}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516406} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c14681-361a-58ea-b4a8-72d314ead574" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a3b35fb-676f-5131-9e77-51463f02e70a" :question/math-academy-id "q-252568" :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{9}⁡9=1$ is equivalent to ${b}^{1}=9,$  then $b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{9}⁡9=1$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=9,$ $b=9,$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${9}^{1}=9.$ Consequently, $b=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4330ae15-f6f7-57b1-809f-2e8698af44d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516246} :answer-field/id #uuid "99dc7578-e9a5-5def-a6d4-42f491265aea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62333d8a-a547-57b1-ac3c-5c56714cd529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516326} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81e3147-4f8f-5dc1-aa20-2dd74fcc8034" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a8971db-b741-5f69-8444-adec12e8e30e" :question/math-academy-id "q-252634" :question/problem "Express the equation ${log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ in exponential form. Write the exponential term on the left-hand side of the equation.\n\n{{field-1}}$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{125},$ $b=5,$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{5}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248c9929-9c65-53a5-a9cc-d21a16479557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516181} {:answer/id #uuid "4da4d7be-c5a8-584c-91b0-619bdf3835fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516271} {:answer/id #uuid "5f494f82-2a8e-511a-a5f7-95c5c399691d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516319} {:answer/id #uuid "63f3ea92-f52c-56c3-8b20-3b383c879868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516328} {:answer/id #uuid "6413616d-1584-5b78-a8c7-cbeb44d42980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516271} :answer-field/id #uuid "934413ab-30d2-543e-88f1-d9983567fd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "64f4689c-0324-5dae-856b-8909998bf3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-2980" :question/problem "The equation ${log}_{5}⁡25=2$ is equivalent to ${5}^{x}=25.$ Determine the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{5}⁡25=2$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=25,$ $b=5,$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${5}^{2}=25.$ Consequently, $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00db171e-86f4-578f-baa9-eeb4a13a294d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516113} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7a31b2-8edf-5725-b08a-4ca04a19f52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516267} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc33023-61a9-57c1-b84d-42d2debd3175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516405} {:answer/id #uuid "8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516411} :answer-field/id #uuid "51585168-5999-52ac-a302-915344f4951e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "784a3ecb-16b0-5fa6-bed2-991dfa6c8b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-344269" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-6,6]$, where $f$ is $12$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $12$-periodic on $[-6,6]$, we have $L=6$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccc9c21-c3ce-5f4a-ba59-583860541dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516269} :answer-field/id #uuid "9688ef1e-aa0f-557f-92e7-8f181132182a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a160ea33-0abc-551b-81fb-aa9297d75028" :question/math-academy-id "q-343758" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{5(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ -2 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=-2{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-2{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1e87fa-a67e-5e2b-a4d6-91106614efde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x+18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516548} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6982d7-16d9-5cb0-8eec-9bbb54099cee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4eeca3f-6b34-52eb-84b0-9a3e73ae1349" :question/math-academy-id "q-341812" :question/problem "If $y=arsinh\\,(\\frac{x-3}{3}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(arsinh\\,(\\frac{x-3}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}+9}{9}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-6x+9)+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x+18}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9c06735-3f62-57f4-8f53-da25745305f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516467} :answer-field/id #uuid "0930c3d0-53cc-5d15-bfd8-a8503884e324" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aae92775-ad9b-57e5-9350-754a9cbc68ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-252538" :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{2}⁡(128)=7$ is equivalent to ${b}^{7}=128,$  then $b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{2}⁡(128)=7$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=128,$ $b=2,$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${2}^{7}=128.$ Consequently,  $b=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e14832-25ec-5cc2-9b80-eef2d0a5f2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516425} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8746fb-2ea4-58ca-a484-de8db975ddb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516429} {:answer/id #uuid "be7962f1-2c0b-553d-affa-31d3acba17bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516514} {:answer/id #uuid "f9874a00-5d86-506a-b77b-4b0ce2fc6efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516425} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8e4fce-3b23-5eb0-9715-6b625c2b4dc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1ec13b9-1623-5452-ac2a-c54844694d83" :question/math-academy-id "q-344034" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $3$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{5}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4dc213e-ad65-5a21-812e-d696846cae8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516487} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ade9f6-f749-5ec9-893d-33df04f83e39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c561c471-e505-55e6-9a2d-28f93bd6cab2" :question/math-academy-id "q-252619" :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${b}^{-3}=a,$  then $a=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=64,$ $b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-3}=64.$ Consequently, $a=64.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6247ae8b-2461-524c-b981-742827e2fda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517668} {:answer/id #uuid "915c0e2a-9156-54ec-b29f-1646bcf7dd2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517760} {:answer/id #uuid "ae075889-b237-5b8c-aed0-c9f5c77b5f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517813} {:answer/id #uuid "aee53829-c80e-5bd1-b6e9-71ef8ea7524f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517816} {:answer/id #uuid "fd707c27-b006-564e-8138-9351ed3b20b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517813} :answer-field/id #uuid "a19335da-c75a-5af0-9446-4461926c8472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25969868-abb0-5d93-a9b3-c99a6eb6f487" :question/math-academy-id "q-99676" :question/problem "${log}_{13}⁡1=$" :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=13.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{13}⁡1=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd368cc-9a2b-551e-b564-ee313f513e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517541} {:answer/id #uuid "20deeaf4-0740-55aa-9dcb-2e6f140632ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517545} {:answer/id #uuid "79c50765-9d84-524e-b588-8c069de3e84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517717} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d57042-0b74-581c-8b12-7c36887caccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} {:answer/id #uuid "fb517908-0db1-516e-8b98-2030ddb3dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff11dfc-8ed3-50df-b798-7c071f983a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fd4a0bf-2844-5191-8ab1-3fd5afa7e748" :question/math-academy-id "q-343666" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $$\n\\displaystyle p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a61f89c-26da-50ae-8ae5-3418c4b833b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517537} {:answer/id #uuid "721d61dd-96f8-5406-a3f6-fcb23bf6f475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517707} {:answer/id #uuid "9aec64e1-f74d-5064-8109-55570118f286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517784} {:answer/id #uuid "a20c2896-a265-53fe-b858-9d8a4b872073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517793} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb701d8-fd21-54d5-abf7-f1c31f1d67ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-$\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517537} :answer-field/id #uuid "495b0eea-23f2-5d2e-91e8-fcc19023131f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "64f9b7ab-1e87-57f9-b089-cc6f9c501397" :question/math-academy-id "q-2974" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{27}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{27}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}={3}^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})=-3\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b9b4a1-9068-553a-ae2e-30dbf20be4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517499} {:answer/id #uuid "814f1f82-22dc-5c8d-891c-3328494b799e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517733} {:answer/id #uuid "81bd13e4-d7a2-5838-845b-af517aaee57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517734} {:answer/id #uuid "81e5843d-2f47-5715-a318-45d7445db611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517735} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b24d2b-5e6b-5a01-8823-5b502061c87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517733} :answer-field/id #uuid "2084aa97-e12f-5fe8-a10d-790d4754b710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c887066-3c08-5cb4-b40e-433eb14c4b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-26042" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{81}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{81}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{81}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}={3}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{81}\\right)={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})=-4\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006ced18-63ca-5194-b549-3abf13fc8a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517495} :answer-field/id #uuid "57eb417a-dd62-519e-be44-c8803a43e66f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78d962fb-98a1-54a9-be40-612e78bd3a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-254909" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[7,\\,6,\\,-2]^{T}$ and $b=[-1,\\,2,\\,4]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|7-(-1)|,|6-2|,|-2-4|) \\\\  & =max(8,4,6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6e500d-6110-570c-ab83-90ca0a6cd642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517571} :answer-field/id #uuid "5175a214-a64f-5932-be81-c533d3570807" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79a48270-e1b9-5009-9c26-f6b63d86b07c" :question/math-academy-id "q-254915" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[0,\\,-6]^{T}$ and $b=[0.5,\\,-7]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|0-0.5|,|-6-(-7)|) \\\\  & =max(0.5,1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2727e3c-066f-56f4-a2a7-2b6b842a164d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517882} :answer-field/id #uuid "132b1cae-24b3-5188-adb0-73906416cf99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ac6fcb3-d9b4-5799-8b69-524dd3ce6133" :question/math-academy-id "q-254910" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[-3,\\,5,\\,1]^{T}$ and $b=[2,\\,7,\\,4]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|-3-2|,|5-7|,|1-4|) \\\\  & =max(5,2,3) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052a816a-dd80-53d9-8744-1364e26474e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517500} {:answer/id #uuid "12ff10ec-5a7a-55fc-b908-c9ff4e0c695d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517524} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8d8649-5cc9-56af-a20d-b5ec14df4541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517605} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2ff1f9-d4c1-52af-87f3-e719c890e1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} 