 [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd14dfe-60bb-58a0-bd26-51dc968d9c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517519} {:answer/id #uuid "22541458-e554-52ec-8376-f299f409d8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517552} {:answer/id #uuid "6bba5b70-c8d5-58be-9d37-6faff7852146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517690} {:answer/id #uuid "86f365f9-48ea-58f6-a215-e979a92b0ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517741} {:answer/id #uuid "e4207623-dab0-5d9b-b03e-704090e79618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517690} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3be8dc-1d5d-59cc-95b3-03af33b2fb4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e61eca8e-14ab-54c4-ad94-08eb1910feb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108073" :question/problem "${log}_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=8$ and $x=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cf4a4a3-14bc-51ba-a37c-be18c84ace28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517540} {:answer/id #uuid "6cde5cd2-e2e9-5557-835f-19622e863fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517694} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d68802-c015-562c-b0cc-e6654c19cc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517829} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c6d849-d0bb-517e-b070-3a6e3ac252ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517838} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5b7f0f-32e3-515a-bd56-56eb0051bb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517945} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3754e4-2314-5993-97b9-7e9cfa346294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f14ba22e-b6ea-5533-a5ad-bd5ce2cfcd84" :question/math-academy-id "q-108076" :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{72}⁡(72).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡n=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=72.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{72}⁡72=1\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "e4de6a79-9670-5cc7-841f-c24ff0103135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{arcosh}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141d92b1-19f8-549f-9528-6eca52259abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519026} {:answer/id #uuid "45425c6b-a98b-51fa-8eca-55d7ba992558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519099} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9c6ce2-7d9b-519d-97b9-3134db09d6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519160} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f87cad-f947-5cf2-a0e5-dd1577365c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519283} {:answer/id #uuid "c32c0b40-a807-5d90-aa7a-5b8fcac8a181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519160} :answer-field/id #uuid "a91548fc-7110-5f63-9d3c-0095f5b5ffe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1395e862-a7e8-537a-97de-69c4caaca685" :question/math-academy-id "q-144839" :question/problem "![](images/21/219e476902934a4272fb0c523fe0b623f27ef5ba2647fcf4658d0b262e64200e.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(5)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =3 \\\\ f(0) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-8 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{3,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9984ad-b58e-50e4-9a28-2c6aa4654635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519014} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad41d34f-e05e-58cf-bab8-f5b61c5398a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13ad1074-2be7-58b6-8b52-de567a736fd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-316301" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcffe721c35a3c729644fb36ee9008095c46298a56109f0dfd75a3c06f3f7cae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right).\n\nGiven that $f(2)=-3,$ what is the maximum value ${f}_{\\text{max}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(4+(-2)) \\\\  & =-3-2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,-1,-5\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe41923-7be4-516e-afbe-7eb6f9a11be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519229} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfb1f5aa-b5e4-53c8-93c1-41b509c0b3ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "82901cf9-17f2-500f-9676-c95d18dab327" :question/math-academy-id "q-344166" :question/problem "![](images/31/31fd7b63abbf8224421ee53b0703e9cc72fac2f9a8ffc3e460218bb42c4f229a.png)\n\nThe function $q(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,9].$ The graph of ${q}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $q(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $12.$\n\nGiven that $q(9)=-1,$ what is the minimum value ${q}_{\\text{min}}$ of $q(x)$ on the interval $[1,9]?$\n\n${q}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $q(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${q}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,9),$ and ${q}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $q$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(9) & =-1 \\\\ q(1) & =q(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{1}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =q(9)-{\\int }_{1}^{9}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1-12 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $q$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $q$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,-13\\}=-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $q$ is ${q}_{\\text{min}}=-13,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $q(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3641a945-2d7f-53df-9532-8edc301d039a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519075} :answer-field/id #uuid "139d21af-01a8-549d-89ac-a4e1d1e8fbed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a322e8e4-fc5c-530f-aece-0d3557ac2a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-344108" :question/problem "![](images/84/84deebb7aa20aee2bb0b9e57db825fbdfac1482ca4aec7202ba5458c5603492f.png)\n\nThe function $t(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${t}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $t(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$\n\nGiven that $t(2)=3,$ what is the maximum value ${t}_{\\text{max}}$ of $t(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${t}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $t(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${t}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${t}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $t$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2) & =3 \\\\ t(-4) & =t(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =t(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3-(-8) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $t$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $t$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{t}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $t$ is ${t}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $t(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee602556-4b14-51e0-a262-9ca407ca2dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519383} :answer-field/id #uuid "371f6b9e-7664-5f83-883e-450ac8a167d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a3fba9ae-fcbd-5c75-9ad3-403a4a333328" :question/math-academy-id "q-344182" :question/problem "![](images/04/04e4299da4d4c29cce751d04ee6ee29380a3dead3ab3456f6645858b5d9d106b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(3)=2,$ what is the minimum value ${f}_{\\text{min}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c30d7e1d2c9eb939726d85b0ee5d51a2bdc0cfe73fab92f0ba29839a9f18be4e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-7.5 \\\\  & =-5.5 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-3+7.5) \\\\  & =2-4.5 \\\\  & =-2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,-5.5,-2.5\\}=-5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b8d084-32d1-5d13-b2b6-29a387928867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519002} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0754120-f98a-50c1-b1fd-4396f034bf3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b09a5298-e405-5905-9c2a-958d82f56eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344104" :question/problem "![](images/21/21e0ed364f3a04068c7f3775715432b218be2ada6b54d97ae6836f28528006da.png)\n\nThe function $s(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${s}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $s(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$\n\nGiven that $s(9)=-2,$ what is the maximum value ${s}_{\\text{max}}$ of $s(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]?$\n\n${s}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $s(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(9) & =-2 \\\\ s(0) & =s(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{0}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =s(9)-{\\int }_{0}^{9}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2-(-6) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,4\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ff0eb8e-cde3-5239-b043-47a136211ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519161} :answer-field/id #uuid "c40dea29-2b27-54dd-9da2-418a3d1e8a16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e747dd9d-512a-59fa-9944-1137ebe8b2f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344167" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dd61819859d65af85ef462c7fb694ecbfd2640aa62f41ff0e27e5bb8073f48c.png)\n\nThe function $a(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${a}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $a(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $11.$\n\nGiven that $a(5)=-4,$ what is the minimum value ${a}_{\\text{min}}$ of $a(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]?$\n\n${a}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $a(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${a}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${a}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $a$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(5) & =-4 \\\\ a(-2) & =a(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =a(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4-(-11) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $a$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $a$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{7,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $a$ is ${a}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $a(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87b49dea-876c-5d58-b701-71dbe94190f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519208} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bf91a3-f797-5574-a5b8-6124d6526096" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ebf67b7c-064d-5c23-8f05-31612119932c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344100" :question/problem "![](images/95/955cda48e8e98f46903a471ceefa657a6032cb2e6feab304569a62750eba9825.png)\n\nThe function $r(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,7].$ The graph of ${r}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $r(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$\n\nGiven that $r(7)=6,$ what is the maximum value ${r}_{\\text{max}}$ of $r(x)$ on the interval $[-1,7]?$\n\n${r}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $r(x)$ on $[-1,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${r}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,7),$ and ${r}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $r$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(7) & =6 \\\\ r(-1) & =r(7)+{\\int }_{7}^{-1}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =r(7)-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6-(-10) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $r$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $r$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{6,16\\}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $r$ is ${r}_{\\text{max}}=16,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $r(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6b1671-0717-5482-8d52-d0c250e890fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520056} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd11be-33ac-578f-99a4-b5fbfef6ba96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520145} {:answer/id #uuid "862feb99-123c-5810-b1cc-80efe435e8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520187} {:answer/id #uuid "d30d25a2-0e79-557a-a62e-539de549b8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} :answer-field/id #uuid "06c9a2bb-e41c-52c6-8b2c-a83b5dffeaf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104994" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(3,-3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(3,-3)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240af742-1ebc-5760-a0f2-621341e0c161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520043} :answer-field/id #uuid "465397d6-a020-5d18-96a2-fa78f2cfd4d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "83895ad6-7f6a-50d5-a3b6-af8475bcabcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-341514" :question/problem "If $y=2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}}]\\cdot {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ede78e57-6cef-5911-b9d8-27b6208cdab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\cot x}{\\sqrt{1+\\sin^4 x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520342} :answer-field/id #uuid "404a0d84-133e-5c7d-befe-504815e54709" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "84e76c93-4252-53d8-b14a-d598aa6e8e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-341483" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡x)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|{sin}^{2}⁡x|\\sqrt{1+{({sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{2}}}]\\cdot {({sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbe8f32-b269-5817-8957-1ff911e57228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln(3+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520113} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cb44d4-8640-5f83-ba9b-96604fc838db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da7e5a10-376b-5a22-a310-bd1bf7fa24cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-300604" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3.$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{9-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29711ca7-b3cc-53ec-bec7-d90a0aeb16e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520050} {:answer/id #uuid "3df5a0e2-1ea8-5fe2-a697-20a5c5faa39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520080} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0ee0e0-9a43-5369-b3d7-8e3c43d21d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520275} {:answer/id #uuid "ce672a55-9e57-56eb-a86f-0f1f3507439c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520280} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9f7794-bd99-5638-b872-bfdbc7829cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520280} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0046f1-2976-582a-85ca-24a28e09b21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105213" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(-1,1)<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,1)=det({f}^{″}(-1,1))=(-2)^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)=({f}^{″}(-1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(-1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0,$ the point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e38fe49d-81c4-5be4-8d42-66e01917801d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521274} :answer-field/id #uuid "f25616a1-e3e9-5a57-bef3-4345308333c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "094cdd1f-e814-5164-bdf6-dc0f3e0f507e" :question/math-academy-id "q-251857" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $40$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{40},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{40}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=54,\\,{S}_{yy}=35,\\,{S}_{xy}=188.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=188.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{188}{40} \\\\  & =4.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f6a7f83-6364-5d0a-9fd6-ef7977380a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520945} :answer-field/id #uuid "203627a4-892f-5453-bd75-4c6fd9a28c76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1470b28d-5083-563f-9b26-d7812411f35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-251861" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $15$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{15}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=105,\\,{S}_{yy}=81,\\,{S}_{xy}=69.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=69.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{69}{15} \\\\  & =4.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49efc351-4e26-566c-99c2-fe234a1dd84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520930} {:answer/id #uuid "83f896a5-bf32-5e5d-9cc6-f790b29f490e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521065} {:answer/id #uuid "96cf3b15-d8b8-5670-bef5-bd091199fa39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521109} {:answer/id #uuid "a99d6b81-4b37-544d-99ea-7af055e746a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521144} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed5de-3dc1-5a83-a649-aaf890348062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521149} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd517342-4196-5078-a01c-54f228cf9d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bdcc65b-8401-5044-8aa5-c9a7e37ffa6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162890" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-7(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-5+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-5t+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-5(2\\pi )+0)-(1-5(0)+0) \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170b7b59-9320-58fb-b101-0feb53b6825e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "101.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520816} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6971a9-86cf-535c-854c-c7408df6402f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3d28482b-a0c8-53e6-a980-b5acf56e83ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-241885" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =0.64$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha =1.25$, where $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ are acute angles measured in degrees, then $\\theta +\\alpha $ in degrees to one decimal place is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ we use $arccos$ and $arctan$ respectively. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0.64 \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64) \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha ) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta +\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\  & =(50.208\\,1…)^{∘}+(51.340\\,1…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(101.548\\,2…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{101.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "297ecca7-1d04-565f-97fa-4bf34f030962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520853} {:answer/id #uuid "38cd3098-2a5e-55de-9315-3b8f18a09933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520887} {:answer/id #uuid "98bf55fb-3a39-5303-9541-a60e5da17d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521111} {:answer/id #uuid "a32d8fdf-be70-57b2-8de9-9bbc2f2e4992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "All of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521131} {:answer/id #uuid "b2930480-2eb9-594e-9c28-dd13242c011e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521162} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d10573-cd86-5734-a745-dadd21dc2271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d61adfc9-5b12-5408-9a76-aa7eede82be4" :question/math-academy-id "q-41715" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. $Q{Q}^{T}=I$\n\nII. ${Q}^{2}$ is the zero matrix\n\nIII. If $A=QB{Q}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\nIt's true by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{2}=Q\\ne O.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(QB{Q}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(Q)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b65618-43ee-532e-a839-a31e265d785c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.812" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522059} {:answer/id #uuid "180aac80-cd6c-553f-9390-bd5447a64e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.917" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522070} {:answer/id #uuid "202305ae-992e-5dff-bc4e-bc45335f1658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.714" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522092} {:answer/id #uuid "6d30e107-e689-5ca8-ade5-ea5a816ad541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.893" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522241} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c52165-df02-5476-941b-b3846aa44552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.765" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522059} :answer-field/id #uuid "e017985b-c529-50e2-9421-397fcbb26ef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c277570-c608-5c8a-9eec-65493369dacb" :question/math-academy-id "q-108372" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{11}⁡(7)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{11}⁡(7)$ by changing the base $11$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=11,$ $X=7,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{11}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(7)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,098}{1.041\\,393} \\\\  & ≈0.812\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c12419-a232-5cd2-b235-39a6a1843fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522164} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3e8381-6b4f-5e93-ae04-8cf9545b1218" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7501f09d-5828-597b-9243-c1ed53d4a63e" :question/math-academy-id "q-262939" :question/problem "If ${log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{k},$ where $k$ is an integer, then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=125$ to base $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125} & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f05b9105-6358-56c2-af8c-ebeaec8564d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522531} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c30e83-ea24-50fa-94a2-e0cd96a4bf88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "834c413e-3660-5a12-aa09-b30d7cd9a51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-262839" :question/problem "Let $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t.$ Express ${log}_{1000}⁡x$ via the variable $t.$\n\n${log}_{1000}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=1000$ to base $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{1000}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)} & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡({10}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{1000}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e53d2060-f4c9-588d-b716-6b6725d895f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.416" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522509} :answer-field/id #uuid "99060676-2b65-517f-864d-a46840395897" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd36bb1c-2d38-5fe5-b95b-ae2bbaecd296" :question/math-academy-id "q-262883" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}⁡(19)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n${log}_{8}⁡(19)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}⁡(19)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=19,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(19) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(19)}{{log}_{10}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(19)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1.278\\,754}{0.903\\,090} \\\\  & ≈1.416\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0546a58c-c189-5300-98ab-59e9dfa06285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523262} {:answer/id #uuid "0718ee2b-934a-5775-88b9-654a98a3df48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{csch}^{2}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523271} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5366df-ea2b-5885-a020-a71b3a496864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523346} {:answer/id #uuid "4a262826-fbb0-5320-bb2e-6379a76bc331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523406} {:answer/id #uuid "51c51e52-9010-5b3c-8047-10aa85488383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x{tanh}^{2}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523419} {:answer/id #uuid "58a1b07b-7bfc-5b97-8af6-3ca183bb6652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523436} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf34f11-a6ed-51e9-8bc5-739fb989d58d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{sech}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523475} {:answer/id #uuid "73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523490} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd63de1-2c45-5706-8764-86c4c4a37694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523524} {:answer/id #uuid "8b14f7f2-bbd6-5875-adfd-d43889b7fb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523541} {:answer/id #uuid "91812201-758f-5436-91d6-fd93a8c56e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{18}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523559} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2721fb-1174-5306-a632-bbe89f079355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523575} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb97b6b-b48e-57d0-b4f2-a804af9d0be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523581} {:answer/id #uuid "a095eeaa-71bd-5a30-9655-035de8150b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1+2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523588} {:answer/id #uuid "b03d0bbd-2440-5230-9491-512dd5859d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523624} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b12e96-3aee-519e-afa6-98c880294233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{csch}^{2}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523626} {:answer/id #uuid "b333d000-5330-53c7-b6f3-3aea595f25e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523632} {:answer/id #uuid "b75d5654-537e-53c4-bc29-9857e0f9d0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523637} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8e33a9-dc6f-59b9-918f-d2977e259e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523648} {:answer/id #uuid "c03bde7f-fe4b-5a37-b96a-bf72de4e1aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523657} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4b8906-6e7c-5f46-b779-8abef094c635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{sech}^{2}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523679} {:answer/id #uuid "ced58843-851c-5823-a2b2-32f84d2a86b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523687} {:answer/id #uuid "e28ecdb1-81b7-55ae-928b-208d2f35a6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(1-2x{coth}^{2}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523728} {:answer/id #uuid "e33ea1d6-0d1e-5b38-a621-13b06c55032e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58a1b07b-7bfc-5b97-8af6-3ca183bb6652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523436} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf34f11-a6ed-51e9-8bc5-739fb989d58d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{sech}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523475} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb97b6b-b48e-57d0-b4f2-a804af9d0be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523581} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8e33a9-dc6f-59b9-918f-d2977e259e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523648} {:answer/id #uuid "e33ea1d6-0d1e-5b38-a621-13b06c55032e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523731}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523648} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b899f25-79c8-5b1d-9f3a-fc090dc3cb6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04ba3fd8-1522-5fe9-a412-8ecb77ad06a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95894" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}^{′}\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot {(2x)}^{′}}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2{csch}^{2}⁡2x)\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot (2)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c27343-2ffe-5ef0-95ae-6705c24c596c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523360} {:answer/id #uuid "7195e49e-14d0-5d15-b093-d2bc20c75842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523488} {:answer/id #uuid "86eef243-02da-54f6-8ff5-46912fa718e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523537} {:answer/id #uuid "9adf3a93-53a6-5212-9c75-fbcceecb6e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523577} {:answer/id #uuid "be8f074c-4255-5799-bd18-aae998046419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523488} :answer-field/id #uuid "0708b89d-7f7a-5637-8493-1ca6f099288c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "14bd2170-88ec-5846-a2ce-ea9d86c753dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86335" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce513880934a99b289c22f734e8a455c3534bddd911968cd06b7fe88f65441aa.png)\n\nA hot air balloon $B$ is flying horizontally to the observer $O$ at a constant height of $300\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the balloon and an observer is increasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the balloon when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/81/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a262826-fbb0-5320-bb2e-6379a76bc331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523406} {:answer/id #uuid "51c51e52-9010-5b3c-8047-10aa85488383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x{tanh}^{2}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523419} {:answer/id #uuid "a095eeaa-71bd-5a30-9655-035de8150b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1+2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523588} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9239d3-c5f2-52a5-a408-c11d0f1f88ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(1+2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523681} {:answer/id #uuid "e28ecdb1-81b7-55ae-928b-208d2f35a6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(1-2x{coth}^{2}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523406} :answer-field/id #uuid "c10c01cc-e0f9-571f-a0fb-7e4e517f6d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26dd88c1-a8ad-595c-af19-c13d906feda9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95587" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=x{sech}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={sech}^{2}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(x)}^{′}\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot {({sech}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x-2x{sech}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1493380f-c708-5ae2-ad63-0ba124cacc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523297} {:answer/id #uuid "368ef356-d80b-56d6-b93f-d58e6e4b200b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.02\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523370} {:answer/id #uuid "69614c5f-1731-5732-8a72-f2c31ba8aa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523471} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbc4914-cd92-5ba6-9302-86b96db75dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523579} {:answer/id #uuid "dddb86d6-5fec-56b1-9fda-ca96845e548d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523719} :answer-field/id #uuid "d828883d-a3eb-5584-b5bd-f62f8f377e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "579cea6b-f87a-550f-8093-6a43bd1625a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-86352" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f4638863dbc6bdfbff00d20d2153bb73eb7779719bbc7235d730539cb92329e.png)\n\nAngela observes that a drone is flying horizontally at a constant height of $5\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. If the drone is flying in the opposite direction from Angela's location at a speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ decreases at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/80/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc2bd856-1319-532a-a82f-52ad02474213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.2\\csc^{2}(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523678} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8015e3-4339-5833-a2d3-370dba776e3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5a9f47ed-553f-5a71-99da-2ec914b9fd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-314884" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf0aa0508025b800bb0707b74ffdc89b08052ad6fced401b8071d7ad741f8e0a.png)\n\nA lighthouse $L$ shines a beam that hits the surface of the sea at point $B.$ The light is mounted at a height of $10\\,\\text{m}$ above the water. As the beam is tilted downward, the angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the beam and the point $B$ is increasing at a rate of $0.12$ radians per second. What is the speed of the light spot as a function of $\\theta ?$ \n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\nThe speed of the light spot is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/bd/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523490} {:answer/id #uuid "91812201-758f-5436-91d6-fd93a8c56e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{18}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523559} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2721fb-1174-5306-a632-bbe89f079355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523575} {:answer/id #uuid "b03d0bbd-2440-5230-9491-512dd5859d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523624} {:answer/id #uuid "b75d5654-537e-53c4-bc29-9857e0f9d0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523490} :answer-field/id #uuid "da00c2cd-b023-5b35-af39-4086a9fdeebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6bda9e93-d670-52bc-bf28-dd5b8dfcba8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-121763" :question/problem "![](images/22/2264aa2a4f3d33b04190608ab638604dbe09dfd278035b2b764aa3d85d947624.png)\n\nGreta is flying a kite $K.$ The kite is  $150\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and moving horizontally towards Greta at a speed of $10\\,\\text{in/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing." :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/430d9bccfa7d9063ef3be511b894f23227fc248eb6afde9a25dc78546dde32aa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between Greta and the kite. The quantity $y=150$ inches does not change with time because the kite height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{150}{x} \\\\ x & =150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-10.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing is $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}$ radians per second."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15fca13-90c5-5a81-bbbf-80c06db848a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523658} :answer-field/id #uuid "d870faa5-187d-5160-bfd1-02d58f3d6875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82671b33-6326-52d6-9a89-56b07cb04dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343159" :question/problem "![](images/55/55e96238e8263a1744c117bad09d1b11947f70a8bf1b837c52af18cf2e244ab7.png)\n\nA park ranger $P$ stands $900\\,\\text{ft}$ away from the base of a lookout tower $T$ to track a drone $D$ that is ascending vertically. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the ranger and the drone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ radians per second. At what rate is the drone gaining altitude at the moment when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone’s altitude is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d3/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}\n$$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{60}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{60}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bda358-e756-5a52-bd52-be83ca51ee6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{csch}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523291} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd63de1-2c45-5706-8764-86c4c4a37694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523524} {:answer/id #uuid "b333d000-5330-53c7-b6f3-3aea595f25e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523632} {:answer/id #uuid "c03bde7f-fe4b-5a37-b96a-bf72de4e1aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523657} {:answer/id #uuid "ced58843-851c-5823-a2b2-32f84d2a86b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa2d316-4c50-510d-84fd-3aca1c107d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9541bdac-540f-537e-a50e-b6a223ada95f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95575" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=3{csch}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=3{g}^{2},\\,g=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6g(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0718ee2b-934a-5775-88b9-654a98a3df48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{csch}^{2}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523271} {:answer/id #uuid "15d0f986-68fd-5c05-b61f-2cb59af8e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sech}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523303} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b12e96-3aee-519e-afa6-98c880294233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{csch}^{2}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523626} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4b8906-6e7c-5f46-b779-8abef094c635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{sech}^{2}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523679} {:answer/id #uuid "ea15c1a2-ef8e-52bd-843d-747ef10b4c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523626} :answer-field/id #uuid "d79f42f3-7a3e-5cfc-ae54-7f17a62555f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c4169769-2e08-50ac-8627-9cd65f653a9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95567" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡g,\\,g=4x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2{csch}^{2}\\,g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2{csch}^{2}⁡g\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31679908-2281-5375-8e4f-ab234176aa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523356} {:answer/id #uuid "5aae7333-f8d9-59d1-980d-c32006c2344a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523438} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3a5aba-80e0-512f-9adf-de9315810712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523523} {:answer/id #uuid "a505aa4e-e343-59d5-ac42-7ab4c259cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523601} {:answer/id #uuid "a859c172-a2a7-57b0-955b-6cfdff5733c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523608} :answer-field/id #uuid "c764bd62-dac9-5c30-b3ea-6e5444aa2fdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e9479f5b-c4df-5efe-97d6-27688a7060c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83678" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a37ae024b0472dfc38ecff01b898d8d3ed2384d90acf432d747efee1568520e.png)\n\nA car $C$ drives toward a $108\\,\\text{m}$ high building $B$ following a straight path. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the car and the top of the building $T$ is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ radians per second. What is the speed of the car when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f2/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}\n$$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6951626e-b13c-5bfd-b94e-4811c1c0288e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523470} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea67d905-4e13-5fb0-9a4b-c810c57cacc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "edce8cb0-3424-5fe2-ac49-b2f607c023b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343202" :question/problem "![](images/30/305398b239bbc784fb5e5a1ec7ef2020a19a033dcda150e84f9c4a2a72a96adf.png)\n\nA lifeguard $L$ is watching a weather balloon $W$ rise vertically from a point $S$ on the beach. The point $S$ is $10\\,\\text{m}$ away from the lifeguard. If the balloon is rising at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the lifeguard and the balloon is increasing at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b4/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "01fd6b21-4e7c-562e-8083-7e0e34e7e8c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524380} {:answer/id #uuid "04767459-77d9-53e0-8f3a-4e50f91b46c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524385} {:answer/id #uuid "2e708ed0-211d-5ee2-b1e8-74d164752177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524467} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2dd98c-0888-5e14-a9da-7bfa796c8fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524538} {:answer/id #uuid "6440a19f-723d-54fa-8469-a5e28443074d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524546} {:answer/id #uuid "68314971-85df-5e1a-bb8d-30df1853ed17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524552} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4b0471-37b3-54f6-a468-ae5368af1054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524633} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9737e3-0ec2-5ba3-add4-cec93d9b1ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524758} {:answer/id #uuid "e46fb9aa-b37c-53cd-95f3-b44d1c1a0d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04767459-77d9-53e0-8f3a-4e50f91b46c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524385} {:answer/id #uuid "6440a19f-723d-54fa-8469-a5e28443074d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524546} {:answer/id #uuid "a28440b4-85d4-5314-bd6f-4661a409d1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524641} {:answer/id #uuid "fa166499-b1c8-5634-aac0-e2d68fea0b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524830} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7a3b35-80dc-562d-8811-ba107d45cb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524641} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f722b7c-71a8-54c2-bd1f-6c40dfef6193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "31ce7adb-3732-5d9f-92f5-a50363ba4c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95547" :question/problem "If $y=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b8fe3d-8331-5609-a33b-334910eff9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524392} {:answer/id #uuid "261a9480-07c6-5439-8161-94b991801e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524454} {:answer/id #uuid "2be118f0-398f-5be1-86b7-522a08c5d5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524461} {:answer/id #uuid "6be9a124-f36b-5039-beb4-9169cea0786a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524556} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e80193-25cd-5cb0-a082-8787876542f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524556} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5f3fbc-3413-5fe3-a14b-7220c2d641bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59718fdb-33b2-584f-912f-49ef25f56ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-144867" :question/problem "![](images/89/89445981059ca7902f22c916f7241573dcfa8b70798b067896b9a30d0913d251.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $9,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(5)=10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =10 \\\\ f(1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-9 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-(-3+9) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{10,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524441} {:answer/id #uuid "47a3f504-906f-5250-a397-60c7605afb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524506} {:answer/id #uuid "8d428376-e2b0-53e2-b314-797b7522cd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524605} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b2a43-bd2d-5fe7-a7da-2538cecd54fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524650} {:answer/id #uuid "e12ce1c7-49ac-5fd0-9ccc-f097ef76e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524441} :answer-field/id #uuid "717c0cdc-ae39-5590-87a2-a75a93baebfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77c551c0-c155-56eb-9f11-d8d660941796" :question/math-academy-id "q-107463" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((4)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\,\\sqrt{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={({4}^{2x+1})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{2(-1)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524434} {:answer/id #uuid "3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524481} {:answer/id #uuid "5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524530} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524638} {:answer/id #uuid "f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524827} :answer-field/id #uuid "079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94800" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d81d09-9d4d-5817-aef8-323fc9bab399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524423} :answer-field/id #uuid "697491e1-d5eb-582f-b6a9-c05cd0efc616" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7b99a120-8ee4-5c4d-80c5-2291fb8275da" :question/math-academy-id "q-313249" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)},$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\frac{1}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1).$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-(2x-1)^{2}}\\cdot (2x-1)^{′}=\\frac{2}{4x-4{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(4x-4{x}^{2})\\,{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{1}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f0e03b-b91a-5f40-83a8-85dcfb2ab7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524700} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec89a11-249d-5e23-acad-e5fe2601910b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "839781ca-f17e-59bc-bcb0-dc6733a65b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344414" :question/problem "![](images/24/24ee434f11bca408587e1baa6b9189e430fa745cef0668bc35cd871761f410ed.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2,$ $6,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $h(3)=2.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =2 \\\\ h(5) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(3)-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2-(-6) \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(-3) & =h(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =8-(-2) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,8,10\\}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fd6b21-4e7c-562e-8083-7e0e34e7e8c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524380} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0a76b4-f502-5e5f-ac2c-3c8acd63b518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524635} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9737e3-0ec2-5ba3-add4-cec93d9b1ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524758} {:answer/id #uuid "e46fb9aa-b37c-53cd-95f3-b44d1c1a0d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524782} {:answer/id #uuid "ea555f5f-939f-58bb-b8b4-8d5821bb15da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524758} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4cb0872-1c1c-5be2-826b-83e8a8aaf2ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8af41eb0-cd12-5003-8c4d-9b2fa7191b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-95528" :question/problem "If $y=-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}\\right)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b298866-d4d3-50ef-99c2-892e1ccbad3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524459} {:answer/id #uuid "43752373-0228-5044-b688-cb27a6eeb3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524504} {:answer/id #uuid "68878cf0-9180-5c61-9b7c-7781cf21cc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524553} {:answer/id #uuid "8970f9a2-de34-5b1a-af61-3790f8b31e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524599} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b58c4c-fba7-5228-b103-b2cb83d6e1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524784} :answer-field/id #uuid "891a58fe-e347-55e1-9227-fafd48e4c74a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9080bcff-95c0-5480-aa15-7e41729ae6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-104260" :question/problem "![](images/39/39ee1ed9a272b610d17b52f5225a1fcbcfa9966e0eb083dd8c9d53b1cd29b8b9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $11,$ $2$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,4]$ if $f(0)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-2 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-11) \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,9,0,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the values of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ are ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0,4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154fd44c-1903-5cec-bc2f-cfb042f862e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524421} {:answer/id #uuid "2e708ed0-211d-5ee2-b1e8-74d164752177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524467} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2dd98c-0888-5e14-a9da-7bfa796c8fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524538} {:answer/id #uuid "68314971-85df-5e1a-bb8d-30df1853ed17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524552} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4b0471-37b3-54f6-a468-ae5368af1054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524538} :answer-field/id #uuid "61946e48-e92a-5775-b260-b027052600f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9939cec3-a9a6-5501-8d65-e82136850623" :question/math-academy-id "q-95558" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+g(x).$ What is $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}\\right)}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fdfdccb-f867-5185-8ab5-657123634d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524517} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7c51fa-cfb6-5f55-b174-77dad9d56fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524658} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ec6c24-2292-511b-9be5-127f37247732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524697} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc7aaea-4301-584f-bdc3-af424dfb7c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524727} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ea1700-7cfc-5ba3-a0a5-c241917d08b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524727} :answer-field/id #uuid "2595da81-f0d2-506b-9398-dff018e8c2ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6c49e6b-d082-5bf8-805d-249ef4b78827" :question/math-academy-id "q-144845" :question/problem "![](images/69/69ae26109021b156ae22879f30a64c393fa9a870d207be72c75533c8b6918bb9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,6]$ given that $f(6)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(5-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,-2,0\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0077e303-78c8-5a4d-9f4e-3d35cf42cb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524378} {:answer/id #uuid "98599ccb-165d-5a9f-89f7-6a0fa52e415c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524626} {:answer/id #uuid "9b90e5cf-272b-5399-83d6-f0c4cec499fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524631} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3dcf13-c55f-5e57-ab59-e148431cea27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524632} {:answer/id #uuid "cb14986c-8086-5ecf-ba35-3b4e666cd1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524716} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5d30eaa-4a91-54ae-a4e2-bfc408fea6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba02335d-7784-52da-a978-380fc6e80144" :question/math-academy-id "q-144840" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c79d3a4de46d41da5edec58514bd7138ff3369032c76c758d57d3816f99e3ef3.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,6]$ given that $f(6)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-2 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-10) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,8\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a91dc7-6957-5530-aee4-3045d16a9de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524586} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d31afb4-f8c1-5171-921e-f2991ad5424b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3b6054b-f3bd-5aa9-aa30-6fbb1ec1ef44" :question/math-academy-id "q-344095" :question/problem "![](images/ca/cad4c483d2678c63382c13d43e3377c0a471ff510bd6b86ea37dad3adb440d9a.png)\n\nThe function $p(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${p}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $p(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$\n\nGiven that $p(3)=-4,$ what is the maximum value ${p}_{\\text{max}}$ of $p(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]?$\n\n${p}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $p(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${p}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${p}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $p$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(3) & =-4 \\\\ p(-5) & =p(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{p}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =p(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{p}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4-9 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $p$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $p$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{p}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-4,-13\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $p$ is ${p}_{\\text{max}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $p(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dcfb3be-68ac-5ef8-ac92-f7ed17e3cd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524634} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e143a5-6376-5cd6-8c7d-32d7f7188f06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5944661-3e94-5778-ad45-da56e2af2415" :question/math-academy-id "q-344398" :question/problem "![](images/66/6613de40d3150985d471d97fd3b5369cd67df2ae248b22ce99af011f3484a20c.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5,$ $4,$ and $7$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]$ given that $g(-2)=3.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2,0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =3 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ g(3) & =g(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+(-7) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ g(-5) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =g(-2)-{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,7,0,-2\\}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=7,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1852df64-07e6-553a-9474-3688514f45f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525255} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5fdc6b-2a4c-54be-b643-16f35e018c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-45{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525316} {:answer/id #uuid "40cf3a7a-0952-5273-a018-e34afb0c062f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{log}_{2}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525366} {:answer/id #uuid "437f44e9-0a0d-50f0-ab34-a609fd8094bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525375} {:answer/id #uuid "4b094227-bc65-5485-9af0-535c85dfc957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525392} {:answer/id #uuid "4df98584-3d9c-5eac-bf56-d2ad321a9f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525403} {:answer/id #uuid "740b37ab-4b45-560a-8a65-81214e71c672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{log}_{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525501} {:answer/id #uuid "b1237af5-4852-5789-b2cd-e262f19e7ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525655} {:answer/id #uuid "b166fa7f-a0e9-5cce-a229-cb09701c3928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{log}_{2}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525657} {:answer/id #uuid "cb035d36-158b-536e-b551-0ba2c7debcb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2})+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525712} {:answer/id #uuid "cb18bdf1-3016-5423-9e6a-39b690cae75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{log}_{2}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525713} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb3c03b-0c40-5e43-a2a1-5a1e290687a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{log}_{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525721} {:answer/id #uuid "d94be6fa-f6b5-513b-86de-6f8f7657c446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5{log}_{12}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525745} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8fb22c-c2d5-5de9-9ec3-5e0357b772f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a6c98c-df20-52e9-b020-5b29c2031ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525369} {:answer/id #uuid "5f74a86d-e3cc-5dbf-bdd7-a4113fc9ffda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-\\frac{x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525448} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc4bc64-d436-586c-8553-f98dd5c6afbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\frac{x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525555} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd11786-9c3e-599a-8f4c-3162daf243f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525560} {:answer/id #uuid "f633a7e0-a621-5680-81e8-02a8418234da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525369} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb0e851-b32c-55d6-a987-0e041012f9ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0dd7b4b0-db82-575a-9dad-d6a7735e5596" :question/math-academy-id "q-114700" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int [{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1]\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+1].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1]\\text{d}x & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+1]-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f2aaec-bdad-58bd-9357-db2f7aec0e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525462} {:answer/id #uuid "660e5a36-c7b3-5888-86e1-6b4e9b38a638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525467} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbc0fd9-f288-5653-866a-e4f8e72943d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(2x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525554} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6cb00b-824e-53d9-8528-2efc1a6d7e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(2x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525600} {:answer/id #uuid "abec248d-6ade-55e3-bdab-e993fd571336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{2x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525467} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e53e3f5-af7a-50b0-9c33-a1155d8c0ec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b5dd752-1f0d-5a16-86ac-253b01c9d23a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71483" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y={log}_{2}⁡(2x+3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{2}⁡(u),\\,u=2x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5fdc6b-2a4c-54be-b643-16f35e018c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-45{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525316} {:answer/id #uuid "437f44e9-0a0d-50f0-ab34-a609fd8094bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525375} {:answer/id #uuid "b1237af5-4852-5789-b2cd-e262f19e7ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525655} {:answer/id #uuid "cb035d36-158b-536e-b551-0ba2c7debcb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2})+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525712} {:answer/id #uuid "d94be6fa-f6b5-513b-86de-6f8f7657c446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5{log}_{12}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525375} :answer-field/id #uuid "b95ad17b-d75e-510f-9ac0-ef89ae64a23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2cf033f6-5b8b-5e8f-8d55-f65db7ada288" :question/math-academy-id "q-24216" :question/problem "$4{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-12{log}_{6}⁡(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of $6$ and an argument of $x.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-12{log}_{6}⁡(x) & =(4-12){log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-8{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37e726d-bc2a-5673-bfc6-c4c9c7ba2754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\ln(x))^2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525622} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc44b4dd-a212-5cf1-811f-8d6f7f853e3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b717300-ca17-51b3-997a-8770eb9ed2a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-341490" :question/problem "If $y=\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)],$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x>0.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|}$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\{arcsch[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01087968-b5e0-5e77-8046-9109c861fbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525211} {:answer/id #uuid "342d5133-1d9b-5d7c-b9e7-094c04737cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525336} {:answer/id #uuid "61bdedb0-a0de-5b58-811f-ca4c7de81d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525453} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dfaa4a-44ef-50c8-ad65-b5ad3e047b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525671} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f9b357-1256-533e-a3ec-63e4e9ef8974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525671} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74c0c25-3def-5bbb-a734-c7a9a9a0dc1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "542b7bfd-761e-58a3-80e1-933f25032116" :question/math-academy-id "q-110771" :question/problem "If $4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x=1,$ then what is $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (4-2){log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x} & ={9}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={9}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96f60cea-d0ed-5e57-b776-5f23c8cb82cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525593} {:answer/id #uuid "a14f080b-5b79-54e6-bf53-74347cda080c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525619} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c53b31-26fe-5859-a29b-b9971753aace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525623} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e68f27-92d0-5e53-b5d3-e8b567498210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525634} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c7f516-6829-5836-adff-04dc38af5587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525593} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c758b8-da8e-5d7a-b4de-50e6b1992bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e67b4cc-0d2e-51f0-a128-4fd093eea642" :question/math-academy-id "q-25563" :question/problem "If ${log}_{36}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then what is $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $36,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x} & ={36}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={36}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085d1057-ff5e-5bfd-84ae-c6c267e09ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "701" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525226} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca4cf8a-ca07-5e32-99ff-1650a91e4c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "753" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525398} {:answer/id #uuid "ae15ec67-0b24-5512-880c-5ce8aff444ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525647} {:answer/id #uuid "e417d031-50a9-529b-8959-d40b2f8ee096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "729" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525771} {:answer/id #uuid "fadffd7b-da29-5db1-a407-909c2c22fc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "705" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525828} :answer-field/id #uuid "f097babc-302d-5abc-8e66-b8adbc6bdc3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a40236b-6da0-50ec-9ab9-4d1b2b103597" :question/math-academy-id "q-100612" :question/problem "If $\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3}=2$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3} & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}=x+24.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+24 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x+24 & =729 \\\\ x & =705.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1852df64-07e6-553a-9474-3688514f45f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525255} {:answer/id #uuid "4b094227-bc65-5485-9af0-535c85dfc957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525392} {:answer/id #uuid "4df98584-3d9c-5eac-bf56-d2ad321a9f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525403} {:answer/id #uuid "c523c99c-0943-5d50-866c-50af7a52bf5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525706} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8fb22c-c2d5-5de9-9ec3-5e0357b772f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525403} :answer-field/id #uuid "950df731-b35d-5f11-87f2-48d0011cad1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c20f239-ec4b-5508-8032-829af339c105" :question/math-academy-id "q-138841" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}({sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b737d1d-48cd-5a7e-b7bb-f5db76229dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}+3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525263} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb31870-9443-5984-b41f-efc5c4e135b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6c56b3c3-015d-5836-a8b0-fb20b975ad9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-347204" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c91e196377aad64db49224cb5e38c1c8336171649708ec78b9cb80ebe6803128.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+\\frac{(-\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}+\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-(-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}+3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8caffdc-7547-5a6e-a21b-aebd9ee316b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525633} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8bb75e-79be-5239-bdbe-829119163778" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "85abb599-4be2-577d-9d0b-59402cc7e2bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-348918" :question/problem "![](images/28/28be65f566e93427105af6c87c46fa51f1a473363247f35a4aa481b93dc70090.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ The area of the region is\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))={sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{(x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right))-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-1)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =3(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eaaba8c-471d-518c-a335-8c5cde9e9c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3293" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525360} {:answer/id #uuid "65eaf07e-bdf4-53cf-8ef4-ced6c5a60ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3893" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525466} {:answer/id #uuid "6ade2813-1c5a-5d24-911e-219441ba006c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525480} {:answer/id #uuid "732c204b-256d-58ac-94af-cde5a02cfff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2398" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525498} {:answer/id #uuid "9323dee0-34d8-5539-a4fe-1ad547c359d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2404" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525578} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b29739-e711-5183-b197-e37019643638" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9324f898-b949-55cf-ad73-91b42fed29fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-100563" :question/problem "If ${log}_{7}⁡(a-3)=4$, then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(a-3) & =4 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)} & ={7}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)}=a-3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-3 & ={7}^{4} \\\\ a-3 & =2401 \\\\ a & =2404.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7cfc336-57d9-5dec-907f-338b4a0deb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-\\frac{3}{\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525780} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8fbe119-4292-551a-8361-7221b4390c27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ada1f0d5-c981-59b4-bce0-235ff7c6a380" :question/math-academy-id "q-340112" :question/problem "Given the function $y=-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d69e3c-ff46-5462-b1dc-f842432f8c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525283} {:answer/id #uuid "30fa87c3-0fc0-5d04-8956-098ecc804898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525323} {:answer/id #uuid "69591349-2fae-5161-83b5-ca206ebd1db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525473} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5cf105-98f1-5ff9-965a-e6cdd361c8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525786} {:answer/id #uuid "ff49e680-ff34-505a-abda-dc9a48e3fc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525323} :answer-field/id #uuid "f060ff52-77ea-5902-887f-7649c45ec084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d546754a-f994-5144-a7ef-1e397b7589a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79381" :question/problem "If $3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)=6$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)}=x+2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =25 \\\\ x & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf04a0a-0bd8-5d2e-812d-5432f2df6590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{5}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525444} {:answer/id #uuid "685cda01-0888-5a6f-a987-bf7af628d202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525470} {:answer/id #uuid "6907ae6b-ecfe-5991-afbd-41207f7fe49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525472} {:answer/id #uuid "aad87334-dafd-5198-b9f8-50bb56d246b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{x}+\\frac{5}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525638} {:answer/id #uuid "ff183bf4-fc17-5f4a-9084-a9260a322616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525843}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525444} :answer-field/id #uuid "58bb8e68-b3ea-5304-b0a6-2288d1ff79da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d56133b4-1a38-5a0f-927e-e0aa0995c936" :question/math-academy-id "q-105259" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={3}^{x}-5{log}_{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}-5{log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{5}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cf3a7a-0952-5273-a018-e34afb0c062f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{log}_{2}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525366} {:answer/id #uuid "740b37ab-4b45-560a-8a65-81214e71c672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{log}_{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525501} {:answer/id #uuid "b166fa7f-a0e9-5cce-a229-cb09701c3928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{log}_{2}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525657} {:answer/id #uuid "cb18bdf1-3016-5423-9e6a-39b690cae75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{log}_{2}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525713} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb3c03b-0c40-5e43-a2a1-5a1e290687a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{log}_{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525721} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde51c75-87d1-50de-8a6d-8f61f9b9669e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2bb36c5-0fc9-5c1b-9d34-184a6674cc87" :question/math-academy-id "q-66634" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $7{log}_{2}⁡3-{log}_{2}⁡3?$" :question/worked-solution "Both logarithmic terms have a base of $2$ and an argument of $3.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{log}_{2}⁡3-{log}_{2}⁡3 & =(7-1){log}_{2}⁡3=6{log}_{2}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098dba23-b7da-562c-9c53-d2da5ac0e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "133" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525228} {:answer/id #uuid "125fe57b-d15c-5087-b2fa-3310763c518c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525245} {:answer/id #uuid "3eec9cbe-47d1-5073-9d62-2079bb3fe062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525362} {:answer/id #uuid "680fe524-1b0c-58a3-9d0c-3b29c4e5eede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "103" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525469} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4ba5b7-2c58-52f0-a9a1-7fba8055f1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525245} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b9c11fc-eb88-5bce-941d-5218b2921e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fa2d3b27-4298-5b73-9f1c-ae2a2ae60004" :question/math-academy-id "q-100565" :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(a-10)=3$, then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(a-10) & =3 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)} & ={5}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)}=a-10.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-10 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ a-10 & =125 \\\\ a & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526497} {:answer/id #uuid "053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526512} {:answer/id #uuid "45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526636} {:answer/id #uuid "4760e0af-7c17-59e4-9428-b7e2b3770e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526641} {:answer/id #uuid "7e75a625-c85d-5866-9506-f4004e7988a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526753} {:answer/id #uuid "a6923305-e2d1-5771-8647-2ea06b7c3f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526821} {:answer/id #uuid "c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526869} {:answer/id #uuid "d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526905} {:answer/id #uuid "dd831f4c-c232-5259-91e3-6a8e05498594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376b6d35-c19b-56a0-93ca-9e64900695ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526607} {:answer/id #uuid "4d63a9d4-f48b-551f-a330-cb4773261398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526660} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd56c0-cc2d-5c47-9c19-0fd322aee1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526683} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6fbe7f-9b92-59d9-8aed-d5539feec274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526755} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b9d20a-a2bf-5378-921c-e265c88f998e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526755} :answer-field/id #uuid "905b588f-0f57-5923-a81f-afb037b87739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33021c77-54f7-572b-82bd-06940f46518d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102691" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526497} {:answer/id #uuid "4760e0af-7c17-59e4-9428-b7e2b3770e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526641} {:answer/id #uuid "7e75a625-c85d-5866-9506-f4004e7988a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526753} {:answer/id #uuid "a6923305-e2d1-5771-8647-2ea06b7c3f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526821} {:answer/id #uuid "dd831f4c-c232-5259-91e3-6a8e05498594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526497} :answer-field/id #uuid "118ed423-ef91-5a50-ac0b-83fc741088e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "431dc348-674b-5da2-8e50-c9106a497552" :question/math-academy-id "q-159349" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={log}_{2}⁡(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+(1){y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526512} {:answer/id #uuid "45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526636} {:answer/id #uuid "53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526673} {:answer/id #uuid "c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526869} {:answer/id #uuid "d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526905} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/math-academy-id "q-138973" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa9602-f624-59b4-90e5-e52ad4429899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527325} :answer-field/id #uuid "557ab1f0-6633-5399-9cab-8d702abcc76a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ac560de-8de4-5482-a34a-4c1b0b94d0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-266630" :question/problem "$6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $sec$  is the reciprocal of $cos,$  $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin$  and $cot$  is the reciprocal of $tan.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}}.\n$$\n\nThe angle ${60}^{∘}$ and ${30}^{∘}$ are special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘} &= 6\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}+\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ &= 12+6+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0091f92f-76bd-5455-b7bb-90620581c533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527308} {:answer/id #uuid "4faca5a4-1b5a-5d74-9628-f54841efcefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527461} {:answer/id #uuid "7fbb2c06-77c6-5ca1-ad81-f51fd4aa46f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527553} {:answer/id #uuid "93f7fcfc-7f4c-5178-9ad6-2289d08312cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527601} {:answer/id #uuid "97a20052-f025-5e71-8ba6-be7ff103ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527613} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3ee0695-4508-5b1d-af93-79c7b38f9ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7bdb766f-cf3d-53c5-a841-7b6efda706a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-28653" :question/problem "$5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "The angle ${45}^{∘}$ is special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2},$ ${cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}$ and  ${tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘} &= 5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}+3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}-7(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2} \\\\ &= 5{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-7(1)^{2} \\\\ &= 8{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-7 \\\\ &= 8\\left(\\frac{2}{4}\\right)-7 \\\\ &= 4-7 \\\\ &= -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07656df9-a2d3-55b7-968e-e7475835e5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527321} {:answer/id #uuid "2710d395-2b8f-5a9c-8242-b8a14e7e70c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527396} {:answer/id #uuid "27f4d9d3-7e96-5c12-b5a3-f9372b87c8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527398} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fa43a8-904c-5b53-a2e7-0be8f2877828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527676} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4bdf40-3643-5dc6-b993-f92752eaaf5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527398} :answer-field/id #uuid "faa2ee71-4e17-514a-a333-0d36f72cde02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cef928b3-a5b9-5b14-83bc-17bf537996f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96454" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & ={sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2) \\\\ {tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & =1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "2a62d9ca-70cf-5bb0-a81d-b44818d65dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528407} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9f0a47-5957-5b08-aa87-f0e9f9dae695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528409} {:answer/id #uuid "3baf6327-0772-5626-bef1-153ab2509f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528452} {:answer/id #uuid "604b5f92-c114-511e-a74d-0dc1e2510766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528528} {:answer/id #uuid "690e9529-0386-51ce-be2d-a3189387b6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528551} {:answer/id #uuid "b065bdc2-bb70-5ec7-bdfa-649ce2e20ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528728} {:answer/id #uuid "b309ec04-8226-537c-b8f0-96608e7c2284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528738} {:answer/id #uuid "d575583a-8ede-5730-b7c0-283f632cd4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528833} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f01578-2351-567b-8c59-f1220aaa327e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c8c1d5-6245-5e5d-af86-ae89e6d91c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{9\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528426} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f185ea2-90bf-5f97-91ff-946b826ac36e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ba92fa4-3450-58d7-9f90-59493b716e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-352331" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3f2350ba3651e928c65e1a78908a8f8e93cb690585a3fa16b723d1cf38a5024.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{9}{2}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9}{2}\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{8}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+4) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{9\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "773ffc42-e2cb-572a-9030-e9a10ba881ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +24}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528576} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bcdf62-382b-5121-8803-109ccdad8245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528689} {:answer/id #uuid "a5649c91-b6ce-5e11-a61e-4473ca3b44bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi -9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528700} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b16dc0-ef14-5998-aff3-022fa9ad94bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +72}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528711} {:answer/id #uuid "b1addd5e-b739-5991-8970-6b78acd4e746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528732} :answer-field/id #uuid "870af8e4-9463-5142-8458-8ef364ddaa4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c3c2375-fac6-5465-bbbf-42c39eaebb70" :question/math-academy-id "q-123912" :question/problem "![](images/98/98bc6f2ea4d71368226f0f784d629e98949e07005bc08158206de35a87bea520.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=2\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(9-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{8}-9+\\frac{9}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e8e637-7057-5738-bf7e-37e493294f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528302} {:answer/id #uuid "15e653f1-5de1-53ea-abbc-55ef743af9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528354} {:answer/id #uuid "41ba3cc1-a57c-5405-a9a7-4fc4022e386b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528470} {:answer/id #uuid "4c18dc99-1f3e-572b-b474-4613ba1ccc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528490} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5b425b-9a81-5998-a220-805626423f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528490} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a913a76-11e5-57cb-aec7-0eda0a258d42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c7bf914-2de6-57ee-85dd-b40dcc8d6cfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-9640" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7ca646111a7708da96fa1bea8aca056abf0c45f9a4295e2beb27181d704aa814.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{8},\n$$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}-\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a62d9ca-70cf-5bb0-a81d-b44818d65dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528407} {:answer/id #uuid "3baf6327-0772-5626-bef1-153ab2509f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528452} {:answer/id #uuid "604b5f92-c114-511e-a74d-0dc1e2510766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528528} {:answer/id #uuid "d575583a-8ede-5730-b7c0-283f632cd4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528833} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f01578-2351-567b-8c59-f1220aaa327e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528407} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2074a7-cded-5820-bd6c-23291aa6aaef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "529c609b-d364-5c28-a5a9-2026822f240a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79626" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded by graph of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "622acefd-6fc4-5289-acf9-eacb640ce3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\frac{13\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528532} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1fe94c-bcca-5ef1-b698-9fc1476d4a23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63188c95-9ec2-54ce-9302-58600c7d5645" :question/math-academy-id "q-352326" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ab46adacda52da11a07c783905161c6a6c8ebadb77423d7c70acdb17321a50e.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+\\frac{5(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{2}+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{13}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13}{2}\\theta -\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13\\pi }{4}+6) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{13\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi +24}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460f98e6-2dad-5164-97ed-ca84fd3c06be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528475} {:answer/id #uuid "771fcce6-ddaf-54b3-a739-a6390529f5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528575} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e72018-e6e3-592b-880f-a6d48f3ebdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528734} {:answer/id #uuid "b294df79-b64d-5dcb-8a58-b77dd62b8429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528736} {:answer/id #uuid "e6233b81-6983-586a-81cd-f71c40f0288b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528734} :answer-field/id #uuid "4863b9c5-7c52-590c-ac4a-f95a2e691add" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "692ede8e-6241-512f-89fd-e5f23e8be1b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-9641" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed702f9d982d599fc0f175dc7d7dd022aeefc3f5463aade2a9db71313faf4866.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\pi -0)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0+\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1ffe733-727d-5ca8-9de6-2f0a4144bd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528896} :answer-field/id #uuid "451b0468-1ab6-5622-abf5-d01927a565b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "858e5587-42fe-5f2f-94b7-94758f1a2164" :question/math-academy-id "q-348315" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f33fde62793530cfe8970a9fea690fc71036312483ea617088a8d0a663d5133a.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\theta +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf4aa39e-778f-58ff-8308-65c8627a83b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528811} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5704968-f186-54d5-a951-55effb0d3ff9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "85d4b81f-f257-5ee9-9249-f54ff8b0fda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-352298" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =2\\pi +0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0b39530-de5a-5c77-986a-201fc9682d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528730} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7cde26-15d1-5ea3-9851-e82954572829" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86ea583e-6e3e-5bb4-93ab-14ccfe18681c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352297" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0fa857c-1d3e-5cf2-baa1-2ef2c08c36f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi}{3}+14\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528851} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea42cbea-59a6-5df3-b3cd-da64d32d78f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "acc4627b-a75b-5710-8130-818c19bd3eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-352321" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +8(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta +24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+12(\\sqrt{3})+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+14\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9f0a47-5957-5b08-aa87-f0e9f9dae695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528409} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1413a7-a029-529d-a86f-b8d38b5b7dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528456} {:answer/id #uuid "690e9529-0386-51ce-be2d-a3189387b6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528551} {:answer/id #uuid "b065bdc2-bb70-5ec7-bdfa-649ce2e20ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528728} {:answer/id #uuid "b309ec04-8226-537c-b8f0-96608e7c2284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528728} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a7f30de-c83e-5e20-b1c0-29f77397e42d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9bf3624-c382-5070-9fe1-d9a42b576ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79793" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by the graph of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\,$ for $\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf30a48-c9e1-56ae-a6e7-d1c98a8ff3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529217} {:answer/id #uuid "c73dcc5f-2dfd-545e-8686-1192c87baf69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529217} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c836807-d3ab-5654-828e-8cfc6899a48e" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3352e3a8-a31e-5489-b928-3600c98b31be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529215} {:answer/id #uuid "7eea3389-ae38-5700-acdf-02838bc43de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233} :answer-field/id #uuid "7319f1b7-7a37-5090-bc1d-79d9eee3c8d9" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59fb7559-abec-5a5a-8f38-66e0e05169a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529221} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1215a-893f-52eb-9b40-de5e574e65b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529249} :answer-field/id #uuid "885ed53f-bd5b-5462-9002-72b92ffeea39" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0f86b247-8f09-549d-8811-1a1520e150f7" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\sqrt{2})+1(- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{20}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$Q$ is a singular matrix” and $det(Q)=0$ are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "623acce1-fff7-50e6-838b-7b0788c7f4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529223} {:answer/id #uuid "6f989a4f-56c1-50c2-8a6c-3fc3965f7ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529223} :answer-field/id #uuid "25dfb153-bf6c-5bc8-81b3-3f8c8e55fd75" :answer-field/key "$R$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ea2a503-d065-55b2-8fd1-d25f4f09f9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529227} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8e8b31-aab5-5ebf-9a6d-c0ce4e4dec83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529227} :answer-field/id #uuid "7227b94e-e860-5cf3-bd06-90e04b0b9867" :answer-field/key "$R$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0849452b-707f-5059-a47a-da630beb226a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529208} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6b1114-f6aa-5ce4-b2fd-bd99b174c462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529208} :answer-field/id #uuid "e852e547-0a05-5456-8b75-a7942f7d0613" :answer-field/key "$det(R)=\\frac{4}{5} - \\sqrt{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2eb19151-d434-5c12-a703-9c37eed13782" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{4})+(- \\sqrt{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} \\\\  & =1- \\frac{7}{10}- \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{13}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(R)=- \\frac{13}{10}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$R$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bbf7977-41f6-5bb2-bc84-9b91b4aa2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529239} {:answer/id #uuid "b655c0ce-1985-5548-8848-5fb9f83b43c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529239} :answer-field/id #uuid "57424796-2385-5961-a89c-2d74ee068fcd" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f10ee21-6b89-5e0f-a04a-e49bcfa4c907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529218} {:answer/id #uuid "fe16b8a0-13d3-5554-8edd-ff60470da1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529255} :answer-field/id #uuid "283cd5ed-404d-513a-8b29-74966f63a5de" :answer-field/key "$D$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bbf7977-41f6-5bb2-bc84-9b91b4aa2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529239} {:answer/id #uuid "b655c0ce-1985-5548-8848-5fb9f83b43c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529239} :answer-field/id #uuid "57424796-2385-5961-a89c-2d74ee068fcd" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "670467a6-e81c-5c99-be97-28f089cc9d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529225} {:answer/id #uuid "db4c2f01-affa-593d-9e86-c0393439ae7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529247} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad6dc2-1046-5c88-93da-58012356da43" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "58253580-dd5c-51ba-b42f-aea83256773d" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\frac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{25}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{3}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{6}{25}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87861eb3-5f89-51bb-a83b-a2a2f34426b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529234} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3191b2-823c-5ff2-9063-c65501bb4dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529252} :answer-field/id #uuid "0187374c-e9df-5d8a-b2e7-160b085f7e8a" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529206} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e9331ae-3672-5960-a137-fec2b5ed94e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529222} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e9128-09e7-5dbd-8651-62e753136ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0b79b6-ad2c-596a-b49a-2f831ec2b99d" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f014eef-cc68-5016-a321-04739726f29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529237} {:answer/id #uuid "9792a9f2-799d-58ba-9247-36ec66135b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529238} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28fbb07-9011-5ca0-97ac-fffd6b36f2ee" :answer-field/key "$D$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "71433954-37ca-5812-b277-05c8f0f0a4e4" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8})+(-1)(- \\frac{7}{5}+\\frac{21}{20})+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}+(-1)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{20}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{40}+\\frac{7}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$ are true."} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3352e3a8-a31e-5489-b928-3600c98b31be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529215} {:answer/id #uuid "7eea3389-ae38-5700-acdf-02838bc43de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233} :answer-field/id #uuid "7319f1b7-7a37-5090-bc1d-79d9eee3c8d9" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e9331ae-3672-5960-a137-fec2b5ed94e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529222} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e9128-09e7-5dbd-8651-62e753136ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0b79b6-ad2c-596a-b49a-2f831ec2b99d" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e93d6b-04f6-5d4f-b5b6-d410c07da27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529210} {:answer/id #uuid "ac318550-2f86-5de9-9b06-3ca0446a83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529210} :answer-field/id #uuid "c469465a-d12b-5568-8d4c-567bbab54c8b" :answer-field/key "$det(N)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0849452b-707f-5059-a47a-da630beb226a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529208} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6b1114-f6aa-5ce4-b2fd-bd99b174c462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529208} :answer-field/id #uuid "e852e547-0a05-5456-8b75-a7942f7d0613" :answer-field/key "$det(R)=\\frac{4}{5} - \\sqrt{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e93d6b-04f6-5d4f-b5b6-d410c07da27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529210} {:answer/id #uuid "ac318550-2f86-5de9-9b06-3ca0446a83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529210} :answer-field/id #uuid "c469465a-d12b-5568-8d4c-567bbab54c8b" :answer-field/key "$det(N)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48843694-108e-5d14-bd01-367c88bbda7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529216} {:answer/id #uuid "66105f24-eeac-5dfa-906a-b29c7b525374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529216} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa6403a-374c-56a6-9e6e-eff87afdb677" :answer-field/key "$N$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529246} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0634ee5a-1fe4-5615-bb1f-de6045b61f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529207} {:answer/id #uuid "25008e01-79c7-58b7-aeed-e63a6324cfd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529211} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd12a97e-9c51-5ed4-8b7d-226953a0be67" :answer-field/key "$N$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f4817fa1-7160-5d08-adc7-564c3155c399" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2})+(-1)(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+2 \\sqrt{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{10 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{9}- \\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$N$ is a singular matrix” and $det(N)=0$ are true."} {:answer/id #uuid "1460b532-0073-5daa-be76-25b294faf196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529619} {:answer/id #uuid "584cf8d9-b744-520d-9318-49ab115da6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529792} {:answer/id #uuid "735f35d2-93c2-528a-acc9-d6a1cf48503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} {:answer/id #uuid "986b58bd-bf64-5ce6-bc2b-489c0d1ff9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1+{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529953} {:answer/id #uuid "e24b7e7c-e405-5b07-bff9-4342ddf9f7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530145} {:answer/id #uuid "f2877592-1c01-5d19-a3fc-8b935ae3acd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1460b532-0073-5daa-be76-25b294faf196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529619} {:answer/id #uuid "584cf8d9-b744-520d-9318-49ab115da6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529792} {:answer/id #uuid "986b58bd-bf64-5ce6-bc2b-489c0d1ff9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1+{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529953} {:answer/id #uuid "e24b7e7c-e405-5b07-bff9-4342ddf9f7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530145} {:answer/id #uuid "f2877592-1c01-5d19-a3fc-8b935ae3acd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529619} :answer-field/id #uuid "c65d02c3-90be-58e3-90b9-927e6d39f12a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1645cfbe-2ae9-5b4a-bc43-086add6513c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-83431" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${cos}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of ${cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we multiply the expansion by itself, as follows:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n- There is a single constant term, given by\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\n- There are two ${x}^{2}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- There are three ${x}^{4}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})\\cdot 1+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first three nonzero terms of the expansion are\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6878ad2d-c3f6-5472-b932-d7a4364f9bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{4}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529841} {:answer/id #uuid "735f35d2-93c2-528a-acc9-d6a1cf48503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} {:answer/id #uuid "8f18c374-0ec5-5b5a-8aa2-bf73f6a415be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{2}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4866bd0-791b-5423-befb-168b1ad8cf39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530030} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c2df3-9d88-53e0-973d-199d14e0ed36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{4}{2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4a256d7-74ef-5f59-aa17-d233ba137369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5349d9de-8c6d-5416-a022-6878977b6004" :question/math-academy-id "q-139057" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y={2}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15a0040-721e-5583-80c4-6b4b6d161036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi(2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b596d95-5abf-5a25-8619-bd46ab687c7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "95891d65-20e2-5732-9bd4-e52bbf49b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352088" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "138e119b-9f29-5728-b024-f296e1407436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530898} {:answer/id #uuid "25efba17-6780-5d23-af19-6462827fe365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530950} {:answer/id #uuid "37b64633-9200-5ac4-9020-49356273a990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530986} {:answer/id #uuid "4fab1f17-f040-5e8d-ba53-5a0cfda68a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531036} {:answer/id #uuid "693cf72a-54a9-5ffe-b86a-e3d60d8e9126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531097} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cf6697-f373-540e-8e34-c5fc49f71fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531230} {:answer/id #uuid "bb77564e-76df-5e96-b697-013fee021596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531261} {:answer/id #uuid "ee47508c-c5ac-50f6-8486-05ab49b69007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138e119b-9f29-5728-b024-f296e1407436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530898} {:answer/id #uuid "4c63f797-f964-543b-8948-8fa3ef071043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531027} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6ad79e-00af-5e1d-b171-f99e8a87b59c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531103} {:answer/id #uuid "a24f9d67-8d4f-5f04-a33c-d625d9ff53b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531215} {:answer/id #uuid "ee47508c-c5ac-50f6-8486-05ab49b69007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531346} :answer-field/id #uuid "770387f5-8ab3-56b6-9ab8-582f315d1f1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1bd5daf3-8ee2-5ee2-ae53-b882ce969a04" :question/math-academy-id "q-9541" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r={3}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{3}^{2\\theta }+{3}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡3){3}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{3}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{\\pi }-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d37de0-fa9a-528c-821a-0e1cbd836c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530971} {:answer/id #uuid "95163aca-07b5-596d-b2dd-ec7271cff2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531185} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7e80c1-d411-5f78-8fa9-5b2e23fb8afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531197} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6fc084-138a-52e5-98fc-f3ff4df2d844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531205} {:answer/id #uuid "b1129f7d-4d42-5274-bad5-368dc2993492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530971} :answer-field/id #uuid "55b2d835-40d6-572d-8824-c8db3cfc4a3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e4f6268-3e58-5684-9bea-f874fd53aaf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-138069" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b64633-9200-5ac4-9020-49356273a990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530986} {:answer/id #uuid "4fab1f17-f040-5e8d-ba53-5a0cfda68a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531036} {:answer/id #uuid "693cf72a-54a9-5ffe-b86a-e3d60d8e9126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531097} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cf6697-f373-540e-8e34-c5fc49f71fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531230} {:answer/id #uuid "bb77564e-76df-5e96-b697-013fee021596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531036} :answer-field/id #uuid "632462c4-14ed-5c49-b96d-6314fe36a641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42788b85-30ff-5f36-a9fd-86e424b97617" :question/math-academy-id "q-138980" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a09810-6e1e-5a7f-a496-a308672d8fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530902} {:answer/id #uuid "70d61664-e8b3-5d91-831f-2ef5a133d2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531115} {:answer/id #uuid "75c79860-2158-5faa-be6a-5b9aa2eb7054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531123} {:answer/id #uuid "931cc387-4c8e-5435-8982-a4705188d119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531181} {:answer/id #uuid "ad39fb78-7dc8-5c7f-b6cd-c9732f59d8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531238} :answer-field/id #uuid "452ad711-f8a6-5c7b-b980-01feac5bbbde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1c1460f-c5a2-5e99-b7f7-17db73e707c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-103828" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901a723b-e6e5-572f-9b48-3f4f2a4dea43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531175} :answer-field/id #uuid "3487b8fc-2024-52a0-8191-9d6c368ac790" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9290c4e-ece3-5a58-aecc-bb62b4894116" :question/math-academy-id "q-353582" :question/problem "![](images/83/83813bff1ff86036e2773d9476150de619688daec9cae2623a555a43290cace3.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}3\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce61a52-02f8-52e3-833d-f0a747be827a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530920} {:answer/id #uuid "25efba17-6780-5d23-af19-6462827fe365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530950} {:answer/id #uuid "44549959-713e-5a87-987f-4262b4d5d3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\sqrt{2}}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531009} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7da443-446d-582f-b934-dcaf57f4a7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531287} {:answer/id #uuid "d25fbccb-dafe-5557-83e3-f260cee1b53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4caf7b-52fc-5e88-b891-82cc9a3a2cf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "da080066-6980-5e46-a310-b96dc7f7120c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9540" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1bf9d989f1fbaeda90325b924f2c9285cd4e37e1948ba63256258b0a84c7e44.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r={2}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={2}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({2}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{2}^{2\\theta }+{2}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2){2}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{2}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {2}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({2}^{\\pi /2}-{2}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "568dd8b3-6aad-5f74-8960-0ca1fcb085bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e381238e-91d4-5561-a74f-947fada01bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532419} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1dc858-f317-5a50-ac0a-63bd26d09cc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "568d7106-115a-5160-be7c-9ea466db4771" :question/math-academy-id "q-352091" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]}^{2}+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)=1$ and ${sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1={tan}^{2}⁡(t)$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{tan}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot |\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi [\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de248ef-eae0-53e3-95b5-fbeb5926dd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532063} {:answer/id #uuid "61bbc39f-0989-5704-9418-422cadcf22ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532151} {:answer/id #uuid "91cf89e0-afa8-591c-bc93-ee02e4978fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532265} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3eced4-b5ba-5283-97ad-820c6e28c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532339} {:answer/id #uuid "cbde0e1b-dda5-5de9-b0d5-ad1180eecbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532372} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d67ad9-bea8-55c4-a76f-57659a7c98ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef442bea-bb64-52fe-bd5a-d969e4e9ca72" :question/math-academy-id "q-123558" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c27ab9b0062fabfe1fdbfd43631bf1f143c0fa4ec80587519e9ce6c4e84e7b2b.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533264} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff80fb-f43a-5cf0-8e42-04896271cf0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533597} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbcdd0a-4848-5292-b557-339794df61df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f04a48-e3d9-5285-9be6-a47bfb7a3162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533221} {:answer/id #uuid "806ffe8b-ec3d-5752-b9c6-7e479c231b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533443} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7ddf96-482e-5261-9f56-9cabb5f0cd3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533600} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ac113d-a092-55a0-828c-3cce578364b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533443} :answer-field/id #uuid "047d2299-ba3b-5217-bd76-392b196edadf" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533773} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175e98b0-2179-5c38-aec4-02ea135fddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533192} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb78a1e-acbc-582f-9896-4a25b60643c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533330} {:answer/id #uuid "85fcf6d7-1b0b-5bf0-afee-87974db835f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533455} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4797d8-fe35-5b5b-b08e-1c83d6082952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533330} :answer-field/id #uuid "20fff794-00dd-50a8-9256-5d3d679c4c80" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa5012c-8bff-5182-bdc1-c3192d2fa42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533179} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a4e473-005c-5bad-b050-c349b4619ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533622} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe5eb6f-4d7a-5247-87cc-a29690e1564d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533644} {:answer/id #uuid "e959566f-e723-58b3-9ab2-a51a923ed3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533714} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e500e9-2419-5436-86f7-bd0983f12961" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a59e584-278d-59db-b6ea-7b7977de0f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "columns" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533159} {:answer/id #uuid "79c8042c-d474-5943-aed4-1c689601cb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "diagonals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533428} {:answer/id #uuid "b483bb15-790a-5e86-a64e-5ba317eecac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "first row" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533578} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4405cf-0772-5f28-9f99-4a05e4a8fb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "rows" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533159} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b3efd3-b7c3-58e8-b4b9-9e7263c863ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "20281150-3bf1-5302-a5be-765695a3b8d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336094" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of determinants." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by the properties of determinants."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65feaefc-9540-5e9e-aba0-1a7effe2a8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}56 & -14 \\\\ 42 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533378} {:answer/id #uuid "87069efe-45b4-5333-8a10-7ed32590e859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533459} {:answer/id #uuid "90d505d3-8405-52e7-b05e-824d062628e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 42 \\\\ -14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533488} {:answer/id #uuid "d975b5e6-4b40-557d-ac26-27b4762c7188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533671} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0899b4-0eb5-5f96-802e-1c29cc2efdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 21 \\\\ -7 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533671} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ec136b-c0f0-5734-828b-fc93c68cab5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "35662c2c-d0a2-5e1c-b319-cae7bd255ca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-265777" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2det(X)\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "395065b4-ace6-5290-9bf5-9ae34338d260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533272} {:answer/id #uuid "a7154eb4-bcc0-5f39-b2ae-a197b10dfcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533543} {:answer/id #uuid "d1260309-ef28-5201-b8ae-5cf3e6edba2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533649} {:answer/id #uuid "d548f9e6-0754-56f7-b875-8b2dee34bc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533659} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c5a65a-fc58-5834-95f8-09e9880a2408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533649} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4e1c8c-757e-5673-8cd0-b270c0c3c7e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" :question/math-academy-id "q-265773" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e68831-30d3-5adb-84ff-01959317c4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533149} {:answer/id #uuid "35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533264} {:answer/id #uuid "b62d29f0-effc-5844-9775-69af319ada0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533583} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff80fb-f43a-5cf0-8e42-04896271cf0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533597} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbcdd0a-4848-5292-b557-339794df61df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533264} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75b49fc-3a38-5d99-88f4-84100e658eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fe120378-20db-5fc9-9e0d-984daf992ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15703" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y={ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)(-{sec}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee433e2e-01a3-589b-be2a-e296c1083d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534249} :answer-field/id #uuid "1293797e-50e4-52c1-9d9c-b8e13430af35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e084855-b516-581f-ad22-fe3ead569b7b" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} & =8\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-7\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(2\\cdot 7-(-6)\\cdot 6)+(-7)((-6)\\cdot 7-(-6)\\cdot (-6))+(-2)((-6)\\cdot 6-2\\cdot (-6)) \\\\  & =8(14-(-36))+(-7)(-42-36)+(-2)(-36-(-12)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 50+(-7)\\cdot (-78)+(-2)\\cdot (-24) \\\\  & =400+546+48 \\\\  & =994\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef9004f-9673-5d56-b880-e9fd7d88fc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-218" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534224} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3b7366-e77f-5264-a6e0-bccb3caa1b1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "10196cf2-c98b-5fbc-8825-8aa283507d40" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+8\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(4\\cdot 6-8\\cdot (-5))+7(7\\cdot 6-8\\cdot 7)+8(7\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =6(24-(-40))+7(42-56)+8(-35-28) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 64+7\\cdot (-14)+8\\cdot (-63) \\\\  & =384-98-504 \\\\  & =-218\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89e81353-39ec-56ad-854e-d6993dbc1d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534235} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4677aae-c9f7-58da-8657-4eea065d1767" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53d9a6a2-222e-5a82-86be-d90af27fc93a" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-7)(7\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 5)+(-2)(7\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 8)+(-2)(7\\cdot 5-7\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =(-7)(-14-15)+(-2)(-14-24)+(-2)(35-56) \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-29)+(-2)\\cdot (-38)+(-2)\\cdot (-21) \\\\  & =203+76+42 \\\\  & =321\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc809fa4-652d-5ac8-974c-677581cdb740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "290" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534242} :answer-field/id #uuid "21e24fff-e059-56bd-a412-331cb6aac184" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e7f085d-8189-5198-aacc-e050d549ba30" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-8\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)(7\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot (-2))+(-8)(6\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 2)+(-3)(6\\cdot (-2)-7\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-2)(-28-6)+(-8)(-24-(-6))+(-3)(-12-14) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-34)+(-8)\\cdot (-18)+(-3)\\cdot (-26) \\\\  & =68+144+78 \\\\  & =290\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87f15d6-01c2-557f-a08c-16a5ceb7d64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534238} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b974bbe-ce42-568d-a217-2141b1101e14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b0697975-fcd3-5a6b-8bab-bac455d6d4bb" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{vmatrix} & =(-10)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -7 \\\\ -5 & -12\\end{vmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -12\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-10)(8\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot (-5))+5((-10)\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot (-10))+4((-10)\\cdot (-5)-8\\cdot (-10)) \\\\  & =(-10)(-96-35)+5(120-70)+4(50-(-80)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-131)+5\\cdot 50+4\\cdot 130 \\\\  & =1310+250+520 \\\\  & =2080\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc802cfd-5dcd-5ae0-9368-5cd44186f5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-163" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534247} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48cbd3e-4fec-5282-b2b0-e02d9a186f35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c662d088-8a5c-538a-9a26-b6ad00819d49" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{vmatrix} & =8\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -9 & -10\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -10\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(8\\cdot (-10)-(-4)\\cdot (-9))+(-5)(9\\cdot (-10)-(-4)\\cdot (-12))+5(9\\cdot (-9)-8\\cdot (-12)) \\\\  & =8(-80-36)+(-5)(-90-48)+5(-81-(-96)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-116)+(-5)\\cdot (-138)+5\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-928+690+75 \\\\  & =-163\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb6d018c-ba33-52b5-b3d7-aa5783cc6736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1022" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534252} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab18f7c-f40c-5379-93c7-a7f21c1e6cce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d662af0a-b4d4-5653-a75a-192a84ba886b" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 7 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+9\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7((-7)\\cdot 5-7\\cdot (-12))+7((-7)\\cdot 5-7\\cdot 12)+9((-7)\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot 12) \\\\  & =7(-35-(-84))+7(-35-84)+9(84-(-84)) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 49+7\\cdot (-119)+9\\cdot 168 \\\\  & =343-833+1512 \\\\  & =1022\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003496f1-3b51-50b5-b886-d6d3f3efcc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1839" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534215} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8282c0-97d4-5fb5-b9eb-de5bff7453bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f47007bd-5cbd-5756-890b-9b75cb25d807" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =(-9)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-9\\begin{vmatrix}12 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+8\\begin{vmatrix}12 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-9)(8\\cdot 8-(-5)\\cdot 5)+(-9)(12\\cdot 8-(-5)\\cdot 6)+8(12\\cdot 5-8\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =(-9)(64-(-25))+(-9)(96-(-30))+8(60-48) \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot 89+(-9)\\cdot 126+8\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-801-1134+96 \\\\  & =-1839\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "968eea0b-a6c3-5719-af51-829806f3263f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534597} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d52a07-3bbb-53b0-a0b0-9e85a23a30bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb7199e-65dd-5bd9-a79d-442fc232faa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "425" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534288} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1ae7c7-2dee-55e1-8b00-d4531dcc5579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "362" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534497} {:answer/id #uuid "cf067dd4-c8bc-5704-b97b-05088b2a487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "343" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534729} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cde56b-bba5-5c32-a43d-c79e4c3cdd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "371" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534782} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a6a46f-1b59-5281-b140-01c12fd66cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "471" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534807} :answer-field/id #uuid "25a7aacd-0ade-5f57-a8d3-34286e4a41cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "00bde4d7-2128-56ee-87f4-eb818e9cb4e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-100626" :question/problem "If $-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)=-48$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =-48 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =\\frac{-48}{-16} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)} & ={7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)}=x-128.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-128 & ={7}^{3} \\\\ x-128 & =343 \\\\ x & =471.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b00f42-9741-5f92-9339-a2a2a3e0a478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534275} {:answer/id #uuid "236555c7-ee6e-5be6-bd74-3a41e1426296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+{e}^{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534341} {:answer/id #uuid "443fcd61-e7db-54c9-9474-4eaf8ee080b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534394} {:answer/id #uuid "69837eb7-597e-5314-9105-3e7be9be3024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534482} {:answer/id #uuid "788fc73e-f6c0-5d4e-9563-fec8ac0a5b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534482} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0f87ed-2e6a-5c16-a67c-0ec64db6f138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "198d2d03-309c-5424-a889-3994cff0e1c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124837" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933e24f-9535-56e6-8bd8-463ca459b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534404} {:answer/id #uuid "54ae8c91-054f-56c1-8736-87af40558e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534433} {:answer/id #uuid "5efef0b8-d72d-5a49-aec0-94be999fd7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534457} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c386ae-4bfc-514a-a465-86fbebbe089e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} :answer-field/id #uuid "a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161328" :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af5318-875b-5713-b76b-c620f034eee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534308} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1d85e8-0bc7-546d-befe-bf2f6875ee46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534489} {:answer/id #uuid "6da12e8f-9a72-519d-aa8c-c280dab33816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534492} {:answer/id #uuid "73c89adb-6ad9-5a52-805c-8e998406f119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534515} {:answer/id #uuid "9e958518-516a-5fe5-b01c-caeda1f4b877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534621} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca90436-78d3-5958-bb30-5ff0939b26c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22b41189-2cbf-5d3c-9c30-272b9cf4d5e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104972" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2y & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=(-2)\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}=-12<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a0d4eb-fefb-5e1d-8b8b-53d270e9ff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534270} {:answer/id #uuid "29ed2488-96ec-539f-848b-3a7f0c77834e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534351} {:answer/id #uuid "5642d196-fd16-5650-b8ce-2b73794cc067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534439} {:answer/id #uuid "6afc72d8-8408-50d9-bca7-f953e40f36f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534485} {:answer/id #uuid "bae2108b-9562-5fdc-b2ed-c916f2046fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534485} :answer-field/id #uuid "15565180-33b3-52ce-9a0a-037fbe40360a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34262fe5-7916-5312-b653-79d60bb7bbe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124877" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1312b130-f076-56dc-a6ca-175281e64d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534305} {:answer/id #uuid "3090c984-5ed8-5035-ba5b-4c5acd082c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534360} {:answer/id #uuid "477bc9a6-005d-535c-b4ab-779587a92597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534401} {:answer/id #uuid "644dced5-34e6-5f21-8852-dbfa2b44f065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534466} {:answer/id #uuid "9f759471-c9f9-5e4b-a90e-babe10376f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534466} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c98d58f-3fec-5e1a-83a6-98106a7cf5a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bcab4b1-53e9-547b-81a4-3bf0881c104a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6891" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =16(1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22201dff-61e9-5435-a6a4-ec1001f8fba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534336} {:answer/id #uuid "968eea0b-a6c3-5719-af51-829806f3263f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534597} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e15da-7973-535e-a572-2e011cbd5da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534619} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d52a07-3bbb-53b0-a0b0-9e85a23a30bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534703} {:answer/id #uuid "efcdd02b-1a12-5093-8ddd-b0ba2d89d81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534336} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0258956-1ae0-545c-833f-e715647fb6c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f3c55a9-3e7c-5106-8273-98cee0c05243" :question/math-academy-id "q-124870" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,i+j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+({sin}^{2}⁡(2t)-{1}^{2})\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c630aef-e5a0-5435-bc55-02515d7f328e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534414} :answer-field/id #uuid "35fe18d9-346e-5613-a2e9-e0356df52875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96145c9c-17f6-5fc5-8bc3-7eda2b993795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^{\\pi/2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534595} :answer-field/id #uuid "f16ea21d-f232-5a8c-ae03-fff1dd1ddc4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "990e6c9c-e46f-5a33-8086-74712d90c327" :question/math-academy-id "q-351160" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13574205-8e5b-557d-b506-8f6098c78c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534307} {:answer/id #uuid "13e49d2a-fab6-5159-b1c0-5a9b594d165b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,-2)$ and a local maximum at $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534311} {:answer/id #uuid "5de0e24f-6b20-5c6e-ace0-a245f8aa51ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local minima at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534454} {:answer/id #uuid "7d90fa6d-adf2-5db3-b9b7-4dc01fdc1df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-2)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534535} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc001e1-be83-5588-9961-a4a046536923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,-2)$ a local maximum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534307} :answer-field/id #uuid "b199132b-7b0e-5674-af57-8442f38abdba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9f714ecf-c225-5f6f-850c-327baa1dc6f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81172" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4y-4x,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-4 & 2(xy-2)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ xy-2=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1,\\,\\,y=2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,-2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(-1,-2))=0\\cdot (-2)-(-4)^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,2)=det({f}^{″}(1,2))=0\\cdot 2-{4}^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35563bcf-877b-5a37-b29d-40f490c08f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$  has a saddle point at  at $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534367} {:answer/id #uuid "58b65e9f-4d99-5093-aa0f-4d2897f123b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum  at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534443} {:answer/id #uuid "87ca91c3-a26a-5635-add5-c073ea764a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum  at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534560} {:answer/id #uuid "c14d984f-72ff-59f8-bdd6-8c050d73fad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum  at $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534697} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ae0c36-9a90-5251-835e-e8a464a23a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum  at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534560} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7fa629-9baf-529b-99af-39063b4618f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac06f66c-0484-5c24-8d99-9d11ac2dbda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81048" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & e-{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=0 \\\\ e-{e}^{y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second  equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ne-{e}^{y}=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$   \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{y})=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))=(-4)\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=4e>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=-4<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207d4a62-e83c-5373-b357-2c60fca0d97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534330} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8cdc89-1beb-5512-8aa9-412f93b2dc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{18}(4-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534377} {:answer/id #uuid "49708f11-fe31-57c8-a150-b389b458ddef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534405} {:answer/id #uuid "8110d653-b1b2-5b3d-ad4c-5d3fcbdf4a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534544} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccd828c-0bdc-55ab-900f-109f767fab40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(9-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534615} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2b773f-820b-5bd4-95ab-10a27879c738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b34fc554-6133-5c96-98c9-93a11d4b7862" :question/math-academy-id "q-90834" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9-4t\\,dt \\\\  & =(9t-2{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(9-\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026784b9-3bc0-5798-b8b5-2ea62f5c5f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535297} {:answer/id #uuid "0cad4500-905a-5858-8c3f-a7b60b352b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535299} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb01dae-041f-5459-8b96-b3482e964c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535304} {:answer/id #uuid "8e67acde-888e-5817-a582-ac7742afc991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535324} {:answer/id #uuid "f8da406d-b2dc-5b56-b3c5-186cce25d980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535324} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f6bfad-4fb4-53b7-b7e6-318260e0f958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b24cc741-a0e1-52fd-84de-966d881c283d" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "490bf319-a19a-5705-874f-041fecb63bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535310} {:answer/id #uuid "56407af4-1fb7-53b1-86b8-13243e6095fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535314} {:answer/id #uuid "5d545206-ca9c-5b98-9471-863533f19aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535315} {:answer/id #uuid "71005229-4609-51a5-b252-aec7c9944620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535321} {:answer/id #uuid "b12e0871-185b-543d-bc3b-20afa90aead8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535321} :answer-field/id #uuid "51400ed4-9db0-5c43-83b2-f1b92948e695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6d7b368-d715-54c1-b3b0-bf00daf35574" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca1d623-6fb3-5bc0-b77b-f3d7f09c20b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535411} {:answer/id #uuid "22645390-9b31-5c8d-90c3-cc8622341dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535412} {:answer/id #uuid "5b318775-181f-578e-bc36-15bc47255b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 16 k z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535418} {:answer/id #uuid "7fabdf3d-f652-5a37-9dd1-4a79130e22e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 16 j z + 2 k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535420} {:answer/id #uuid "d45c9920-d159-56c8-b089-9363351e511d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16 j z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535423} :answer-field/id #uuid "01ad855e-80d9-5f79-b007-f3946d4f9a68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45a79900-6a44-513e-8ee7-36f0f63a0a43" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=8{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 8{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-16z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =16z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363634aa-e8d9-52c3-bf0e-3eda14caeb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14 j z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535413} {:answer/id #uuid "36ba0c31-5354-56ad-8374-df8a429ac86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 14 j z + 2 k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535414} {:answer/id #uuid "5056ab9e-f935-5e99-9c47-d970637a2506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535417} {:answer/id #uuid "789103f3-62e6-55d8-a195-73f4a7b6d1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 14 k z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535419} {:answer/id #uuid "8da18fb1-ae45-5312-9cdd-f5ffa291065c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535421}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535413} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01fff05-afdf-5fb6-a143-4ff309517c87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ab6255a-2668-509c-9456-b75fdf8371c2" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=7{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 7{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(7{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-14z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =14z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0538fe89-8dc3-52a1-ae06-27b01e1b4965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535445} {:answer/id #uuid "55d1530f-3fa2-5a08-8bcc-98aa2c0fd192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535569} {:answer/id #uuid "972bf55d-1dab-510d-9213-5a9e7ecde914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535672} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aca940-e7f6-56a7-a52f-f3fc13aea2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535784} {:answer/id #uuid "fc85b5f0-dcd6-58f0-b09d-4cc19f8fcf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0538fe89-8dc3-52a1-ae06-27b01e1b4965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535445} {:answer/id #uuid "55d1530f-3fa2-5a08-8bcc-98aa2c0fd192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535569} {:answer/id #uuid "972bf55d-1dab-510d-9213-5a9e7ecde914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535672} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aca940-e7f6-56a7-a52f-f3fc13aea2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535784} {:answer/id #uuid "fc85b5f0-dcd6-58f0-b09d-4cc19f8fcf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535445} :answer-field/id #uuid "58834ca7-0094-5902-ba0d-d4e72392d1de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6be7f528-8a2e-5e94-a08f-bb7c33bc8c26" :question/math-academy-id "q-125400" :question/problem "![](images/90/90980bb04eb7dd9906ec9a99ea3a1b7cfd9c00969bbd9b12c60fe0f80eb88840.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+2{y}^{2}=1$ and $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)$ for $y\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equation of our first curve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2{y}^{2} & =1\\,⟹\\,x=1-2{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/16/16ca1c96786eb8e7ef12608f70d03b34b679e63d34ef41135b4fd6801bcccc27.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/27/276dea7b2d9f50e3fe171cceb64c1c7a12f84cfb18bdbe242102802ad9790f49.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right))\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c6fb140bb3eaf4c49cba76af157fb46a568bc2dc24e4763facede62720f844.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(1-2{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[2{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right))}^{2}-{(2{y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0a54b6a7-f164-57c2-9d18-a35fbbab2114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536301} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb53bbb-85a3-5bf3-a469-97f9b6d515c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={sinh}^{2}⁡x+{cosh}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536355} {:answer/id #uuid "3076c98e-4b5f-5295-a960-def6c68868a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536357} {:answer/id #uuid "44e24fff-6edb-58dd-b6c8-7731cb263263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536388} {:answer/id #uuid "454d23bc-df8f-5fb0-9ac4-bbc97aefba45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536390} {:answer/id #uuid "49555273-5842-5a42-a1d3-66dd471f73a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536399} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3e4413-d9ee-532c-ae52-619bdac2ce6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536415} {:answer/id #uuid "5985a81f-c958-57c9-9d4b-af828a400130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536442} {:answer/id #uuid "790e9e20-c3f7-5319-bd5c-6ff1a8086450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536485} {:answer/id #uuid "951bf2e0-4ba8-5e1e-9dc3-e53cb361313b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536521} {:answer/id #uuid "9c651855-a335-5bd9-bf70-edb22dff84f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536536} {:answer/id #uuid "c9355724-e50d-561b-a5bc-0428aabebe3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={sinh}^{2}⁡x-{cosh}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536600} {:answer/id #uuid "d05d1a37-cbd0-5beb-a2c1-20973dce818c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536613} {:answer/id #uuid "ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037ba1f6-bab8-5644-a9f9-1d0997895160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536290} {:answer/id #uuid "05a68fca-8c3b-59b9-b009-99d530c3e80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536293} {:answer/id #uuid "3058b8fa-e6f3-593c-9d60-424339d2e4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536356} {:answer/id #uuid "44b1da90-8ca0-5d57-b272-29370ec5c888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536387} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8cb1aa-d2c5-5da0-96f0-51596aafbb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536356} :answer-field/id #uuid "33e72fa2-cc65-5b80-8195-b5d8928f38d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15a967e4-1f67-5afa-8031-c76c4fbb09cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96263" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to express the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{288}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536436} {:answer/id #uuid "84f09ef6-af8c-5278-868c-878e38fd43f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536501} {:answer/id #uuid "94da350d-d600-5d09-a5e8-a507d6bea83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{288}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536520} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8049a3-094e-56a7-a9bb-cd14ccc9b2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536540} {:answer/id #uuid "e4be4b9f-6f9d-51ae-b85e-355d75cde324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e082909-a6e2-55c2-a4a7-4878e06b14ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "390d417d-9926-5446-a159-d0e16ff521f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-138954" :question/problem "$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ada2e3d-0769-5b5a-85e6-218cbeec1c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536348} {:answer/id #uuid "4b33b710-8754-5317-ad8d-edc5c3f9305d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536402} {:answer/id #uuid "725bc856-b7c0-56dc-9518-bdc1f5428c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536473} {:answer/id #uuid "cc05201a-8232-5a04-8ce3-e3ac354a9f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536605} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d4553a-62e1-59c9-8854-5587cc1e9c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536473} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6fa46cf-d1f0-5fd4-a76c-c62db6438a9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50970995-9b99-52ed-ae6b-c20b930d578c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9537" :question/problem "![](images/50/5034bb902aadd151cc1a28764e054dbdd6a694ad653f50557e5fc208c8ec2342.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing $x=\\theta $ in the above identity, the integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98919bb4-2785-53dd-9466-f9d0288a76cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536528} :answer-field/id #uuid "408af374-202e-561f-90a6-1910a956b51e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f16bf74-fd62-5389-9de5-031fc7e1da5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352935" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2})^{2}}-\\frac{4}{({e}^{0}+{e}^{0})^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1/2+2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3076c98e-4b5f-5295-a960-def6c68868a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536357} {:answer/id #uuid "790e9e20-c3f7-5319-bd5c-6ff1a8086450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536485} {:answer/id #uuid "951bf2e0-4ba8-5e1e-9dc3-e53cb361313b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536521} {:answer/id #uuid "d05d1a37-cbd0-5beb-a2c1-20973dce818c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536613} {:answer/id #uuid "ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536658} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71d4fa5-c4c0-5c1e-bcc7-f6ccd2a9c51a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8879f5ec-f2c2-51de-a1ef-edc8baad4b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-96498" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity and rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{cosh}^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcdb4ad-14d5-5f0f-bf9e-41acc5a1d22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536329} :answer-field/id #uuid "241e61f4-57a8-5907-834c-7042fc78b039" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fa8b923-c944-5ab9-8f5d-a280f219b3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352916" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{sech}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a24661b-9376-5478-ba22-345b80fbea58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536531} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a779e2-c85d-5324-8778-80dd2097d44f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c94b3bf4-462a-5a97-a5df-84bb2aaaa4fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-346474" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{coth}^{2}⁡x{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{coth}^{2}⁡x{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}-{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-{3}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ae9a87-b6bd-553c-894c-fb7b9c46e795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536434} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b5624b-d189-5839-a384-dfb6d3457d12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d4fc19ee-fc09-58f6-b456-25b6d7c144cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352684" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2+{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{2}}{2+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d743ce-fa5c-525f-8823-17339a57955f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536298} {:answer/id #uuid "2a34da52-d120-5dcc-93a2-7d72465b9e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536347} {:answer/id #uuid "57f3fce6-97f1-54f3-9d05-c42e5d7d1c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536439} {:answer/id #uuid "79abcb54-0989-52b4-ab9d-387cf2aef302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536486} {:answer/id #uuid "7d80df60-47c8-599f-a716-04722fc55d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536439} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6dad051-9aa8-5418-a3f1-657be400f97d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da9958bc-8f70-50e7-9f99-fa451b16875b" :question/math-academy-id "q-100672" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{7}⁡(12)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{7}⁡(12)$ by changing the base $7$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=7,$ $X=12,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(12) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(12)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(12)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1.079\\,18}{0.845\\,10} \\\\  & ≈1.28\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b11b91-37c1-568c-9d1a-8a39694f67a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536369} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb4c32a-3d20-50c8-8f9e-4bb89fd9ad7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee29c156-d06d-572b-b229-09cda1e3a6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352915" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23897a01-3104-53af-ae5c-e8c22b2f28ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536335} {:answer/id #uuid "4cde3fc2-93fa-5b19-a0da-c6b974156e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536411} {:answer/id #uuid "7a564c7d-cc32-50b9-b0d1-9b64ae3f4ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536489} {:answer/id #uuid "87d2482c-4c9c-5bd0-9062-e840a46ef7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536505} {:answer/id #uuid "ed54b21e-804e-52cf-85a5-0db316e72703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536411} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f3278f-b153-5a85-8c35-53f0388ef9ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f38bc0fe-199e-5ced-bb28-b4453873ecf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-100679" :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{36}⁡(216)$ without using a calculator." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36,$ and $X=216.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡(216) & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡(216)}{{log}_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{3})}{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536346} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af736d1-f000-5f15-b738-443b7520d5c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f7758d7e-a5ba-50ee-a45d-ff68b3cc6226" :question/math-academy-id "q-352968" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}u\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{25}{9}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234cc714-ee95-51d3-9f60-96338562378e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536333} {:answer/id #uuid "4f31d1d3-9617-5495-b1c8-0bd332072ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536419} {:answer/id #uuid "529a7029-8e13-5a97-9918-a3b488d2c78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536424} {:answer/id #uuid "70329641-1279-5076-9f37-fb06772be638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536471} {:answer/id #uuid "fffb787f-ff79-5c83-b983-661bbcea077a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536333} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f4c241c-0bbe-5dbc-9897-1ee7bd328728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb7adc98-109e-57e5-9f19-72ef71c8b698" :question/math-academy-id "q-96265" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to express the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-{4}^{-1}}+\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e8e7d-e3db-5f7c-9347-4abfc31811d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f83f8a6-2631-55b9-a475-cf21a17cb881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{0}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537015} {:answer/id #uuid "9d691199-900d-5dbc-95ff-fd5da1703bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537043} {:answer/id #uuid "b727e49c-dd3f-5d45-b030-cf65baa91799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537053} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d59057-b6e1-58d4-8bca-71b7a0d1be8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536996} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb63a029-3502-5e90-a9fa-8ddfb79c7c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "00623118-ee32-53b0-8e64-30db43b56fa6" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30aa1de6-9825-58df-a0bb-9361d44a7568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536998} {:answer/id #uuid "32b99b29-cd14-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537001} {:answer/id #uuid "9605e9f5-cd80-5de4-9598-0eef1d67fdca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537039} {:answer/id #uuid "9c51e829-74a8-59f0-b024-a19f558413ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537042} {:answer/id #uuid "dff20a83-a22c-52be-b879-f991bfe4e845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537001} :answer-field/id #uuid "c677a795-89fc-56d8-8a2d-f295aa205e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3421c850-1e07-524f-a0e9-45c83fcec039" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321df476-4949-5c09-820e-9305d838bc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537000} {:answer/id #uuid "4e59d9d4-da68-5e58-aff1-c4c4a3337e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537014} {:answer/id #uuid "9a68c5dc-993d-52d2-9754-5b09d9106e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537041} {:answer/id #uuid "b4961f55-574a-53f6-91ac-244a2907cf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}^{-5}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537051} {:answer/id #uuid "c82cc82c-e96d-5c71-9bfd-49718a58969e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537000} :answer-field/id #uuid "346ca32c-f65c-5df0-9d38-5355dcbfffd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3595f3ec-49d1-5481-82e6-c23464cfa603" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30efa546-da45-5a01-977f-f55cd5e7ba61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536999} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc4f541-1cf6-594c-ba21-8359f5bbbc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{7}^{4}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537011} {:answer/id #uuid "7a324aba-9b63-5874-a2b1-733a50c7781b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537033} {:answer/id #uuid "81de05b4-209e-5bc0-9833-10807223dd7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537035} {:answer/id #uuid "e388a7a5-3cdc-52bc-8ddf-defa6c22aae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536999} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f21aac-e7ac-5168-a171-1b5b4f594e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "590500ab-ca5a-598f-be1f-2910c46fae9a" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=7.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da30d8b-b4d8-596a-8c80-6b14e0374096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536987} {:answer/id #uuid "0e208e2f-cf97-52a6-913e-893119de1c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536988} {:answer/id #uuid "20ec8899-9f98-5939-b1be-717c8a36b84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536994} {:answer/id #uuid "37eefddf-45ba-5950-8013-940c638fd35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{7}^{4}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537007} {:answer/id #uuid "9f831628-be52-5b60-9299-337a30d46fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536988} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7df119-1e10-5a12-b44e-c332dcfb8b0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "69ed0333-f7ae-5aef-adf8-ef1e138c3825" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,y\\ge 0,\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/80/80a919b28ae7ed559b06b34bd8512859794a7d6550970b56347e2deb653bf76d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=7.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e702de4-81fb-57f8-b700-52c7a2d5b037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}^{-1}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537029} {:answer/id #uuid "6eef909b-2ae7-54ad-8347-4b8012df300a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537030} {:answer/id #uuid "a4798cdd-ba94-5538-b8db-9acc2dd4aebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537046} {:answer/id #uuid "d2013466-8cd9-50cf-ab08-7466182070c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537061} {:answer/id #uuid "f189cf4d-34b4-53e8-8fda-d1dc11be310f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537030} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e63057c-fad2-57b0-ab28-4c0735601f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a4d1aae-ff57-5336-93e6-e2feb480e2e3" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,-1\\le z\\le x + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f8/f830c1469527c615d213ffcd11e472083c77d7e5fbc8c1577a85244fb5739fc4.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(-2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x + 5=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\,-1\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0525826c-a736-5227-9f0a-27dd1c50c845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-4}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536984} {:answer/id #uuid "202eb17e-51e1-5247-9d96-d74c13ad2578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-4}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1}\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536992} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfe631f-2545-5c41-af46-41c806290c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1}^{-4}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537060} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a68dfb-291a-522b-9d54-5f6067f6ec23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-4}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 