ion $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\,-4\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-4}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b0e90e7-4add-5e33-8547-4c25dae10c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-6}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537013} {:answer/id #uuid "51c76675-9e2c-59b9-8577-4dc096a8cdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}^{-6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537016} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3b8ee2-e524-5b41-8574-e188f91ae2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-6}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537020} {:answer/id #uuid "a36208fb-a2e1-572d-a6a9-8b8ccf4ee98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-6}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537045} {:answer/id #uuid "d3040689-2239-57e5-8500-1ac134938960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-6}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537045} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c179be8-68d6-5589-9fd5-03692660f907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4df6a09-850b-5ef6-8bf5-1f79b92be5fa" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,-6\\le z\\le x + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8b/8b16f7e69a4ce84b0b7c4597defd9e9f55593e50623f344f001d24518d59c483.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(-4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-6,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x + 5=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\,-6\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-6}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098d3517-e148-57fb-b802-0b2dbc349278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536985} {:answer/id #uuid "24acc4ff-e0b0-5c78-95ce-b81732e7ce1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536997} {:answer/id #uuid "3554a9cb-6c59-5f65-87eb-c96e1206a180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537005} {:answer/id #uuid "42573f2c-f0d7-5b0a-a697-3d745e18a680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537012} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ef5227-2542-5600-9c83-8758d74eee3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536997} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1ed8e4-2fa2-59d9-98df-3af8a6aba784" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ab4dd324-45c0-5df5-a923-9696a0511377" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,x\\ge 0,\\,-2\\le z\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/26/262fdb09ab8f6ee5de1a31cec2d238cb633ae2d5dfa2e99d80ed58d4cb849ec5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,-2\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a83957d-072f-56dd-967f-e6e3cad6b479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536986} {:answer/id #uuid "2156c48d-60f6-5a75-b7bd-532244308380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536995} {:answer/id #uuid "55850eab-9b9d-5708-b164-b99babf0d7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537017} {:answer/id #uuid "8d424d92-a14d-56fd-9646-621bf9980044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{2}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537036} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b1f40f-3ec1-5b86-a168-1bf950f81dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537065}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537017} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c67f654-3428-5c4c-b065-5f85356ec766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5e1ca18-2705-5bdb-9505-7d24e5965b22" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,y\\ge 0,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/80/80a919b28ae7ed559b06b34bd8512859794a7d6550970b56347e2deb653bf76d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f5725f-873a-5cb2-9d93-8dd091bb64fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536989} {:answer/id #uuid "341da02c-a537-571e-98d0-36040cb4c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{4}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537002} {:answer/id #uuid "55f37382-115f-5176-b367-f0f7693481cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537018} {:answer/id #uuid "95c46129-e371-5472-a8c0-39ca8937604f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{4}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537038} {:answer/id #uuid "dede804f-1f13-5287-811a-67eb0c9566f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{4}\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537002} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3833d31-d0a7-5f2a-ba09-40678b399d56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c80df8b3-485c-5c4b-a01f-65b076ecb41f" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 4\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/51/510a5ecd48459be9372c1a797d00cc78a167b7ebc0563063e4f1a1e187e32bd0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{4}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6797e6ea-0e50-5bcf-8670-1502efade88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537023} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1f36d1-2f5f-5b5d-b048-0f5b93961b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{6}^{4}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537026} {:answer/id #uuid "80b41028-1697-5a18-89f0-2ed59ee0559f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537034} {:answer/id #uuid "c719c5f0-f401-5da6-91a4-572901eb262b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537057} {:answer/id #uuid "fb13a566-d121-503a-8bc9-4bd42bb083df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537057} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d4af9d5-325d-5634-94af-ef6d75847f12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d488f663-9171-597d-b16b-2f0b7032086a" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,4\\le z\\le 6\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,4\\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "682a228b-b79d-5fcb-b15e-e67fdbaa8be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537024} {:answer/id #uuid "6af500dd-3fdb-56d1-8617-670cec01eb55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537028} {:answer/id #uuid "7301b34c-817c-56a3-8683-9aaaf6122853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537032} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cad40a-69b5-528c-86d1-d835cef665eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537069} {:answer/id #uuid "e796db0a-5eac-585e-902e-dc1ce488d7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{10}^{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537032} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c84f22-679b-57d9-9b4d-6d3035a36e20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "de103878-75c8-5f73-9ce2-41a2334b4eac" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 10\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d8/d8d5ba5f13ed464fa4e2b53fb2cd4fbdab92b3de371d3f7b535aeac8d0adb6d5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le 10\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15fafcac-b57c-5a49-aadc-f7ea513877d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536990} {:answer/id #uuid "208a838f-c0e2-54fe-b2e6-dc7e12747ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536993} {:answer/id #uuid "7084a39e-ec6e-5c74-b493-fb831dae5598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537031} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f22e85-6995-51aa-910a-ab0f4ae5632a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537054} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd1b677-e296-592e-b708-0635f034aaf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536993} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac38c3ec-fa0a-5519-a3eb-cc2f31de45a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eea1a108-64c8-59f3-971b-a2f53cbd0dc5" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}\\le 16,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5e/5ed28b1e7b49276cdb59da432056597e5992b8c4015ac8e9384f6bfe3f1fb7ba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2=r^{2} + 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1902df-1e1a-57b7-92b9-f031a1d54cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537289} {:answer/id #uuid "1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537294} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee8abd7-c8b0-5395-b1d2-db97d2fbe877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537329} {:answer/id #uuid "4697b16a-b954-5ce9-9fdc-2eb3d67c270a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537374} {:answer/id #uuid "46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537377} {:answer/id #uuid "4a79f07c-c8a1-549a-81cf-2a1164ba3d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537386} {:answer/id #uuid "656fd776-3d31-5e32-af9a-81ea6f3a0abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537431} {:answer/id #uuid "b867ae05-4768-52a1-a7ab-9ac578fa106a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537587} {:answer/id #uuid "b95fcc5a-b97c-570a-b6aa-9be9f9c37ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537589} {:answer/id #uuid "c58fc5b6-7692-51a1-abef-c6e7a022b6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x-4)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537612} {:answer/id #uuid "d99198fe-8160-5db2-9ef6-8b317c7d5096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x)}{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537662} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e17c2b-0b45-5f50-a9cb-32c3aec63676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537683} {:answer/id #uuid "e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537684} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ef0cbc-d0f2-5d87-96ee-056aed052a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d5f670-27f0-5761-9a2d-2b27183ed602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537235} {:answer/id #uuid "475499d3-6def-57f9-991e-f031b8de6556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537378} {:answer/id #uuid "6f28b0d3-c0bf-5fe7-bb38-6f7358465bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537444} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a81b59-7130-5b2b-ab0f-bb39e54adc0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537541} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5c2a17-7497-5332-bf7f-3b0c44e70ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537235} :answer-field/id #uuid "588c50be-27c2-58c4-af3b-6c4e820c32d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "007da683-6a06-5103-8ccf-d7e5e491284b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96324" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1-{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1-(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161bbede-61ba-5590-8f41-6ce72d807195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537273} {:answer/id #uuid "61a9fdab-c6b1-5177-a8d3-66e8ab9d8e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537422} {:answer/id #uuid "7577d28a-3de8-5ca6-81d4-c07e03712aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537463} {:answer/id #uuid "84da75f0-b8f7-5316-851b-9650d5ba1bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537488} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a9352d-3699-501f-8b2e-6e6f26adfaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537488} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a45213-b6de-5733-ae9b-b7816fa719f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02054798-e30b-5bba-b50a-1ea23b8ac8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-90426" :question/problem "The function $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)$ has an inverse function given by $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x+2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right) \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡((5y-1)/k)} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5y-1}{k} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ 5y-1 & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k \\\\ 5y & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k={3}^{2}=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "187fa676-ce69-5db4-bb19-2d5aded139fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537281} {:answer/id #uuid "3170b4a1-795f-56db-b544-2a4162816f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537336} {:answer/id #uuid "928181c8-6764-5052-ab91-3e3d8b3ae162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537505} {:answer/id #uuid "98505c70-07f9-5bd5-816f-3af8a4adc0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537517} {:answer/id #uuid "a2148279-dc40-5ab9-8eaa-86cdbddf62fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537534}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537517} :answer-field/id #uuid "81975dcf-378c-5d3d-b3d0-17574131c4e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "032608f7-6edc-5183-bb2d-4ede3e9c1645" :question/math-academy-id "q-96306" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({cosh}^{3}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cosh}^{3}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x({cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({3}^{4x-4})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537372} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d74a14-bc3d-5d29-8730-61d8c4d650f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" :question/math-academy-id "q-250505" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ded49d0-0325-529f-9419-2090bfab98aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537252} {:answer/id #uuid "8cebce10-a6c9-5515-93f2-fa7b8b926abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537497} {:answer/id #uuid "a4415743-8c2d-5029-baae-5ce1e6986656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537539} {:answer/id #uuid "c45f024d-5689-5adb-9c42-f9395e9c0cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537605} {:answer/id #uuid "f54d4a0c-06bf-5ce6-992a-91eb96c3cba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537734}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537605} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae402e9-62e8-57e1-bd92-31044e84dfce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1212fe46-96e8-51be-81fa-22f7749ff713" :question/math-academy-id "q-97017" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cosh}^{2}⁡2x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x={cosh}^{2}⁡2x+{sinh}^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cosh}^{2}⁡2x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cosh}^{2}⁡2x+{sinh}^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc2ab74-5abc-5136-83a2-898739e322fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537248} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2e8257-bfe6-5e3e-9807-69a66ed6f4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537313} {:answer/id #uuid "31ae5f0f-b924-5d5b-a2bd-1c8a5115b5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537337} {:answer/id #uuid "6961d82f-9d68-5c51-9119-5d2172d51b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537435} {:answer/id #uuid "b6be647a-f32e-58b3-b595-e16d7a2bc472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537583} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2943f3-7b0e-5a8d-9f07-ea94989cfcbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12bc09cc-29a3-5e9b-995b-e577844956a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97085" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[1-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(9-8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(9x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)]-[9\\cdot 0-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2({2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dba3b70-c074-5458-a323-b19ef1d35432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537522} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a1cb41-1b5b-5d2e-ab6e-6d8b55c026eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f2feab-92a8-538c-8fa0-8749a77b4343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537236} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5630480-4fe9-588e-bdf0-1ee399688150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d3e5918-3dfe-5112-97f6-219db70ddf3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537390} :answer-field/id #uuid "db14b8e4-522b-5e2d-8024-a70ac890758f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12efb8aa-cbcc-5328-91fe-5d769b12ac7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-351902" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({cosh}^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({cosh}^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537308} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ffe6b8-fc77-5a91-a8ee-c6dda37b1ac2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" :question/math-academy-id "q-250510" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)=3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{3}\\right)-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{3}\\right)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right)-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right)-k \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right) & =x+k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128c44ff-5002-57ad-a45f-4e742d10c24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+2{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537263} {:answer/id #uuid "14056c1d-1503-5c16-903f-a42353bc7679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537266} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2f4d68-d3cb-5e06-9684-ade0a7155990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537439} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcf0690-e390-5dbd-b7f0-70fd10a011b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}+{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537630} {:answer/id #uuid "d825bf38-2203-5280-977c-5f960b7c5773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537439} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dacd865-577a-5c58-b803-da35ca44d6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1c959d7c-d68c-591a-a3b7-ea71063de12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96466" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2[{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\cdot \\text{d}x+\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x+{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a24b83cf-35df-57fb-bfe2-9bb91087aee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537535} {:answer/id #uuid "b063029c-31ed-5fb8-bd08-c53673fc0531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537564} {:answer/id #uuid "b605189f-b236-5c14-b566-17cbfac34bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537580} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf14566-40b7-51bb-9c49-37e8d525189a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537673} {:answer/id #uuid "f920fbe8-305f-58a8-bcd2-bef18daf9ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537673} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1ca330f-ffdf-51b2-9a3c-b44ad29a0d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1ddb9340-9af2-51a6-8581-bb4c21f42fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-97073" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{sinh}^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{sinh}^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2359ac3e-05f6-59cf-b866-9b382c762ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537304} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f2aba8-3ad3-5527-af94-606d696f3adf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7db4dc2-4afa-5530-8d81-ba6a2b0a54c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537585} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc429605-87f5-544d-978a-0285dffc3203" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537850} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de0c8485-931d-5e14-9d0b-1416b5ec8693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537676} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5421c1-33c8-52c3-a934-b3f293d407a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ebb4c16-c258-518a-a825-ce276a761a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351898" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0126ad9c-e616-5191-8573-27abdacfd4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537225} {:answer/id #uuid "208c24da-be3a-55c3-9799-185a231a9712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537300} {:answer/id #uuid "2e98764c-cbfc-5c9b-944c-3ae0a975ef78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537328} {:answer/id #uuid "64308c73-80b0-5749-9c4f-adff75e44307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537428} {:answer/id #uuid "b17c939b-7ee6-5751-a726-e418f1a0ea59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537300} :answer-field/id #uuid "b387d932-1baa-5085-8949-e65a26d02b68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "216e3ef8-6751-5e3e-9d7f-5d48b7476c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97010" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+1)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x={cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+1)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2({cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C \\\\  & \\,=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11623763-75f4-582c-8cf1-89865dc45e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537258} {:answer/id #uuid "173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537277} {:answer/id #uuid "60e2afc2-23df-527e-8d4d-06bcc3f5634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537420} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d10f1c-8d35-5c14-a45c-90112e2423c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537739} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d7fe5d-6a2b-5e6d-aa5b-be15665a7768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537277} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd89537-63c4-5254-bd6d-b7533a181887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758" :question/math-academy-id "q-148367" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\n$$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a908713-5ac5-5134-a0e7-86afc9750420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537315} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3c99bb-9d3b-5885-8411-3a5f94462ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537392} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac49e22-956a-5757-b843-898b336ff080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537469} {:answer/id #uuid "d67bbc9b-a120-5ae6-be39-d2884fe54ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537656} {:answer/id #uuid "fedaa517-46ad-50d1-9223-915aeb1842d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537315} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef0a448-ccbc-545d-a9c9-133e5c77b5fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "347de47e-5245-5fd2-b46a-2d80ee9ca643" :question/math-academy-id "q-165872" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f919a0d-34cf-5c87-9355-e889ab85f2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537297} {:answer/id #uuid "9807cc06-0ffd-5b61-ad8a-0beb97bc28e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537516} {:answer/id #uuid "9e310136-b483-5416-b6c1-753bd73f9e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537524} {:answer/id #uuid "a02ac5b9-33ba-5281-baef-70b3880d7010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537530} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7393b4-08fb-5fb1-83f1-d004cc94717c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537594} :answer-field/id #uuid "6903f8c0-f369-5cc9-83aa-7a2303f7c793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "36b4a778-aa5a-54d9-9b61-eb9431bef188" :question/math-academy-id "q-96435" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-1={csch}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{({coth}^{2}⁡x-1)({coth}^{2}⁡x+1)}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({coth}^{2}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17}{15}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537730} :answer-field/id #uuid "075d7fee-b2d5-5957-a28d-a7eec528b93a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-250507" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{5}\\right)-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{5}\\right)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right)-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right)-k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a853eb43-35ad-5318-b3ea-fd258865a714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537546} :answer-field/id #uuid "98cc11c0-aee2-5fda-a252-3022cc314d32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab22a23-f9cc-56d5-95ac-279199c70b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537436} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45263fa-64ca-5fca-a711-c9b1f2ec9306" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0395874-ffc8-59ad-a520-d723c8af1b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537532} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ee657f-8a91-5462-a83e-e961791c1d5e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4b85bc81-6104-5328-b444-83c8973655d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-352792" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24173e0a-d957-5f97-a5dc-7d867846fe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537307} {:answer/id #uuid "7178716b-d298-5054-a295-621e46c31b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537450} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfe03f4-86e8-5f5d-a0ca-5eb20dd2e305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537472} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de1550-9555-521c-8641-82bf296c4387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537608} {:answer/id #uuid "ce716d8d-6f47-5a0f-9225-7a0aa1742627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537472} :answer-field/id #uuid "99b9d7f0-5aa1-5376-9c8e-7d9cbe16f0be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e26e540-b6e1-55b9-88da-a99de2917513" :question/math-academy-id "q-96413" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{sech}^{2}⁡x={tanh}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{3}^{2}+1}{{3}^{2}-1}+\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14f6e04e-fb88-5362-9549-483100fa12e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537271} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0b4705-45a1-5421-a9c7-5785f003fa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537317} {:answer/id #uuid "84b7088f-f334-521c-a837-e560bd2f92fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537486} {:answer/id #uuid "d41fcdf0-a6d8-5e72-ac3e-0c3d81a914c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537648} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c2410b-a921-5ac6-85cf-3cff52fdee7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537271} :answer-field/id #uuid "65a3f970-a115-5042-b8a1-b96f4f8015c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "56c765b2-a04b-54c3-b2ad-79e20147ade9" :question/math-academy-id "q-165894" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & =1 \\\\ 1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & ={cosh}^{2}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x))}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d41088e7-fcca-5795-a840-68f0d4b1a3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_8(3x)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537647} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff656e20-f3df-5084-9cd4-4ad94db5cfe5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "675df8f0-06ab-563c-85f0-2a4a4ed296f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-250406" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3} \\\\ 3x & ={8}^{6y} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{8}⁡({8}^{6y}) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & =6y \\\\ 6y & ={log}_{8}⁡(3x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840a654a-86fc-5489-8324-6801e6feeda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537482} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fdce0b-aebb-517e-8390-5a6aaec5bb75" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70b18141-2bee-5ae8-b9c2-69d17c5a68b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537448} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84af63e-7ff1-5d27-8af7-764ee61799ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b367b19-8f35-5f68-a044-eb7b9764c38d" :question/math-academy-id "q-351903" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{cosh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,-\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{cosh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2})]-[0-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1734e68b-49b7-5657-a8ab-e7fd14ec92c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537276} {:answer/id #uuid "6de2d048-caee-5e8a-baf6-c37fde229e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537442} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bcfa39-281d-566a-8e63-d65c56907c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537568} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a99a7f-7e6c-5818-a83d-a13f8c260bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537692} {:answer/id #uuid "faef0ce8-c4e4-53a3-9255-f04524119f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537276} :answer-field/id #uuid "215a951f-a6c0-593d-8a1c-917970be4ccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6bf4783e-de85-5ad3-95bc-1eadaee336f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-96307" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0035feb3-cb79-5047-8afd-04eee047fe8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537224} {:answer/id #uuid "37efae25-c6d0-510f-8a84-02b69180da3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537343} {:answer/id #uuid "71d70417-7ef2-5fec-908e-11a78c840b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537451} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f2dbd6-7b66-5d4d-b301-a9bd61a52a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537542} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fc32da-9081-513c-b08e-78ad9623f9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537451} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f36f1c-baeb-5d0c-a838-31d70af0c742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c61796b-b24c-5403-8a9e-cd8bda63e7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96405" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2d80338-bd01-53da-9ff6-138404ba74a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537641} :answer-field/id #uuid "db4c0633-bccb-5234-a9ce-6b6aa8a3a63d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "70e33acb-bebf-51b2-9607-6c52a3430a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-362032" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{|}_{2}^{7} \\\\  & =7-2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "620344d8-2da9-55c3-b8ec-d1845745c666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537423} :answer-field/id #uuid "52fbaaa5-e6e2-563a-8fc8-919869cd64cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7461b1f3-1aea-5d09-ab06-c9f25cb75e4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-348024" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot (1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-(0)}}{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185f59eb-c2e7-5a3f-a3b7-be2964f06456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537280} {:answer/id #uuid "4d024158-1b7c-5e9c-9ace-8dd0eea976bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537389} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2953bb-7aa8-5c3f-9a2d-3cafbe68e61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537632} {:answer/id #uuid "e3091fa4-afc7-563c-9eae-29ea28d513b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537688} {:answer/id #uuid "f153bba0-8285-5094-83d7-3b533736f1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537280} :answer-field/id #uuid "571ff74d-f457-5675-971d-04ff30533fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7649fd25-c8c5-5938-a768-41b9d4f9aa06" :question/math-academy-id "q-96453" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x-1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1 \\\\ {cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e15788-2fff-568a-a3d7-e43b6c609b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537400} {:answer/id #uuid "53242edd-a396-563b-8aa5-73886a38edb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537401} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb51c3e-f35f-58f3-8931-5422fe5d6f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537629} {:answer/id #uuid "edd19e76-dcf2-5721-a377-64323522bc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537718} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3563a9-4100-54a7-b530-a404469d2003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537718} :answer-field/id #uuid "93fd6742-7170-5522-9954-f5c8bd0f65a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b5373c3-faa7-5442-9a9c-2d5aebc96c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-165874" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2{sinh}^{2}⁡3x+2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x={cosh}^{2}⁡3x+{sinh}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{sinh}^{2}⁡3x+2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2({cosh}^{2}⁡3x+{sinh}^{2}⁡3x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x-x){|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ba324a1-6779-572b-99bc-3815b4a02bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537413} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cecfe57-733c-5892-b9ce-085b5206a4dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ddcb9f3-50a1-505b-ae57-c8a55dc1f652" :question/math-academy-id "q-347992" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{sech}^{2}⁡x={tanh}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{4+{4}^{-1}}{4-{4}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{17}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b81cdd-3fb5-51c1-a54c-615d09c8f887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537410}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537410} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc85decb-7916-5052-8222-edcbe0f6343d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9ea30f92-f367-5355-89e8-50b8c17d0449" :question/math-academy-id "q-348034" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{csch}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{csch}^{2}⁡x={coth}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{csch}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(1+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{2}{3-{3}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee8abd7-c8b0-5395-b1d2-db97d2fbe877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537329} {:answer/id #uuid "46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537377} {:answer/id #uuid "c58fc5b6-7692-51a1-abef-c6e7a022b6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x-4)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537612} {:answer/id #uuid "d99198fe-8160-5db2-9ef6-8b317c7d5096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x)}{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537662} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e17c2b-0b45-5f50-a9cb-32c3aec63676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537377} :answer-field/id #uuid "90658ef9-d513-512b-9c60-b9724abb9088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2f709a7-c2aa-55fd-a6a5-4424cd8b1fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-148313" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={7}^{x-2}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={7}^{x-2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & ={7}^{y-2} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & ={log}_{7}⁡({7}^{y-2}) \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & =y-2 \\\\ y & ={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2efeda09-6488-58c8-b51d-5cb115113e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537330} {:answer/id #uuid "3694787f-4267-5b85-9a9d-c811b7ca8c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537341} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2f505b-06f8-527d-b1b4-f411adee504d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537415} {:answer/id #uuid "bd388afb-e318-5bb0-ad11-815dec250e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537596} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6a0a2b-3a0c-5024-a882-d244026f8d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537330} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f1aa97-2aa4-5f57-8fa7-34a54ff2bfe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a49f44d3-1abb-5067-966c-70ae50d2155e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96429" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1-{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}-2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{0}-1}{{e}^{0}+1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2}-1}{{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537294} {:answer/id #uuid "307eac12-8ffa-5b27-b7eb-9fa2f8ffbd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537332} {:answer/id #uuid "4697b16a-b954-5ce9-9fdc-2eb3d67c270a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537374} {:answer/id #uuid "4a79f07c-c8a1-549a-81cf-2a1164ba3d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537386} {:answer/id #uuid "656fd776-3d31-5e32-af9a-81ea6f3a0abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537294} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf819579-d6fd-53c1-af84-bcfb66bf0607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af0d631e-6f1f-57fd-bd84-ccd6cde384ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-126546" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}x},\\hspace{0.222em}x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d13c05-6da4-5912-9d1f-919a238ff2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537368} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d0a7db-40e9-524e-a61b-ec49a93196d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc295d1d-76d5-5f67-a844-a3b5be954577" :question/math-academy-id "q-352419" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196682b1-bbf3-576e-ba6f-a649f7fc16ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537282} {:answer/id #uuid "40d40771-4eff-5689-a13b-0ce325f7611e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537364} {:answer/id #uuid "7d88a917-1a9a-5eac-a57a-00685fc9b5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537474} {:answer/id #uuid "c58c52c9-7d88-54cc-b33c-6e8f43208112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537611} {:answer/id #uuid "d73af309-c34a-5c48-b101-ba26f74e0435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537364} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8174fdc-5c9a-5c07-a9f4-f2a0413bcb78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc418213-398e-54af-8583-a0bcd69d268b" :question/math-academy-id "q-46622" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equations, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y) & =x-8 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y)} & ={2}^{x-8} \\\\ y & ={2}^{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9742393d-a500-5c2a-8afe-aa9646453e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537515} {:answer/id #uuid "bf467dbf-ef4a-570d-9381-8136807b7ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537600} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d1307c-5521-5cc2-9ba5-b15d1d2d5bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537693} {:answer/id #uuid "e53abd82-d6d2-5619-b674-b500de889e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537696} {:answer/id #uuid "efcebe4a-a0df-535b-8f64-7bd2e11ab057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537696} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a7f0e3-b811-5999-abd0-ca813f824353" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c7a3ee0b-1bd5-5d07-b45d-54312bc57611" :question/math-academy-id "q-165895" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x+2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2) & =1 \\\\ 1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2) & ={cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {sech}^{2}⁡(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6a86c9-5f1e-5b55-a779-0b27d17b9cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537241} {:answer/id #uuid "133a3fb8-a470-51e3-9aa8-75064677269e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537264} {:answer/id #uuid "48807227-d099-5e79-866e-1ce8d84016ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537382} {:answer/id #uuid "5c34cd29-1831-563e-a117-a4a9c3fbd985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537416} {:answer/id #uuid "858ba711-5287-57e4-a23a-9253523a5ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537382} :answer-field/id #uuid "345e42f3-499f-5ae0-9f6c-526a1f93142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf4092b0-8e64-55b4-94ba-2a8ea4d2ac81" :question/math-academy-id "q-165889" :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(2x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x)|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})]-[2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c584e3-4715-56f3-957f-04a6776e7cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\ln(3)-\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537464} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73b1863-d6f0-5b10-b3fa-815bfcf7b139" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0857a55-78b2-5e38-b682-a21c51974c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352425" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(8-6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)]-[0-0] \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{3}^{2}-{3}^{-2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3cfb59-acb3-5458-a2b9-9970c1414d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537318} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb58dffd-ddd5-56a7-b417-3fbe9d0a8b25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d50512b3-2d0b-53b0-a169-ed8ad3f44908" :question/math-academy-id "q-348041" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{csch}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{csch}^{2}⁡x={coth}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{csch}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-{8}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{16}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82dfab9f-2880-5450-8652-1147b1151f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_3(-x)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537481} :answer-field/id #uuid "1415ab41-137c-58c7-97a0-9e85dd2a2d52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6cea5e8-bf96-5756-ad7c-875f953f91a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250405" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=-{3}^{4x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y} \\\\ -x & ={3}^{4y} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4y}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & =4y \\\\ 4y & ={log}_{3}⁡(-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1902df-1e1a-57b7-92b9-f031a1d54cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537289} {:answer/id #uuid "b867ae05-4768-52a1-a7ab-9ac578fa106a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537587} {:answer/id #uuid "b95fcc5a-b97c-570a-b6aa-9be9f9c37ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537589} {:answer/id #uuid "e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537684} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ef0cbc-d0f2-5d87-96ee-056aed052a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537684} :answer-field/id #uuid "52664e87-7542-5e32-b8c8-3ec5d80cbfd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e0086fad-533f-5e80-a92f-0ac75b17640c" :question/math-academy-id "q-148359" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y} & =x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {3}^{2y} & =4x+2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dc7a7fb-89cd-51cd-aab3-8e7b474b89ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537475} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b368e1-f7b0-5dd8-92cb-a1654e1ce507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537548} {:answer/id #uuid "d97a0c7a-a10a-50ef-8a0c-e16fb5ef9b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537661} {:answer/id #uuid "dd74e5e2-9f80-5316-85b3-b0b8a53ad347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537675} {:answer/id #uuid "f46ac096-0fba-5cb8-8f28-45008d53cdf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537675} :answer-field/id #uuid "919f8a47-bdd5-52ef-8d35-92386471095f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f321e82d-23c7-5f15-828b-dbb232e439d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96522" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{csch}^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={csch}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =7x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-1 & ={csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2) \\\\ {coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2) & ={csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{csch}^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)[{csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1]}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2){coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef6e90d4-7f02-516b-ac2e-50a86e66d6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2^{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537721} :answer-field/id #uuid "94613fd4-07f9-5587-8c52-2ee0c031ef37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4092e37-c259-56c3-8698-e5c1a205391f" :question/math-academy-id "q-250483" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ 2x-1 & ={log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & ={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y} \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & =y \\\\ y & ={2}^{2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{2x-1}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036eaa53-7479-5120-8bab-05396fca46ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537230} {:answer/id #uuid "56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537406} {:answer/id #uuid "be5dfe54-d2c6-51bc-ba60-5b77415cb27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537597} {:answer/id #uuid "c7815962-506a-58a7-a401-291b446f0170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537617} {:answer/id #uuid "ead0b0b9-c829-5ec7-932a-5da8c8741b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce6d141-0aa1-5d3d-a0d1-784268e63420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" :question/math-academy-id "q-148370" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98cbc359-2b03-5efc-8078-0d38570b0f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537520} :answer-field/id #uuid "05363739-1254-5772-8cac-8bed71ed94ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fe6b40d3-8971-55c8-aabc-4ac03f347ec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-348060" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{coth}^{2}⁡x+x{csch}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{coth}^{2}⁡x+x{csch}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x({coth}^{2}⁡x+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)({coth}^{2}⁡x+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aacd782-dbd4-58d3-8bc5-764458467ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538513} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15e41c7-21f5-50db-bcda-4b29a0f68ef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b7d68eb-3099-57db-9579-cc9c26274e37" :question/math-academy-id "q-350681" :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\,1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?\n$$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{{(1/3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(1/3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(1/3)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0174e176-0cb7-5b47-a8ba-9fe3b08757a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538238} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2d612f-1d4f-54a0-829f-81ec557baecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538402} {:answer/id #uuid "53c58a6b-8263-5f64-84ba-2acd0232972e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538412} {:answer/id #uuid "623914d6-e6b8-5dc3-8327-81f1d2919a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538452} {:answer/id #uuid "fce51717-00cf-5470-b72d-97e68246b052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538452} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce54675-4c82-511c-b17c-ef8ba86365cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9820b99c-2acb-5f80-b231-9670a302c372" :question/math-academy-id "q-37402" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a641869-5b2a-5c93-8fec-70c216419ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538258} {:answer/id #uuid "1007ddbe-c62c-5841-8280-8935b703fe12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538271} {:answer/id #uuid "27ac4eb7-866d-591b-99fd-4e95d4aa9bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538314} {:answer/id #uuid "6f849d03-4388-5222-bb73-a0d236018103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538482} {:answer/id #uuid "abc230ef-4240-534b-bd00-d63ca4cdb3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538636}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538314} :answer-field/id #uuid "b87ce471-30f2-56c2-afaf-a050300cb9c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "98df2a56-4669-5921-84a6-4e54451b6791" :question/math-academy-id "q-1131" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35)={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & ={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {v}^{2}+35 & =-12v-1 \\\\ {v}^{2}+12v+36 & =0 \\\\ (v+6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡((-6)^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12(-6)-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡(71) & ={log}_{12}⁡(71).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-6$ is a valid solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fe331b-623c-552e-9c20-0255b34584e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538414} {:answer/id #uuid "75580bdf-e42f-542a-b4aa-5db9e8a21d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538494} {:answer/id #uuid "85689f35-93ad-5798-b798-e9f1e7536146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538539} {:answer/id #uuid "910dd9c5-7c4a-5d17-a062-a66f81d657dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538571} {:answer/id #uuid "d303cba2-df7f-5aef-a154-da6afc090924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538494} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1b6915c-b642-5726-bac9-d65678766025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b0b21ed-9399-541e-870b-dc048c3a5954" :question/math-academy-id "q-79867" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{3}⁡(x+4)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡({25}^{1/2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x+4 & =5 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(1+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c346939-819e-56f9-9e9d-bad5be5003b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538262} {:answer/id #uuid "52306a77-dde4-5acb-b5ef-6d1b99a085ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538409} {:answer/id #uuid "5a947b84-8ada-55d3-ad03-49d9532b991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538429} {:answer/id #uuid "91b13347-8026-55f4-a9cb-9cb2fff7d699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538574} {:answer/id #uuid "c1987a3b-528f-539a-be0c-b67b5536db72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538429} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ab10ac-4174-5934-8818-7632587d49cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b409a03d-9253-56f1-9515-c6248febada5" :question/math-academy-id "q-27729" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x)={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & ={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+19x & =10x-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+8) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-1)^{2}+19(-1)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-1)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-18) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-18)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-18)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-8)^{2}+19(-8)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-8)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-88) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-88)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-88)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-8$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"No solutions.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79de5745-5bf0-555e-a601-3b494c6bd938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538509} {:answer/id #uuid "8be64e16-04ad-51d8-827e-a16e9b00296b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538560} {:answer/id #uuid "a1508db0-4863-5e06-8a60-94207ae22916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} {:answer/id #uuid "add9a8e9-c820-5de7-a37b-45ae258e7857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538642} {:answer/id #uuid "b7237201-cb21-5414-84de-7f2cfcb1a828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a61e66-3aca-536b-aa8a-9b7a2020142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecdce78b-6ad1-5fcc-9999-f701ae74931a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100773" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7)={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+7 & =2{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}=-1$ has no real solutions. Therefore, our original logarithmic equation has no solutions either."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278b3e92-0930-54df-8b78-9d1eb2eb134a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{2} t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539133} {:answer/id #uuid "27d15098-9f4d-5025-b663-55253273ac10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539134} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e304d5-2e98-55e6-88b1-e67c5a9ca1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539194} {:answer/id #uuid "e45f8e18-3e75-5cb3-8aee-472302b82414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539195} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1b2043-64b1-5186-9ca4-d5be02457609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539195} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b51734-7cf3-59d4-b15c-c37a8d472ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "77c0932d-edde-5a3d-a95b-c9e88ef0198f" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddac435-f4c5-5790-a848-0d7fd8aecc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}10 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539131} {:answer/id #uuid "49e747ab-2e87-5b28-b3b1-52bfbfa14251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}5 \\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539145} {:answer/id #uuid "4a119ce1-666c-589c-913e-71a12513c765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539146} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1d6599-ceb5-5eed-83dc-ed06fd4e1d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}5 \\sqrt{2} t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539165} {:answer/id #uuid "804f7a7d-ca0c-5b72-99dd-1e2bd2065c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}5 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539145} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4985361-8dc5-544a-a26a-fb6bff95a3f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88d1c090-6bb7-523f-a9f3-d7ec7807cd7a" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2})\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "020e75fa-c086-57b4-9ebf-21542d8c63cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539480} {:answer/id #uuid "03195d80-906e-5fc4-b665-ca11a907a266" :answer/type {:db/i