swer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}{sin}^{2}⁡(5+\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544646} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c14f59e-67b0-5401-9f45-fbeeee924851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544250} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f795ef-1bd6-53d0-93e4-2f6075fc9d94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f16dc6-ec6f-552f-87c4-2a50bc7836ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sin^2\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544128} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4daa789-6251-5dc2-bff5-c7b29f5e7178" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04c21b11-d6eb-5e2f-9a68-02bdca097cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-133225" :question/problem "Let $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ represent the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of a point on the given curve. Write expressions for $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ in terms of $\\theta .$\n\n$x(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$y(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use \n\n> $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ and $\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$\n\nwhere $x,$ $y,$ and $r$ are functions of $\\theta .$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064a3c82-ae53-5db3-ad5c-b3fcfc6fb6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡(4)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544101} {:answer/id #uuid "49f84153-b03e-5266-a48d-c82f2db5b6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544247} {:answer/id #uuid "7b506411-a799-5138-a14f-4b0c6672b0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(5)+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544360} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3750bd-081d-5764-9284-5e0451f625eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(5)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544522} {:answer/id #uuid "ee944174-45cf-5629-b838-1acad19f5360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(7)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544247} :answer-field/id #uuid "3300a13e-fe37-59f5-9d51-81da8e4c45f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07d6770c-e373-5366-b946-966ce2aa6000" :question/math-academy-id "q-100163" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation ${7}^{x+4}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{7}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+4} & =5 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡({7}^{x+4}) & ={log}_{7}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+4={log}_{7}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & ={log}_{7}⁡(5) \\\\ x & ={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1efbdd8f-088c-54e2-94f2-33585aa4d9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544155} {:answer/id #uuid "3eedec8a-d18b-5b88-83c4-00e34bb3b972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544229} {:answer/id #uuid "b26a6ab9-c41c-52e2-907b-4ffdb43b0d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544464} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a6b41e-820d-56bf-ae6b-fd6bae64c165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544613} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fb72b-e66d-5acb-a71a-2e252f1c84fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544464} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c60208-16e5-5779-9065-2feb76aee1f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e2ee0d0-657b-565c-90c3-ae6c29ac6b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83516" :question/problem "![](images/14/140947c29667712d4a1071305396fff56a24f0a5c11f26e31368b8cc011f2863.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 4=8$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=8\\cdot A=12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdda5ba-c7b8-5b1e-8e9a-e2ebee5f3bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{11\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544216} {:answer/id #uuid "45b57772-1a24-517a-95cc-031e14b7b047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{9\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544240} {:answer/id #uuid "4c27fd42-8cff-578f-b7b7-132d0e72f699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544251} {:answer/id #uuid "640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544303} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2ff5b2-7d3f-5bc8-8b07-edf54d53bb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544303} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f71d5c-aae5-521c-b9e4-78cdc0efc697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1ca5b3d9-2cf9-5f0b-b261-8040bf2d2224" :question/math-academy-id "q-125695" :question/problem "![](images/17/172737d1a5b28b9a60958dc7e6b1748db1912021b7df4ad26045648063037fd8.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}]}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{8}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057a3821-57db-58af-a32b-c16f7c0710fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544098}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c39e3b-1f65-52da-8eaa-e6691231acd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2749929e-e01c-5706-82d9-161e96c84d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-133226" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\ny(\\theta )=6(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $y(\\theta )$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=6{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=12u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & =12u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ef4703-6639-5623-8c0b-2ffb2cd82831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544178} {:answer/id #uuid "42047be1-5cd9-5f0f-9112-70b8e78c7e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544233} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6a7060-6e4d-5e7b-9e87-0feb10e6a475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544325} {:answer/id #uuid "a98b5b54-648a-5f5c-abff-c03076a297d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544451} {:answer/id #uuid "e724f53d-777b-5184-bede-b852c936bd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544583} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db885ae-a359-50aa-b94a-866c4319d576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3aec8613-8bc5-55d8-bb63-25dfe3e5bcce" :question/math-academy-id "q-127800" :question/problem "![](images/44/446278a820ba21da7f5a1f15639e8cc4f96e38f38f3b5905b926bb9d2a106948.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb18d81-1f63-599d-9330-be80543a661b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544329} {:answer/id #uuid "b2eef91a-4bda-57e9-8dc9-d19c65f2191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544465} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c8d26d-0f5e-5aa7-80ac-7d5f0bd84561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544590} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc61141-892a-5b47-bb30-ac12b2c0629b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{5\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544597} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544614} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c945d12-5ddf-5d29-b1ff-6f3e26849c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "43e3bf66-f5d8-57b0-9e72-5dc370f879ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-83484" :question/problem "![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f147060-2e3d-530e-8605-1ba13a4114d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544364} {:answer/id #uuid "9dec9e09-3ab9-51d5-93f5-5553f7a2ae1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544429} {:answer/id #uuid "cd163f76-39be-503b-8e02-0fc0fc56c0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{3}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544521} {:answer/id #uuid "d87bd39e-276b-5919-ae48-a488a12d6528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{{log}_{3}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544553} {:answer/id #uuid "f4426217-c85d-5fd5-a049-5d1b721018a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544521} :answer-field/id #uuid "479444fd-611e-5a03-bcad-55f19ebf647d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dbd2fd5-279c-5b4f-83af-6acbee35f908" :question/math-academy-id "q-66801" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation ${3}^{x}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We take ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =5 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx={log}_{3}⁡(5)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e7683d-837d-5f5c-88b4-9f43a2d9f138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544088} {:answer/id #uuid "1747e57c-4ef9-5157-a45d-bc4f36bf748e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544135} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb3e12e-9656-5b69-bd45-2db95263545f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544249} {:answer/id #uuid "831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544373} {:answer/id #uuid "dacb2b57-d53d-5242-94a7-23fcf1973ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{3\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf4a6ed-16c7-5aba-80c0-63cd22eaebcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50affdee-0a40-5255-b196-53de7a7824c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125863" :question/problem "![](images/66/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $7$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=0,$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0 \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{7}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\pi }{7},\\frac{3\\pi }{7}]}_{4\\text{th}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{3\\pi }{7},\\frac{4\\pi }{7}]}_{5\\text{th}}\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{4\\pi }{7},\\frac{5\\pi }{7}]}_{6\\text{th}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{7},\\frac{6\\pi }{7}]}_{7\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fd65f66-345e-5e02-a44f-83cf02b3d0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544335} :answer-field/id #uuid "248fba5d-72db-5572-a2be-4b009978848e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bcca525-fc41-52cc-be8b-f245ac9162a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133231" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line to our polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nThe tangent line is $y\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5450b792-cfd2-5428-85cb-6e721aad8477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544273} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac577e2-de00-593f-842f-889f13634e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544386} {:answer/id #uuid "b86b5b0e-81d1-54fb-8de3-463acc2dbc18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544477} {:answer/id #uuid "ec10ba12-c948-5373-9400-a4298e8eaf68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544598} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544647} :answer-field/id #uuid "835941de-1b21-50b1-bc5e-e054f925540b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "623b63a4-baae-557b-86dd-761ba0b029fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-124682" :question/problem "![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d3ae86-fe5b-5459-9248-c229a4cb9d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544126} {:answer/id #uuid "1844ef73-602c-5b30-88ea-ffffbf088136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544139} {:answer/id #uuid "682eb163-f2ab-5256-9c37-ff22d6e12633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544313} {:answer/id #uuid "82bafe14-c2b9-5557-b37c-58363f34c732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544371} {:answer/id #uuid "85fa656d-5dd4-543f-afaa-83f9f26d96bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544313} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca50bb8-65fb-5887-be84-d31d773a1ff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "68c240a3-d1c2-54cd-a001-e4698ed241e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125494" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a010feba178f2db834e1939a91c81fd95131d65b10c7616bd92140fa46eaa305.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca5b55e-64e9-57fc-988e-cb6395776e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544146} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecded90-01b7-5d43-b3ff-b3f9fe7aed89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544154} {:answer/id #uuid "706f5975-5ca9-5baa-a6cc-d37212c61ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544337} {:answer/id #uuid "7b31c624-e893-58d9-9540-be3f1b71efc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544359} {:answer/id #uuid "d8708299-6b74-5928-9a08-9d2da799883a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544146} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af88dd3-9486-5aea-a0ba-f768f6515698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "77bce5e4-123e-5ff9-af9f-15d1cb34dfd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127796" :question/problem "![](images/19/192007bbd5aee67607897bc1e4e3e29d106c8f6041fe8b06025f48999e68fcfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $9\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n9\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24532688-eec4-5621-92fa-532033042c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544165} {:answer/id #uuid "28cb0b75-f16a-56d1-a298-8e7854f23b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544181} {:answer/id #uuid "3301cca7-812b-5032-929a-442a1ea385d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /5}^{\\pi /5}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544202} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0fc7b3-5743-5f2c-83ef-929c57f0d1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544525} {:answer/id #uuid "d84ce1a1-cfbc-5d9c-a9e0-a3de51b4f70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544181} :answer-field/id #uuid "b53aa41e-e9c5-50c8-ab7e-c6508a46ac1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "790dcc10-7f22-59d7-b4bc-0f6943a2af9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14916" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f62e6a4cec5b2a877f2491a101f3929edd6664424b86daab4bbfe8096c5c9282.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249cec14-666a-5fae-9615-1544a0eec215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.370" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544166} {:answer/id #uuid "3755bb99-c614-5cf1-80fc-6be88288dbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.370" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544208} {:answer/id #uuid "767e7c4e-ad51-5769-a990-6385b5908531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.260" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544349} {:answer/id #uuid "b73616bd-6754-5514-aa8a-eead41959119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.740" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544475} {:answer/id #uuid "f6221a2d-763a-5c87-bb34-2b68cf8fa1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.740" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544208} :answer-field/id #uuid "51e41568-3547-5b0e-b361-b42d6abf90f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e1ee8c2-d8a9-5564-8fe2-55ce248be31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31891" :question/problem "Given that ${5}^{2a+3}-13=25,$ find the value of $a$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3}-13 & =25 \\\\ {5}^{2a+3} & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{5}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3} & =38 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2a+3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(38) \\\\ 2a+3 & ={log}_{5}⁡(38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ 2a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\na≈-0.370\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63855fed-118f-5138-a879-f0b81c16d4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(4+\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544301} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0cbc70-aa26-5af9-a6bb-3e3f85dc4845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544321} {:answer/id #uuid "726eeee2-98d9-5930-919c-11fd667234f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544341} {:answer/id #uuid "83b74220-3ac7-54d2-b4de-ea3f0fdab608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544374} {:answer/id #uuid "b13fd3a4-0f4b-5c2c-b0f7-8169cad6dd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544374} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c931c3-cfba-5275-ba59-84cd3742ca6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f3edede-f2b8-5413-b0b9-835c57755de4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124414" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ab11405aeb635dafaeadef1b174db163f395a62126225f99de07884b3d8cb9.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "788347b9-f328-50bb-9f8d-44dd99b00eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544354} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ca41cb-2481-5f25-ab16-b237d166c5ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f4b1de5-2584-553e-ad73-4df20f726215" :question/math-academy-id "q-351293" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c8afd0a30a2796cc1590e97c8d71e1d47128ac1993d2875072877149631642e5.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/caacb78b0f37dcc58d6568a571c99bc569c7eb95aeacf724d5df33e35ba21fa7.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b68c56-30ad-5a97-b72c-21abfa85a7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544195} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6c810c-df15-56ca-b73a-c69a46243cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544226} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5423ac-fe72-5892-8908-80ac3a1cfa10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544425} {:answer/id #uuid "bf25fda7-ec73-59b4-b184-e2d87135abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544493} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5b4cd2-2eef-5d45-be9d-b231d277d3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544195} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5052787-b20b-5d33-ba51-bf4f48076c76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "809efbd7-39b4-57dd-a5fb-9c26fa380f5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83792" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3196a19057d5a5f658faad1ef028b12a12b87de56f93dc3f2df1aa03f21fcf2.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $7\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544111} {:answer/id #uuid "3f16e717-df6e-5066-9901-a556dc9ad746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544230} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f7afa-35a1-5941-9320-ab16f063a2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544444} {:answer/id #uuid "f332fbbe-56f7-5d0e-a468-4f968b7b69ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544616} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f5048c-ee98-5951-88c7-66b4a1c08544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544111} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45758eb-810c-5093-acfa-015e3b2ab053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8b33c7a8-87dc-53ea-9478-54f9a2f3e5e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83485" :question/problem "![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e01582e-53ea-5a54-9f0c-d53b90245089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544227} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a47526-4f69-5723-8a3e-3d9029c72ee7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9165f710-3b4c-5eb0-a90d-63ff961aee99" :question/math-academy-id "q-319490" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=5+12\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b33f10d-c2bd-51b2-9314-14748e5b22b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544143} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd2fcc1-904f-5624-a891-21600d6a3559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544159} {:answer/id #uuid "319af86b-63e6-5b3a-8f84-af301c7e96fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544198} {:answer/id #uuid "5de93751-8e37-5c0e-ade7-b00a0286b1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544291} {:answer/id #uuid "b62a2b7d-1d9a-5e11-8aa5-b6a9b07a1289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544143} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f2fe27-3752-50bd-8e74-45d47d4357a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9182ab7c-62c2-510f-9183-c91606315f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-125329" :question/problem "![](images/94/94a5da0925a82b91d345fa7811e41c219c19ca48d76b30927e920efed3b2edb3.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\n![](images/7f/7f1a9ba1b4d9a86e23b8634dedaf6d4e1a651efbe4fc7aa27ac4dbb5d443018f.png)\n\nSince there are $12$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =12\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 8{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =48{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12} \\\\  & =48[(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =48(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a7be4c-c258-5104-8444-2a5ac6bf1461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.685" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544163} {:answer/id #uuid "78981f9f-c8a0-501c-a93c-1f3d6d8a9843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.604" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544355} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20cb11-444e-5551-a283-109595f4bfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544511} {:answer/id #uuid "d6441217-85f1-5384-af07-4fb4353c7e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544544} {:answer/id #uuid "ddefe1b3-5955-5cb7-9393-02639a571285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.604" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544544} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb33b195-f963-5860-8754-612425a1f549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "918c847a-dee1-5e90-aa75-e08ca9d39fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-66811" :question/problem "Given that ${3}^{4-2x}=\\sqrt{2},$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-2x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4-2x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ 4-2x & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{1/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-2x=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ -2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-4 \\\\ x & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈1.842\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a984d6-e24d-5ad2-9d64-1bf9b0d07a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544382} :answer-field/id #uuid "972f7a9a-9438-5af7-a972-1e1761110b8a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "950e52f2-cc86-53ef-af25-6a46ca25d518" :question/math-academy-id "q-351393" :question/problem "![](images/21/219480e656f2f5913d7a83d93871c09b334600365d46a041ae63607d3f2f8326.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e1f3651f2c120b65063015a17a7e657ccb5b2e87df475660785d35748c05557a.png)\n\nSince there are $8$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =8\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =32[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =32(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be217e1-8af5-5001-b379-27a9f68b7e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544112} {:answer/id #uuid "48a4b65d-2629-5920-82b0-6f811ae028d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544245} {:answer/id #uuid "988ee9dd-88bf-5f48-a619-635ec25c4e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544418} {:answer/id #uuid "b937f4f6-23c9-5053-860a-c16580c2fbb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544479} {:answer/id #uuid "e771cf3c-1b99-5030-acbb-373402951169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544245} :answer-field/id #uuid "86333074-728b-5345-a40e-5dbc3ddeb7aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "96dc50ad-7c40-5c2b-a752-a6f1cca71c8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127794" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b7475f0a169e62121711d52daaf51e8954053fc35738266d94240b6e70de6258.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3965b942-4bfa-58af-87a2-5bee1c709bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\cos^3(2\\theta)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544211} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75a3698-1ee7-50e3-b39c-ded471e1e2de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9dac1de0-3399-5e2c-82ff-248d3a51fed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-133230" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for our polar curve.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ec012b5-f731-5c5e-96d4-923c5b550d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544362} {:answer/id #uuid "7f594d21-de94-5f4b-a0d8-17674ad8f887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544365} {:answer/id #uuid "c0162a02-455a-5f09-a239-5b7fe41f7d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544497} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaf2d7c-3557-543d-af50-fae40376027f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544520} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc1fc67-a5a0-5126-89ee-1dd84d13b93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}{sin}^{2}⁡(5+\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544362} :answer-field/id #uuid "79734443-de36-5288-80ea-687e1187b2c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9a8bb7e-ad2c-5185-87eb-ce30902a04e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124424" :question/problem "![](images/97/97b30cfa241a6e1b9db4d5332749ae503a926711c0e7c78ab188c2327463c89b.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043e78d4-5f01-563f-8dd0-f38020b32e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544095} :answer-field/id #uuid "be158308-89e7-524f-868f-9564cb669864" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b75e23ab-5c6f-5e0d-91f4-1cb8debf07f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-351400" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcec4003e842d279dbed89307ef84a230b16443fe515a8cc621aea72bf8829d3.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c35b0e3c4e51b164097e6deb11e4418726927f478f934e41d1250388e9a27e5.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{(\\theta -\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =20[(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20de621f-e8f8-5627-bd64-6ba71b41077b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544161} :answer-field/id #uuid "52688c42-4059-5864-ac3d-963accbc9ab7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b96d4599-55b6-52a4-b5e9-744fec2eb4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-319509" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7-3\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10304769-0b45-5ddb-960a-5561ac8890cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.141" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544119} {:answer/id #uuid "26a853f4-f0a8-5a09-b9c0-56c7fc01358d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544174} {:answer/id #uuid "66790320-0c2c-5cc3-b140-997d9491efef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.981" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544309} {:answer/id #uuid "945201fd-0d4a-5e7f-a3b1-d14b90583f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.454" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544412} {:answer/id #uuid "feaf6731-e3b7-599f-a042-7ed2790987cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.885" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544643} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4daa940-75d3-59a9-9409-b1cafb9f426c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d436286b-8ea5-5b92-b174-4ebb9b14bf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-973" :question/problem "Given that ${11}^{4-y}=\\sqrt[4]{3},$ find the value of $y$  rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking ${log}_{11}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{4-y} & =\\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ {log}_{11}⁡({11}^{4-y}) & ={log}_{11}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{3}) \\\\ 4-y & ={log}_{11}⁡({3}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-y=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{11}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\ -y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}-4 \\\\ y & =4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈3.885\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25444643-934c-52ab-b890-db2ee6187d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544169} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b7463f-7f4d-5f03-a9e1-2942e0699bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544505} {:answer/id #uuid "e7711dd9-3cc9-51a7-ba89-7107451ca153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544584} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c3f927-4c06-5422-a227-69b04f7920cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544615} {:answer/id #uuid "f6289bae-3a41-5c9e-8f1d-1c68a61a70d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544584} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c411aaa-ddaa-5464-8abb-dd307e74030d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9ba8fdb-9397-5097-95f0-204a59ce4162" :question/math-academy-id "q-124684" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0d95c75c-644f-536a-b8f4-48004542e397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545224} {:answer/id #uuid "0c86496a-2445-598d-8655-4f9b16fe096a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}(3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546273} {:answer/id #uuid "118f5681-350a-503b-8d5f-f2b362cd6698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546289} {:answer/id #uuid "14b519fd-79bd-5904-b181-2a8b8d5356a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}({sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )-2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546294} {:answer/id #uuid "15014d3a-71e7-518b-aadf-e899615ac4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}\\right))\\,\\text{d}\\thet